WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en-GB

00:00:03.600 --> 00:00:06.870
- Thank you, so let's
call the Board to order

00:00:06.870 --> 00:00:08.553
and welcome, everybody.

00:00:09.660 --> 00:00:11.190
Just for some of the housekeeping matters.

00:00:11.190 --> 00:00:14.370
We have some apologies for
absence of this Board meeting.

00:00:14.370 --> 00:00:17.070
So actually Suresh Viswanathan

00:00:17.070 --> 00:00:18.840
and Mark Walport who were with us

00:00:18.840 --> 00:00:20.850
in this morning's Private Board,

00:00:20.850 --> 00:00:23.100
had to send apologies for this afternoon.

00:00:23.100 --> 00:00:26.310
And Mike Cooper sent his
apologies for this morning's Board

00:00:26.310 --> 00:00:29.910
and also I'm afraid for
this afternoon's Board.

00:00:29.910 --> 00:00:34.910
And let me also just
announce it's too big a word,

00:00:34.945 --> 00:00:38.280
but that Tanuj Kapilashrami
has actually tendered

00:00:38.280 --> 00:00:43.050
her resignation as a
non-exec to the Board,

00:00:43.050 --> 00:00:46.470
actually given the significant
increase in her role

00:00:46.470 --> 00:00:48.270
and responsibilities
at Standard Chartered,

00:00:48.270 --> 00:00:51.300
where she is not just
the global head of HR,

00:00:51.300 --> 00:00:54.900
but also now of strategy
and a series of other roles.

00:00:54.900 --> 00:00:58.200
And the travel requirements in that role

00:00:58.200 --> 00:01:00.480
have simply made it impossible
for her to continue.

00:01:00.480 --> 00:01:02.880
But we will miss her.

00:01:02.880 --> 00:01:04.980
Sarah-Jane, I know you have to leave

00:01:04.980 --> 00:01:08.670
just around about five o'clock,
so thank you for being here

00:01:08.670 --> 00:01:10.230
and if you just want to get up

00:01:10.230 --> 00:01:13.920
and leave at the right
moment, that would be okay.

00:01:13.920 --> 00:01:15.660
So let's go, declarations of interest.

00:01:15.660 --> 00:01:18.330
Are there any new declarations of interest

00:01:18.330 --> 00:01:20.180
that any Board members need to share?

00:01:21.780 --> 00:01:23.373
Okay, thank you very much.

00:01:24.990 --> 00:01:28.530
Can I ask the Board for
its approval of the minutes

00:01:28.530 --> 00:01:30.780
of the Board meeting held
on the 6th of February?

00:01:30.780 --> 00:01:34.293
Any comments on the Board meeting minutes?

00:01:35.130 --> 00:01:37.050
Okay, so we'll approve those minutes.

00:01:37.050 --> 00:01:37.883
I'll adopt those.

00:01:37.883 --> 00:01:39.330
Thank you very much.

00:01:39.330 --> 00:01:42.300
So if you'll humour me

00:01:42.300 --> 00:01:43.980
'cause there's rather
a bittersweet moment.

00:01:43.980 --> 00:01:48.300
Actually, I'm chairing
my last Board here now

00:01:48.300 --> 00:01:51.450
for NHS England.

00:01:51.450 --> 00:01:54.570
So I'm going to recite
some of the activities

00:01:54.570 --> 00:01:56.280
that I've undertaken since the last Board,

00:01:56.280 --> 00:02:00.610
but I'd also just like
to share some reflections

00:02:02.400 --> 00:02:06.060
just to say it's been an
immense privilege, an honour

00:02:06.060 --> 00:02:08.280
and a privilege to
actually have been asked

00:02:08.280 --> 00:02:10.233
to chair NHS England.

00:02:11.510 --> 00:02:13.500
It's a critical organisation
right at the heart

00:02:13.500 --> 00:02:16.110
of the way our society operates.

00:02:16.110 --> 00:02:18.450
It's a really important part of, I think

00:02:18.450 --> 00:02:20.670
what makes our country really brilliant.

00:02:20.670 --> 00:02:22.850
And that's the NHS.

00:02:22.850 --> 00:02:24.300
So to have had the opportunity

00:02:24.300 --> 00:02:27.303
to chair NHS England has
been a huge privilege.

00:02:28.530 --> 00:02:31.380
It's been three years, quite
challenging three years,

00:02:31.380 --> 00:02:33.960
lots of change, but
also lots of challenge,

00:02:33.960 --> 00:02:36.060
which has made the job very interesting,

00:02:36.060 --> 00:02:39.090
but also I think perhaps
the most demanding role

00:02:39.090 --> 00:02:39.933
I've ever had.

00:02:41.071 --> 00:02:44.400
But in many ways, perhaps
the best role I've ever had.

00:02:44.400 --> 00:02:46.763
And it's been a privilege
to work with the Board.

00:02:47.700 --> 00:02:50.070
So previous non-executives who timed out

00:02:50.070 --> 00:02:53.610
and then then colleagues
around the Board table today.

00:02:53.610 --> 00:02:57.870
So non-executives, the
executive team working

00:02:57.870 --> 00:02:59.640
with trust chairs and leaders

00:02:59.640 --> 00:03:01.350
throughout the systems,

00:03:01.350 --> 00:03:03.930
the regions into the trusts

00:03:03.930 --> 00:03:05.627
and engaged with primary
care, engaging with many

00:03:05.627 --> 00:03:09.210
of the other arms length bodies leaders.

00:03:09.210 --> 00:03:12.093
I mean, it has been a real privilege,

00:03:13.244 --> 00:03:16.530
but the best thing actually
has been the number

00:03:16.530 --> 00:03:18.810
of times I've been able to go
actually into the front line.

00:03:18.810 --> 00:03:20.673
I sometimes feel a bit performative,

00:03:21.510 --> 00:03:22.950
but actually to have the opportunity

00:03:22.950 --> 00:03:24.970
to go into the front line and to see

00:03:26.130 --> 00:03:28.740
the brilliant, passionate,
committed NHS staff

00:03:28.740 --> 00:03:30.600
as they engage with patients,

00:03:30.600 --> 00:03:33.840
whether it's in ambulance shifts
or on the front line in ED

00:03:33.840 --> 00:03:37.530
or in our acute hospitals in
primary care, meeting patients

00:03:37.530 --> 00:03:40.440
and doing patient Q&amp;A, meeting the GPs

00:03:40.440 --> 00:03:43.110
and the other primary care practitioners.

00:03:43.110 --> 00:03:46.745
It has been in many ways
the best part of the job.

00:03:46.745 --> 00:03:48.600
And it has been, as I keep using the word,

00:03:48.600 --> 00:03:50.800
it's been a real privilege
to be able to do that.

00:03:50.800 --> 00:03:51.873
And I will miss it.

00:03:54.570 --> 00:03:59.370
We're joined today by,
well by Jim, incoming CEO

00:03:59.370 --> 00:04:01.590
and Penny Dash, the incoming chair

00:04:01.590 --> 00:04:03.420
is going to be joining us imminently.

00:04:03.420 --> 00:04:06.300
She's in another meeting
at the moment, I think,

00:04:06.300 --> 00:04:07.920
with the Department,
and she'll be coming in

00:04:07.920 --> 00:04:09.810
as soon as that meeting finishes.

00:04:09.810 --> 00:04:12.360
So welcome to Penny when
she comes and welcome Jim.

00:04:14.550 --> 00:04:15.840
There've been a number of announcements,

00:04:15.840 --> 00:04:17.490
really quite material announcements

00:04:17.490 --> 00:04:21.270
as we know since we last met
as a Board, the abolition

00:04:21.270 --> 00:04:24.840
of NHS England, obviously that's
got to be managed through,

00:04:24.840 --> 00:04:26.460
take some time to do it.

00:04:26.460 --> 00:04:28.140
But the government's statement

00:04:28.140 --> 00:04:31.110
and intention to essentially
abolish NHS England

00:04:31.110 --> 00:04:32.880
and absorb it within

00:04:32.880 --> 00:04:36.420
and work more closely with the Department.

00:04:36.420 --> 00:04:38.010
We have the announcement of the new chair

00:04:38.010 --> 00:04:40.290
and the new chief executive.

00:04:40.290 --> 00:04:42.660
We have an announcement
and the appointment

00:04:42.660 --> 00:04:44.430
of a new finance director

00:04:44.430 --> 00:04:47.670
and also of a new transition team.

00:04:47.670 --> 00:04:51.480
And also very significant
reductions in headcount,

00:04:51.480 --> 00:04:54.690
both at NHS England and at the Centre,

00:04:54.690 --> 00:04:57.030
but also actually in the ICBs

00:04:57.030 --> 00:04:59.940
in terms of an expense run rate reduction.

00:04:59.940 --> 00:05:03.300
And so actually there's
been a lot of news,

00:05:03.300 --> 00:05:05.880
highly material news since we last met.

00:05:05.880 --> 00:05:09.990
And it will make the next
few months very challenging

00:05:09.990 --> 00:05:12.420
in addition to the challenge actually

00:05:12.420 --> 00:05:16.800
of helping to run and organise and oversee

00:05:16.800 --> 00:05:19.890
and manage the performance
of the NHS more broadly

00:05:19.890 --> 00:05:23.040
and in its partnerships
with local authorities,

00:05:23.040 --> 00:05:25.173
with the community, et cetera.

00:05:28.170 --> 00:05:30.150
Let me just, if you will humour me,

00:05:30.150 --> 00:05:34.653
then just reflect a little
bit on my three years.

00:05:35.900 --> 00:05:36.733
I mean, when I came into the role,

00:05:36.733 --> 00:05:39.090
I had sort of three broad themes.

00:05:39.090 --> 00:05:42.690
They were, I often talk
about prioritisation

00:05:42.690 --> 00:05:44.403
and therefore trade offs.

00:05:45.600 --> 00:05:48.960
I would talk about delivery.

00:05:48.960 --> 00:05:50.160
So it's really important

00:05:50.160 --> 00:05:52.110
that we deliver, we don't just talk.

00:05:52.110 --> 00:05:54.780
And then thirdly, after news
is the phrase fact-based,

00:05:54.780 --> 00:05:56.050
there's a lot of emotion

00:05:57.000 --> 00:05:59.550
and a lot of commentary
around the NHS generally

00:05:59.550 --> 00:06:02.790
across our society, wholly understandably.

00:06:02.790 --> 00:06:05.640
And actually it needs to
be factually informed.

00:06:05.640 --> 00:06:07.410
So really good fact base helps, I think,

00:06:07.410 --> 00:06:09.267
better understanding about the challenge

00:06:09.267 --> 00:06:10.743
and the opportunities.

00:06:11.640 --> 00:06:14.160
My second reflection coming in actually

00:06:14.160 --> 00:06:15.870
was just the sheer scale of the system

00:06:15.870 --> 00:06:19.230
is little understood
that it's an ecosystem

00:06:19.230 --> 00:06:22.770
and not a single organisation,
often best described

00:06:22.770 --> 00:06:25.538
as a flotilla and not a dreadnought.

00:06:25.538 --> 00:06:28.920
And within that ecosystem,
multiple layers of governance

00:06:28.920 --> 00:06:30.600
and multiple legal contracts.

00:06:30.600 --> 00:06:33.540
So hundreds of Boards
each with their own legal

00:06:33.540 --> 00:06:35.490
and fiduciary responsibilities.

00:06:35.490 --> 00:06:37.650
Thousands of primary care practises,

00:06:37.650 --> 00:06:39.240
private sector suppliers,

00:06:39.240 --> 00:06:41.700
local authority mutual dependents,

00:06:41.700 --> 00:06:43.470
and working within a financial system,

00:06:43.470 --> 00:06:47.430
which allocates from NHS
England down through the system,

00:06:47.430 --> 00:06:51.300
but which cannot by law run at a deficit.

00:06:51.300 --> 00:06:55.803
So it's a hugely complex
ecosystem to engage with.

00:06:56.790 --> 00:06:58.680
And when I came into the role,

00:06:58.680 --> 00:07:03.680
there were a number of really
quite stark factors at play.

00:07:04.290 --> 00:07:06.780
First of all, we were
just starting the recovery

00:07:06.780 --> 00:07:11.780
from the pandemic and
that was a huge, huge task

00:07:12.660 --> 00:07:13.770
where the Health Service had done

00:07:13.770 --> 00:07:16.320
an absolutely brilliant
job through that period,

00:07:16.320 --> 00:07:19.440
but perhaps distorting
some of its normal focus.

00:07:19.440 --> 00:07:21.270
And actually the recovery from that

00:07:21.270 --> 00:07:24.003
has been hugely, hugely demanding.

00:07:24.840 --> 00:07:27.450
There was a new structure
being introduced of ICBs.

00:07:27.450 --> 00:07:30.720
So the 42 Integrated Care
Boards coming into play.

00:07:30.720 --> 00:07:34.590
Now, they have emerged well and strongly,

00:07:34.590 --> 00:07:37.020
although lots of different
challenges around them.

00:07:37.020 --> 00:07:39.000
And they each face different challenges

00:07:39.000 --> 00:07:42.000
according to the communities
they face off to,

00:07:42.000 --> 00:07:44.820
but also this clear
also financial challenge

00:07:44.820 --> 00:07:46.680
that they have to manage.

00:07:46.680 --> 00:07:48.810
We also face a huge task of absorbing

00:07:48.810 --> 00:07:53.400
or merging with Health Education
England and NHS Digital.

00:07:53.400 --> 00:07:57.540
Health Education England,
overseeing all education

00:07:57.540 --> 00:08:00.360
and training, initial and continuing,

00:08:00.360 --> 00:08:02.133
across the medical world.

00:08:03.360 --> 00:08:06.720
And NHS Digital, just to
give some sense to it,

00:08:06.720 --> 00:08:11.460
and now absorb within NHS
England, we now run live systems.

00:08:11.460 --> 00:08:15.210
So we run about 270 live systems,

00:08:15.210 --> 00:08:18.450
which connect with 26,000
different organisations

00:08:18.450 --> 00:08:21.720
and 44,000 IT systems.

00:08:21.720 --> 00:08:23.520
And they are essentially overseen

00:08:23.520 --> 00:08:27.180
and supervised essentially
through NHS England

00:08:27.180 --> 00:08:29.403
having absorbed NHS Digital.

00:08:32.310 --> 00:08:33.660
As I reflected on the three years,

00:08:33.660 --> 00:08:37.110
one of the things that strikes me most

00:08:37.110 --> 00:08:39.870
and I come as a finance director
from the private sector,

00:08:39.870 --> 00:08:42.576
is how well the money is actually managed.

00:08:42.576 --> 00:08:44.580
We know there's lots of
efficiency we can get

00:08:44.580 --> 00:08:46.440
and we need to be more
effective in how we do it,

00:08:46.440 --> 00:08:49.380
but actually it's a very
significant sum of money spread

00:08:49.380 --> 00:08:50.940
through that ecosystem.

00:08:50.940 --> 00:08:53.703
And I pay a huge tribute to
Julian sitting on my left

00:08:53.703 --> 00:08:55.983
that that has been
managed over this period.

00:08:56.820 --> 00:08:59.550
The whole of the process has
been managed within the money.

00:08:59.550 --> 00:09:01.203
It's not easy to do that.

00:09:02.190 --> 00:09:05.790
There's lots and lots of challenge
in actually achieving it,

00:09:05.790 --> 00:09:10.620
but actually the system has
been managed well strongly

00:09:10.620 --> 00:09:12.390
and actually, and within that productivity

00:09:12.390 --> 00:09:15.030
has been central to our thinking.

00:09:15.030 --> 00:09:16.710
And if I think about the latest year,

00:09:16.710 --> 00:09:18.720
I think we're seeing an over 2.5%,

00:09:18.720 --> 00:09:23.070
perhaps slightly better than
that productivity improvement,

00:09:23.070 --> 00:09:27.420
made cost savings of seven
billion pounds in 23-24.

00:09:27.420 --> 00:09:29.880
And we're targeting will
achieve over nine billion pounds

00:09:29.880 --> 00:09:32.560
of cost savings in the
year finishing actually

00:09:33.625 --> 00:09:35.939
at the end of this month.

00:09:35.939 --> 00:09:37.290
So the productivity picture whilst we have

00:09:37.290 --> 00:09:39.240
to do better in the oncoming year.

00:09:39.240 --> 00:09:43.713
Still, actually has been
a record of performance.

00:09:47.700 --> 00:09:48.960
What else?

00:09:48.960 --> 00:09:50.500
So I talked about delivery

00:09:52.020 --> 00:09:54.673
and I often recite delivery for the NHS.

00:09:55.647 --> 00:09:58.740
So in my first year
here, Amanda and the team

00:09:58.740 --> 00:10:00.810
produced the first ever
Longterm Workforce Plan

00:10:00.810 --> 00:10:03.333
in the history of the NHS.

00:10:04.290 --> 00:10:06.090
A seminal piece of
work, foundational piece

00:10:06.090 --> 00:10:09.210
of work that Navina leads on.

00:10:09.210 --> 00:10:12.150
We produce a long-term
infrastructure plan, capital plan,

00:10:12.150 --> 00:10:14.670
not published, but shared
with the Department of Health

00:10:14.670 --> 00:10:15.510
and with the Treasury.

00:10:15.510 --> 00:10:18.330
And that's very much the
foundational piece of work.

00:10:18.330 --> 00:10:20.190
Underpinning one of the enabling groups

00:10:20.190 --> 00:10:23.850
of the 10-year health plan.

00:10:23.850 --> 00:10:25.440
And then I think about other deliveries,

00:10:25.440 --> 00:10:28.410
and then I go into the recital
around doubling the number

00:10:28.410 --> 00:10:31.200
of community diagnostic
centres over the period,

00:10:31.200 --> 00:10:34.320
rolling out same day
emergency care into well over

00:10:34.320 --> 00:10:37.650
80% of type one hospital trusts now.

00:10:37.650 --> 00:10:40.503
Urgent treatment centres,

00:10:41.370 --> 00:10:43.890
if you actually look at virtual wards,

00:10:43.890 --> 00:10:45.260
over 12,000 virtual wards,

00:10:45.260 --> 00:10:48.420
12,700 I think from a standing start,

00:10:48.420 --> 00:10:51.150
essentially two and a half years ago,

00:10:51.150 --> 00:10:54.390
certainly taking treatment
out of the hospitals

00:10:54.390 --> 00:10:56.820
and into the community.

00:10:56.820 --> 00:10:59.220
And then you look at the
NHS app, turbocharged,

00:10:59.220 --> 00:11:00.840
in terms of the number of subscribers

00:11:00.840 --> 00:11:02.444
and the functionality now offered

00:11:02.444 --> 00:11:05.760
and the connectivity
with repeat prescriptions

00:11:05.760 --> 00:11:08.640
or actually with appointments in acute

00:11:08.640 --> 00:11:11.590
and/or in secondary care,
the award of the FDP

00:11:12.750 --> 00:11:15.120
essentially to a consortium,

00:11:15.120 --> 00:11:16.950
but led by Palantir,

00:11:16.950 --> 00:11:19.830
which actually is getting our data across

00:11:19.830 --> 00:11:22.260
our acute hospitals, interoperable

00:11:22.260 --> 00:11:24.780
and allows us actually to manage
with greater effectiveness

00:11:24.780 --> 00:11:27.190
and efficiency and see a national picture

00:11:28.110 --> 00:11:29.703
on an aggregate basis.

00:11:30.630 --> 00:11:32.040
And when I talk about the NHS,

00:11:32.040 --> 00:11:33.750
I tend to talk in four themes.

00:11:33.750 --> 00:11:36.299
So I first of all talk about demand

00:11:36.299 --> 00:11:37.380
and then I talk about capacity

00:11:37.380 --> 00:11:39.420
with which we meet that demand.

00:11:39.420 --> 00:11:43.620
And I talk about absolute record
levels of health provision

00:11:43.620 --> 00:11:45.150
and I talk about innovation.

00:11:45.150 --> 00:11:47.550
And if you let me just very
quickly touch on those,

00:11:47.550 --> 00:11:51.300
demand continues to grow

00:11:51.300 --> 00:11:53.910
and very strongly, the
population's 15% greater

00:11:53.910 --> 00:11:55.350
than it was at the turn of the century.

00:11:55.350 --> 00:11:56.280
It's older.

00:11:56.280 --> 00:12:00.810
The very good Ara Darzi
report in one narrative

00:12:00.810 --> 00:12:04.050
cogently set out the
challenge we see in demand,

00:12:04.050 --> 00:12:05.220
essentially from a growing

00:12:05.220 --> 00:12:09.180
and sicker society, so
much greater obesity,

00:12:09.180 --> 00:12:12.750
mental health issues surging,
cancer rates surging.

00:12:12.750 --> 00:12:14.820
And what we know in demand
is that many of the causes

00:12:14.820 --> 00:12:18.860
of ill health actually are
not, they're not within,

00:12:18.860 --> 00:12:22.200
if I say narrowly within
health, they emerge from factors

00:12:22.200 --> 00:12:25.470
outside health, poor housing,
deprivation, air quality,

00:12:25.470 --> 00:12:29.250
ultra processed foods,
alcohol abuse, gambling.

00:12:29.250 --> 00:12:33.990
So actually these causes of
ill health impact the citizen.

00:12:33.990 --> 00:12:36.000
And then the NHS deals with whoever comes

00:12:36.000 --> 00:12:39.060
through those gates in whatever
condition the NHS deals

00:12:39.060 --> 00:12:41.910
with whoever comes through
those gates and demand is up.

00:12:41.910 --> 00:12:45.060
And then our brilliant
academic medical scientists

00:12:45.060 --> 00:12:49.740
find ever new new ways to treat and cure

00:12:49.740 --> 00:12:51.780
and ameliorate conditions.

00:12:51.780 --> 00:12:56.163
So we also add our ability
to meet that demand.

00:12:57.180 --> 00:12:59.130
Perhaps the most striking
thing when I came into the role

00:12:59.130 --> 00:13:00.513
was capacity constraint.

00:13:01.380 --> 00:13:03.690
And so I would go around and I still do,

00:13:03.690 --> 00:13:05.400
and I say that, you
know, we've got a third

00:13:05.400 --> 00:13:06.840
scaled to population.

00:13:06.840 --> 00:13:11.070
We have a third the beds of
Germany, we have half the beds

00:13:11.070 --> 00:13:13.020
of much of Europe scaled to population.

00:13:13.020 --> 00:13:15.690
Our diagnostic capacity is less than half

00:13:15.690 --> 00:13:17.820
the average diagnostic capacity.

00:13:17.820 --> 00:13:21.810
That's Echo, MOI, CT scanners
less than half the average

00:13:21.810 --> 00:13:24.990
diagnostic capacity for the OECD.

00:13:24.990 --> 00:13:27.720
And so you see that and
one understands that

00:13:27.720 --> 00:13:29.310
as demand grows, we meet that

00:13:29.310 --> 00:13:32.340
with a very constrained capacity.

00:13:32.340 --> 00:13:34.830
And our physical estate clearly
is under very real pressure

00:13:34.830 --> 00:13:38.370
with very significant
backlog maintenance required.

00:13:38.370 --> 00:13:42.030
And we have workforce shortages
in some key disciplines.

00:13:42.030 --> 00:13:45.780
And so actually the
capacity with which we meet

00:13:45.780 --> 00:13:49.320
this demand requires real focus.

00:13:49.320 --> 00:13:51.960
And for me, the question
is partly how do we build

00:13:51.960 --> 00:13:54.030
that capacity, incremental capacity,

00:13:54.030 --> 00:13:55.740
but better probably,

00:13:55.740 --> 00:13:58.080
how do we utilise the capacity
we've got differently?

00:13:58.080 --> 00:14:00.600
How do we make sure that
patients come to the right part

00:14:00.600 --> 00:14:03.600
of our capacity at the right
time for the better treatment?

00:14:03.600 --> 00:14:06.810
And in that way we can
utilise that capacity better.

00:14:06.810 --> 00:14:09.390
And then the two last points.

00:14:09.390 --> 00:14:12.600
I talk about the absolute
record levels of provision.

00:14:12.600 --> 00:14:15.270
Today, the National Health Service

00:14:15.270 --> 00:14:17.730
is providing absolute record levels

00:14:17.730 --> 00:14:20.490
of healthcare, more health provision

00:14:20.490 --> 00:14:22.470
than we have ever provided.

00:14:22.470 --> 00:14:25.740
And so with a huge tribute to the GPs

00:14:25.740 --> 00:14:29.149
and primary care, the
fact that last year we did

00:14:29.149 --> 00:14:32.460
about 380 million primary
care appointments,

00:14:32.460 --> 00:14:35.703
that was less than 320 million in 2019.

00:14:36.930 --> 00:14:39.540
So absolute record levels
of primary care, almost half

00:14:39.540 --> 00:14:42.360
of those appointments are
appointments seen on the same day,

00:14:42.360 --> 00:14:44.190
they're the phone call
or the online booking

00:14:44.190 --> 00:14:45.420
is made for an appointment.

00:14:45.420 --> 00:14:47.070
But mainly the phone call.

00:14:47.070 --> 00:14:49.182
And about 70% of those appointments

00:14:49.182 --> 00:14:51.570
are physical appointments.

00:14:51.570 --> 00:14:56.070
Last year we did 3.2 million
cancer checks and referrals.

00:14:56.070 --> 00:14:59.400
Eight or nine years ago,
we were doing 1.3 million.

00:14:59.400 --> 00:15:01.620
And so again, if you look
at the volume of provision

00:15:01.620 --> 00:15:04.740
by the Health Service to the population,

00:15:04.740 --> 00:15:06.960
we are providing absolute record levels.

00:15:06.960 --> 00:15:09.960
And I say that, I'm
not being pollyannaish.

00:15:09.960 --> 00:15:11.490
I do understand

00:15:11.490 --> 00:15:14.580
that actually we are in many
areas not meeting the demand.

00:15:14.580 --> 00:15:16.020
We're not keeping up with the growth

00:15:16.020 --> 00:15:18.030
in demand that we're seeing.

00:15:18.030 --> 00:15:19.890
And in many areas
actually we're simply not.

00:15:19.890 --> 00:15:22.020
We're certainly not meeting
our constitutional standards.

00:15:22.020 --> 00:15:23.700
So there's much more to do.

00:15:23.700 --> 00:15:26.220
And I'm not being sort of either
it's the wrong word, vain,

00:15:26.220 --> 00:15:28.890
glorious or pollyannaish.

00:15:28.890 --> 00:15:30.000
There's much more to do.

00:15:30.000 --> 00:15:31.860
But at the same time, we should recognise

00:15:31.860 --> 00:15:33.780
that the Health Service is providing

00:15:33.780 --> 00:15:35.400
absolute record levels of healthcare.

00:15:35.400 --> 00:15:38.130
And then the last point I
talk about is innovation.

00:15:38.130 --> 00:15:40.290
And I've referenced the NHS app already

00:15:40.290 --> 00:15:41.700
turbocharging the NHS app.

00:15:41.700 --> 00:15:44.670
But if I think about
genomics, we are world leading

00:15:44.670 --> 00:15:47.970
amongst the world leaders,
the NHS in genomics.

00:15:47.970 --> 00:15:52.800
Last year, 800,000 genomics
tests, either whole genome

00:15:52.800 --> 00:15:54.030
or panel or single test.

00:15:54.030 --> 00:15:58.050
We scanned for 200 cancers
and 7,000 rare diseases.

00:15:58.050 --> 00:15:59.500
We finished the whole genome,

00:16:01.714 --> 00:16:03.960
a hundred thousand whole genome sequence.

00:16:03.960 --> 00:16:06.120
And we've just launched
the newborn sequence

00:16:06.120 --> 00:16:08.160
and actually we've started and initiated

00:16:08.160 --> 00:16:10.350
the National Genomics Research Library.

00:16:10.350 --> 00:16:14.700
So the UK, the NHS in England
is absolutely world leading.

00:16:14.700 --> 00:16:17.910
And then I think about
cancer vaccination last year,

00:16:17.910 --> 00:16:20.850
cancer vaccinations bespoke
to the DNA of the tumour

00:16:20.850 --> 00:16:22.230
and the DNA of the patient.

00:16:22.230 --> 00:16:23.760
Remarkable.

00:16:23.760 --> 00:16:25.470
And again, I always look around carefully

00:16:25.470 --> 00:16:29.339
to my very able esteemed
medical colleagues

00:16:29.339 --> 00:16:33.150
'cause this is where I go,
my layman descriptions emerge

00:16:33.150 --> 00:16:35.730
and I want to get them
right, but I then often say,

00:16:35.730 --> 00:16:37.200
we can imagine a world today,

00:16:37.200 --> 00:16:39.480
we can conceive of a world today

00:16:39.480 --> 00:16:42.030
where we might have a fourth armament

00:16:42.030 --> 00:16:43.650
with which to fight cancer.

00:16:43.650 --> 00:16:46.470
So not just actually
surgery and chemotherapy

00:16:46.470 --> 00:16:50.280
and radiotherapy, but vaccination
and vaccinations bespoke

00:16:50.280 --> 00:16:52.560
and tailored to the DNA of the tumour

00:16:52.560 --> 00:16:54.270
and the DNA of the patient.

00:16:54.270 --> 00:16:56.700
And we won't be able to
do it for every cancer

00:16:56.700 --> 00:16:59.280
of every type, but we can see a potential

00:16:59.280 --> 00:17:01.080
where it might well become

00:17:01.080 --> 00:17:04.200
a significant additional armament.

00:17:04.200 --> 00:17:05.940
And then I think about robotic surgery,

00:17:05.940 --> 00:17:07.800
I think about nanorobotics capable

00:17:07.800 --> 00:17:09.540
of individual cell repair.

00:17:09.540 --> 00:17:12.840
I think AI, so AI applied in screening

00:17:12.840 --> 00:17:14.370
and scans in dermatology

00:17:14.370 --> 00:17:16.710
and histology, in brain scans,

00:17:16.710 --> 00:17:18.570
in breast and other screening.

00:17:18.570 --> 00:17:21.000
We have liquid biopsies,
we have cyto sponges.

00:17:21.000 --> 00:17:23.100
And this is in the last
two or three years.

00:17:23.100 --> 00:17:25.290
And you think this is the NHS

00:17:25.290 --> 00:17:28.260
and that innovation is
really, really remarkable.

00:17:28.260 --> 00:17:32.763
And we can lose sight of it,
I think if we're not careful.

00:17:33.750 --> 00:17:38.750
So what do I think as I leave?

00:17:39.818 --> 00:17:41.880
I said right at the start,

00:17:41.880 --> 00:17:45.900
it's been a real privilege
to have this role.

00:17:45.900 --> 00:17:50.040
But if I was to finish with
two things, one would be

00:17:50.040 --> 00:17:52.410
there's often a common, we
have more pilots in the RAF,

00:17:52.410 --> 00:17:54.605
I'm not sure that's really quite true.

00:17:54.605 --> 00:17:55.980
But we do pilot

00:17:55.980 --> 00:17:58.590
and there were two pilot
exercises which are really

00:17:58.590 --> 00:18:01.562
important I think in the
last couple of years.

00:18:01.562 --> 00:18:04.620
And one has been a very
thoughtful cogent piece of work

00:18:04.620 --> 00:18:08.163
looking to pilot potential
new models of primary care.

00:18:09.150 --> 00:18:10.950
And that could be revolutionary, I think.

00:18:10.950 --> 00:18:14.507
And it's very much caught
up with and guided by

00:18:14.507 --> 00:18:15.450
sort of the Fuller stock take

00:18:15.450 --> 00:18:16.410
and then working from that

00:18:16.410 --> 00:18:17.610
and into neighbourhood teams

00:18:17.610 --> 00:18:20.670
and thinking of how primary
care can act as the nodal point

00:18:20.670 --> 00:18:23.340
as we think about addressing
particular cohorts

00:18:23.340 --> 00:18:25.860
of the population more proactively.

00:18:25.860 --> 00:18:27.300
And that's the second one.

00:18:27.300 --> 00:18:29.430
The second pilot is can we think

00:18:29.430 --> 00:18:33.990
about who is utilising our
constrained capacity the most?

00:18:33.990 --> 00:18:36.390
And can we make sure we
treat them in the right place

00:18:36.390 --> 00:18:37.347
and free up that capacity?

00:18:37.347 --> 00:18:41.460
And that tends to be the
frail and elderly and complex,

00:18:41.460 --> 00:18:44.280
but it's predominantly
the frail and elderly.

00:18:44.280 --> 00:18:46.407
And when we look at what
happens to us in the winter

00:18:46.407 --> 00:18:48.870
and we look at bed occupancy.

00:18:48.870 --> 00:18:51.747
So again, the stat the Board
has heard me too often,

00:18:51.747 --> 00:18:52.890
and I'm sorry I repeat it

00:18:52.890 --> 00:18:54.330
and it's a bit boring, but it's important.

00:18:54.330 --> 00:18:57.480
25% of the population is over 65.

00:18:57.480 --> 00:19:02.480
They represent 50% of admissions
and 74% of bed occupancy

00:19:02.850 --> 00:19:05.910
and 25% of our beds in acute are occupied

00:19:05.910 --> 00:19:07.560
by dementia patients.

00:19:07.560 --> 00:19:10.380
And dementia has high correlation to age.

00:19:10.380 --> 00:19:13.111
And of those dementia
patients, it's not quite 50,

00:19:13.111 --> 00:19:16.350
it's 46% our data would share with us.

00:19:16.350 --> 00:19:19.200
46% are in hospital

00:19:19.200 --> 00:19:21.030
because they've been brought in with a UTI

00:19:21.030 --> 00:19:22.050
or chest infection,

00:19:22.050 --> 00:19:23.700
which doesn't need treatment in hospital.

00:19:23.700 --> 00:19:26.460
Now it doesn't mean that
they don't have to be in

00:19:26.460 --> 00:19:28.110
for other things,

00:19:28.110 --> 00:19:30.210
but actually many of them should not have

00:19:30.210 --> 00:19:31.536
been brought into hospital.

00:19:31.536 --> 00:19:33.420
And if you think about
that capacity utilisation,

00:19:33.420 --> 00:19:37.890
how we might free it,
free it up, that pilot

00:19:37.890 --> 00:19:39.120
in how we think about looking

00:19:39.120 --> 00:19:41.100
after frail and elderly
has real potential.

00:19:41.100 --> 00:19:43.050
And it's revolutionary I think

00:19:43.050 --> 00:19:45.360
for the way the Health
Service I hope will develop.

00:19:45.360 --> 00:19:46.920
If I was writing the 10-year plan today,

00:19:46.920 --> 00:19:49.426
that would be a key enabling step.

00:19:49.426 --> 00:19:51.720
It's not necessarily

00:19:51.720 --> 00:19:54.810
that the end point in
10-years time that you say

00:19:54.810 --> 00:19:57.510
that's my target, but
for me it's the first

00:19:57.510 --> 00:19:59.670
and most important
enabling step to allow us

00:19:59.670 --> 00:20:02.493
to get to that 10-year improved vision.

00:20:03.840 --> 00:20:08.070
And then lastly, I was going
to finish just with, it's been,

00:20:08.070 --> 00:20:11.130
I think for me under Amanda's leadership,

00:20:11.130 --> 00:20:12.390
and it's been an absolute privilege

00:20:12.390 --> 00:20:14.730
to work alongside Amanda.

00:20:14.730 --> 00:20:18.420
It has been a remarkable three
years of delivery actually,

00:20:18.420 --> 00:20:21.720
and of steady operational improvement

00:20:21.720 --> 00:20:22.980
in each of those years.

00:20:22.980 --> 00:20:25.080
So absolute record levels
of healthcare are being

00:20:25.080 --> 00:20:29.040
provided today from an
under-invested capacity.

00:20:29.040 --> 00:20:33.150
And at the same time facing
fast rising new demand.

00:20:33.150 --> 00:20:36.630
And solving this equation,
trying to keep up,

00:20:36.630 --> 00:20:39.153
it cannot be done by the NHS alone,

00:20:40.560 --> 00:20:43.620
actually it can be done if
we do better cross boundary

00:20:43.620 --> 00:20:46.080
working with a much better invested

00:20:46.080 --> 00:20:48.300
and run social care system.

00:20:48.300 --> 00:20:51.120
And perhaps, and this is where
I get myself in some trouble,

00:20:51.120 --> 00:20:54.300
I think with a more
interventionist government

00:20:54.300 --> 00:20:56.700
on the broader causes of ill health.

00:20:56.700 --> 00:21:00.540
So actually how can we manage
causes of ill health better

00:21:00.540 --> 00:21:02.910
to reduce the demand that's
coming at the Health Service?

00:21:02.910 --> 00:21:05.670
Otherwise, we deal with whoever
comes through those gates.

00:21:05.670 --> 00:21:08.370
So thank you for listening to me

00:21:08.370 --> 00:21:12.303
and I'm going to finish
with huge thanks actually.

00:21:13.650 --> 00:21:17.610
So Amanda Prichard, you know,

00:21:17.610 --> 00:21:20.910
I was partly brought in to mentor you.

00:21:20.910 --> 00:21:23.460
It's actually been the other
way round to be honest.

00:21:23.460 --> 00:21:26.373
And Amanda, you are an
outstanding chief executive.

00:21:27.510 --> 00:21:30.960
I said earlier I was a
finance director in my past

00:21:30.960 --> 00:21:33.690
and then I met a real one in Julian Kelly

00:21:33.690 --> 00:21:37.170
who has been outstanding
as the finance director.

00:21:37.170 --> 00:21:39.423
And Emily, where is Emily?

00:21:40.590 --> 00:21:41.910
And Emily, you,

00:21:41.910 --> 00:21:43.620
I'm not going to use my
earlier description about being

00:21:43.620 --> 00:21:46.650
Cardinal Woolsey and true to history,

00:21:46.650 --> 00:21:49.260
but actually the way you
have managed and balanced

00:21:49.260 --> 00:21:52.050
and engaged with the
systems and the trusts

00:21:52.050 --> 00:21:53.730
and the operational oversight

00:21:53.730 --> 00:21:55.230
and the performance has been outstanding.

00:21:55.230 --> 00:21:57.390
And we will miss you hugely

00:21:57.390 --> 00:22:02.010
and Steve Russell, Steve not
just carrying vaccination

00:22:02.010 --> 00:22:04.113
and screening, but
actually much of the way

00:22:04.113 --> 00:22:08.910
that NHS England runs and
operates is run by you

00:22:08.910 --> 00:22:11.790
and it's a hugely, hugely demanding job

00:22:11.790 --> 00:22:13.920
and you've done it absolutely excellently.

00:22:13.920 --> 00:22:15.330
And so we will miss you.

00:22:15.330 --> 00:22:17.700
So the four of you going,
I think it's a huge loss

00:22:17.700 --> 00:22:22.700
for the NHS, but then
actually Jim, welcome to you

00:22:22.980 --> 00:22:24.750
and then we have a new
finance expert coming

00:22:24.750 --> 00:22:25.770
in the transitional team.

00:22:25.770 --> 00:22:28.500
And when Penny joins us from her meeting,

00:22:28.500 --> 00:22:29.790
we have a new chair.

00:22:29.790 --> 00:22:33.000
But my thanks hugely
to Amanda and to Julian

00:22:33.000 --> 00:22:35.550
and to Emily and to Steve.

00:22:35.550 --> 00:22:38.010
So thank you for hearing me on that.

00:22:38.010 --> 00:22:39.760
I'm going to hand Amanda to you now for

00:22:39.760 --> 00:22:42.210
a Chief Execs Update.

00:22:42.210 --> 00:22:43.517
- Richard, thank you very much indeed.

00:22:43.517 --> 00:22:45.550
It was an absolute tour de force

00:22:46.740 --> 00:22:49.470
of a reflection back
on the last few years.

00:22:49.470 --> 00:22:51.180
And forgive me colleagues,

00:22:51.180 --> 00:22:53.670
'cause I'm going to just not
let people get away without

00:22:53.670 --> 00:22:55.290
me saying a word about
some of my colleagues

00:22:55.290 --> 00:22:57.300
who are stepping away as well.

00:22:57.300 --> 00:22:58.710
But I think just worth saying,

00:22:58.710 --> 00:23:00.660
of course I will touch on it in a moment,

00:23:00.660 --> 00:23:02.520
but a lot has happened since

00:23:02.520 --> 00:23:05.190
we were last together as a Board.

00:23:05.190 --> 00:23:07.920
So we will want to talk
about some of that.

00:23:07.920 --> 00:23:09.210
And I'll just want to say a word

00:23:09.210 --> 00:23:11.910
or two about that as well, if I may.

00:23:11.910 --> 00:23:15.990
But before I do, I would
like, I think partly to echo

00:23:15.990 --> 00:23:17.580
what Richard has said,

00:23:17.580 --> 00:23:22.500
but just to say an enormous
thank you to my colleagues

00:23:22.500 --> 00:23:25.980
who will be leaving us after this Board.

00:23:25.980 --> 00:23:29.711
So Emily, you've, I know you
can't bear it, by the way,

00:23:29.711 --> 00:23:31.020
I know no one can bear
it and it's just awful

00:23:31.020 --> 00:23:32.730
but I'm going to do it anyway.

00:23:32.730 --> 00:23:35.880
But you know, Emily, from
that extraordinary job you did

00:23:35.880 --> 00:23:40.410
for us in the pandemic leading
the COVID vaccine rollout

00:23:40.410 --> 00:23:43.200
and then doing that thing
that really great people do,

00:23:43.200 --> 00:23:45.900
which is say, okay, I hear
it, your country needs you,

00:23:45.900 --> 00:23:50.100
I'll come back having stepped
out to support number 10

00:23:50.100 --> 00:23:52.710
for a period of time, thank you.

00:23:52.710 --> 00:23:56.280
And then leading the delivery
over the last couple of years

00:23:56.280 --> 00:24:00.030
to Steve who then picked up
the baton on vaccine screening,

00:24:00.030 --> 00:24:04.590
as we've said, first ever
rollout of the RSV vaccine,

00:24:04.590 --> 00:24:05.940
as well as all of the other things

00:24:05.940 --> 00:24:07.410
that you've done as
part of that programme,

00:24:07.410 --> 00:24:10.170
delivering the new NHS England programme.

00:24:10.170 --> 00:24:11.760
I know we're about to go again,

00:24:11.760 --> 00:24:15.150
but worth just saying that
did release about, you know,

00:24:15.150 --> 00:24:16.530
half a billion pounds

00:24:16.530 --> 00:24:20.070
of taxpayers money into supporting
the frontline of the NHS

00:24:20.070 --> 00:24:23.670
and we have already reduced
by about what, 35 plus percent

00:24:23.670 --> 00:24:25.797
as a result of that and brought together

00:24:25.797 --> 00:24:29.730
NHS Digital, HEE as part
of our organisation.

00:24:29.730 --> 00:24:32.670
So a huge, hugely complex
programme of work.

00:24:32.670 --> 00:24:35.293
- Can I interrupt just on that point?

00:24:35.293 --> 00:24:36.126
And so that programme,
we should just reflect,

00:24:36.126 --> 00:24:39.870
I'm sorry if I'm sounding a bit stats mad,

00:24:39.870 --> 00:24:43.820
but when those entities
came together in July '22,

00:24:43.820 --> 00:24:46.410
it actually produced a joint
headcount in essentially

00:24:46.410 --> 00:24:48.213
the head office of 24,000.

00:24:49.320 --> 00:24:50.610
And 20 months later

00:24:50.610 --> 00:24:53.100
that had been reduced to 15,000.

00:24:53.100 --> 00:24:56.160
That 9,000 reduction is the
source of the 500 million

00:24:56.160 --> 00:24:57.390
of freed up resource.

00:24:57.390 --> 00:24:59.730
It also de-bureaucratized a lot

00:24:59.730 --> 00:25:02.673
of the pressures from the
centre down into the system.

00:25:03.780 --> 00:25:05.400
That number 9,000 is probably,

00:25:05.400 --> 00:25:10.050
I think the largest single
public sector reduction

00:25:10.050 --> 00:25:11.220
that I've been able to find,

00:25:11.220 --> 00:25:14.010
the largest single reduction exercise.

00:25:14.010 --> 00:25:17.070
And it was executed,
carried out brilliantly well

00:25:17.070 --> 00:25:20.250
by a group of non-execs
and execs working together.

00:25:20.250 --> 00:25:23.323
So Mark Cubbon started,
Steve, you picked it up

00:25:23.323 --> 00:25:24.156
and finished that.

00:25:24.156 --> 00:25:24.989
So thank you.

00:25:24.989 --> 00:25:26.430
The numbers today are down further.

00:25:26.430 --> 00:25:28.530
So we've continued to reduce
the headcount already.

00:25:28.530 --> 00:25:32.173
So the actual headcount
is now a touch over 13,000

00:25:33.736 --> 00:25:35.040
in that combined group.

00:25:35.040 --> 00:25:37.140
And now we have to go
again with, you know,

00:25:37.140 --> 00:25:39.870
the new desires effectively to again

00:25:39.870 --> 00:25:44.007
to rationalise further
the way that we operate

00:25:44.007 --> 00:25:46.590
and work ever more closely
with the Department.

00:25:46.590 --> 00:25:48.062
But sorry to interrupt.

00:25:48.062 --> 00:25:49.440
- Yeah, no absolutely right Richard.

00:25:49.440 --> 00:25:50.273
And I'm going to go on and say

00:25:50.273 --> 00:25:52.200
just a bit more if I may,
about the change programme.

00:25:52.200 --> 00:25:53.970
But it's really just to acknowledge Steve,

00:25:53.970 --> 00:25:55.260
your role in leading that and also

00:25:55.260 --> 00:25:57.360
of course there are hugely
valuable learning from that

00:25:57.360 --> 00:25:59.790
which we'll now make sure
is fed into the next phase

00:25:59.790 --> 00:26:00.663
of the work.

00:26:01.530 --> 00:26:05.520
To Julian, who has made an
extraordinary contribution

00:26:05.520 --> 00:26:06.960
as not just chief finance officer

00:26:06.960 --> 00:26:09.720
but as deputy chief executive including,

00:26:09.720 --> 00:26:11.610
and we're going to talk
about this a bit later,

00:26:11.610 --> 00:26:15.900
driving significant productivity
improvements post pandemic

00:26:15.900 --> 00:26:18.630
but also, which is less, I think obvious,

00:26:18.630 --> 00:26:19.980
but Julian's role in many

00:26:19.980 --> 00:26:22.920
of the key strategic developments
over the past few years.

00:26:22.920 --> 00:26:25.140
You've mentioned the
Longterm Workforce Plan,

00:26:25.140 --> 00:26:27.300
Julian, the tech and data agenda

00:26:27.300 --> 00:26:28.500
almost wherever you look you'll find

00:26:28.500 --> 00:26:31.680
Julian's hands on it behind the scenes.

00:26:31.680 --> 00:26:34.440
And I was delighted

00:26:34.440 --> 00:26:36.750
to hear Julian's contribution recognised

00:26:36.750 --> 00:26:38.760
by the Secretary of State
in the House recently

00:26:38.760 --> 00:26:39.840
when he described Julian

00:26:39.840 --> 00:26:44.190
as one of the outstanding public
servants of his generation,

00:26:44.190 --> 00:26:46.413
which I think we would all agree with.

00:26:47.460 --> 00:26:49.710
Steve Powis is not here with us yet.

00:26:49.710 --> 00:26:51.240
I know he's joining us a bit later

00:26:51.240 --> 00:26:52.680
and this won't be his last Board

00:26:52.680 --> 00:26:55.290
'cause he doesn't go
'til later in the summer.

00:26:55.290 --> 00:26:56.610
But I didn't want him

00:26:56.610 --> 00:26:59.670
to get away without having
something said about him.

00:26:59.670 --> 00:27:03.250
So again, I just like to
say from my perspective

00:27:04.110 --> 00:27:05.940
the value of Steve's contribution,

00:27:05.940 --> 00:27:08.970
if we all remember those
hard yards in the pandemic.

00:27:08.970 --> 00:27:12.000
And Steve really did reach
right across government

00:27:12.000 --> 00:27:15.240
through into through clinical leadership,

00:27:15.240 --> 00:27:17.040
right into people's living rooms

00:27:17.040 --> 00:27:19.500
as we saw him many times appear next

00:27:19.500 --> 00:27:22.590
to the prime minister at
the press conferences.

00:27:22.590 --> 00:27:24.720
But you know, he has gone on from there

00:27:24.720 --> 00:27:28.080
to oversee some really important
work on quality, on safety,

00:27:28.080 --> 00:27:30.120
on secondary prevention
among many other things.

00:27:30.120 --> 00:27:32.013
So again, a huge contribution.

00:27:32.910 --> 00:27:34.610
On behalf, if I may,

00:27:34.610 --> 00:27:37.260
of all of my executive
colleagues, I would also

00:27:37.260 --> 00:27:41.340
just like to thank our
non-executive colleagues

00:27:41.340 --> 00:27:45.150
because you have provided
fantastic guidance,

00:27:45.150 --> 00:27:50.067
support, challenge over
the last few years.

00:27:50.067 --> 00:27:52.860
And I know you will go
on to do the same for Jim

00:27:52.860 --> 00:27:54.240
and for Penny.

00:27:54.240 --> 00:27:55.503
To Andrew and Wol,

00:27:56.657 --> 00:27:59.010
we said what we wanted to
say about them last time

00:27:59.010 --> 00:28:01.680
and they've had their last meetings,

00:28:01.680 --> 00:28:04.320
but obviously also to you Richard,

00:28:04.320 --> 00:28:06.300
I did talk a bit at the last meeting about

00:28:06.300 --> 00:28:07.410
some of the things that you've done.

00:28:07.410 --> 00:28:08.243
You've also just talked about a lot

00:28:08.243 --> 00:28:10.663
of the things you've done
the last few minutes,

00:28:11.820 --> 00:28:14.430
but brilliant because
it's absolutely right

00:28:14.430 --> 00:28:16.020
to just take a moment to look back

00:28:16.020 --> 00:28:18.090
when you reach the end of the tenure.

00:28:18.090 --> 00:28:20.250
But I think again, the thing I wanted

00:28:20.250 --> 00:28:21.720
to say is it's not just us

00:28:21.720 --> 00:28:23.670
that have noticed the contribution

00:28:23.670 --> 00:28:25.560
that Richard has made
over the past few years.

00:28:25.560 --> 00:28:28.950
Again, fantastic to see that
the work you've done, Richard,

00:28:28.950 --> 00:28:30.540
has now been publicly acknowledged

00:28:30.540 --> 00:28:33.870
with the lifetime achievement
award at a very prestigious

00:28:33.870 --> 00:28:38.070
non-executive director
awards just recently.

00:28:38.070 --> 00:28:40.650
And on a personal level, just thank you

00:28:40.650 --> 00:28:43.380
for the absolutely invaluable support

00:28:43.380 --> 00:28:45.240
you have given me over the last years.

00:28:45.240 --> 00:28:48.090
It has been an enormous
pleasure, a privilege

00:28:48.090 --> 00:28:50.460
to work with you Richard.

00:28:50.460 --> 00:28:52.560
So for all of my other executive

00:28:52.560 --> 00:28:54.420
and non-executive colleagues

00:28:54.420 --> 00:28:57.690
who will be remaining on
the Board, Jim, Penny,

00:28:57.690 --> 00:28:59.160
I keep looking at an empty chair,

00:28:59.160 --> 00:29:00.810
which feels very unfair, doesn't it?

00:29:00.810 --> 00:29:02.700
Jim, Penny and the transition team

00:29:02.700 --> 00:29:04.290
that are coming in will bring

00:29:04.290 --> 00:29:07.530
with them enormous energy, expertise.

00:29:07.530 --> 00:29:10.772
I cannot think of anybody that you know

00:29:10.772 --> 00:29:12.030
who could provide better leadership

00:29:12.030 --> 00:29:14.280
for the NHS right now, Jim, than you.

00:29:14.280 --> 00:29:17.250
But I know that you
will all give Jim, Penny

00:29:17.250 --> 00:29:20.250
and the team the support
that you have given us

00:29:20.250 --> 00:29:22.830
and that you will bring some
of that sort of, you know,

00:29:22.830 --> 00:29:24.120
organisational memory

00:29:24.120 --> 00:29:26.820
and some of the continuity from

00:29:26.820 --> 00:29:29.310
where we've been over the last
years, which will, you know,

00:29:29.310 --> 00:29:31.500
complement that new energy

00:29:31.500 --> 00:29:35.160
and fresh perspective
that they will bring.

00:29:35.160 --> 00:29:37.837
So thank you kind of in advance for

00:29:37.837 --> 00:29:39.750
what I know colleagues
will do to support Jim

00:29:39.750 --> 00:29:41.730
and to support Penny.

00:29:41.730 --> 00:29:44.730
Oh, I should say actually yeah,
Richard's just prompted me.

00:29:44.730 --> 00:29:46.620
We did say it would be Andrew,
I did say a moment ago,

00:29:46.620 --> 00:29:49.200
it was Andrew's last Board,
it was Andrew's last Board

00:29:49.200 --> 00:29:52.170
before he agreed to stay
on for another six months.

00:29:52.170 --> 00:29:55.050
So actually Andrew is
another really important part

00:29:55.050 --> 00:29:56.730
of providing a bit of that continuity

00:29:56.730 --> 00:29:58.173
and support to the team.

00:29:59.043 --> 00:29:59.876
- Oh Amanda, can I just say a word?

00:29:59.876 --> 00:30:01.967
So just like Frank Sinatra.

00:30:01.967 --> 00:30:02.937
- There is.

00:30:02.937 --> 00:30:05.650
- But also, I just want
to pay tribute to Richard

00:30:06.510 --> 00:30:07.710
for all the work he's done.

00:30:07.710 --> 00:30:11.530
So this man is a complete
encyclopaedia of facts

00:30:12.390 --> 00:30:15.760
to do with the NHS, a master of narrative

00:30:16.620 --> 00:30:19.500
and he's been really, you know, top class

00:30:19.500 --> 00:30:22.800
in terms of galvanising the Board,

00:30:22.800 --> 00:30:26.970
getting it focused on a
number of critical issues

00:30:26.970 --> 00:30:29.970
and you know, levering up performance.

00:30:29.970 --> 00:30:31.770
And he's been a pleasure to work with.

00:30:31.770 --> 00:30:35.130
This has been a truly,
you know, unitary Board

00:30:35.130 --> 00:30:37.710
where everyone has a contribution

00:30:37.710 --> 00:30:40.680
and you've always made sure
no matter what the point is,

00:30:40.680 --> 00:30:42.180
everyone has a say,

00:30:42.180 --> 00:30:44.400
which I think is really, really important.

00:30:44.400 --> 00:30:47.850
But your focus is on delivering
the best possible care

00:30:47.850 --> 00:30:50.100
for the population of this country.

00:30:50.100 --> 00:30:53.160
And that has been, you know,
absolutely at the forefront

00:30:53.160 --> 00:30:55.170
of every agenda that we've had

00:30:55.170 --> 00:30:56.670
and we have seen some improvements.

00:30:56.670 --> 00:31:00.180
So for the first time,
performance against key targets

00:31:00.180 --> 00:31:01.890
has started to improve.

00:31:01.890 --> 00:31:03.030
So we did it last year.

00:31:03.030 --> 00:31:04.650
The same will happen this year.

00:31:04.650 --> 00:31:06.450
And that's all down to your leadership

00:31:06.450 --> 00:31:09.870
and the way in which you,
you know, worked with Amanda

00:31:09.870 --> 00:31:13.710
and got the whole of the NHS
moving in the right direction.

00:31:13.710 --> 00:31:16.260
And the NHS is a blooming,
you know, big tanker,

00:31:16.260 --> 00:31:21.260
186 billion a year employing
one and a half million people.

00:31:21.750 --> 00:31:24.060
And it is moving in the right direction.

00:31:24.060 --> 00:31:26.130
And I think that's due to, you know,

00:31:26.130 --> 00:31:29.310
some really outstanding
leadership from both of you.

00:31:29.310 --> 00:31:31.060
So on behalf of the NEDs,
thank you very much.

00:31:33.330 --> 00:31:34.710
- Andrew, thank you.

00:31:34.710 --> 00:31:37.650
And I'm conscious just as
we're reflecting on some

00:31:37.650 --> 00:31:39.300
of the changes that are
happening at executive

00:31:39.300 --> 00:31:42.129
and non-executive level

00:31:42.129 --> 00:31:44.970
and it's, we get in a sense
the luxury of having a moment

00:31:44.970 --> 00:31:48.990
to acknowledge that in this setting

00:31:48.990 --> 00:31:51.330
and to welcome our successors.

00:31:51.330 --> 00:31:53.220
I think if I just might move on

00:31:53.220 --> 00:31:54.720
just to talk a bit about the changes

00:31:54.720 --> 00:31:55.860
within our organisation.

00:31:55.860 --> 00:31:58.380
I am really conscious
that we have an awful lot

00:31:58.380 --> 00:32:00.090
of colleagues who are feeling really

00:32:00.090 --> 00:32:02.310
very unsettled at the moment.

00:32:02.310 --> 00:32:04.560
So aware of some of those
changes, aware of some

00:32:04.560 --> 00:32:06.420
of the bigger changes
for the organisation,

00:32:06.420 --> 00:32:09.870
but are not yet clear exactly
what that is going to mean

00:32:09.870 --> 00:32:14.190
for them in their professional lives.

00:32:14.190 --> 00:32:18.120
So again, just to sort
of say, which I know

00:32:18.120 --> 00:32:19.770
I have said before, but to sort put it,

00:32:19.770 --> 00:32:22.710
I guess on the record, you all know

00:32:22.710 --> 00:32:23.760
because I've said it before,

00:32:23.760 --> 00:32:26.643
that I do support the
need for a smaller centre.

00:32:27.833 --> 00:32:30.620
And for the Department, for NHS England

00:32:30.620 --> 00:32:33.180
and for other parts of the
centre to work better together

00:32:33.180 --> 00:32:37.080
to reduce duplication, to drive
priorities more effectively

00:32:37.080 --> 00:32:39.510
and to give local teams
in the NHS the space

00:32:39.510 --> 00:32:42.963
and the support to innovate
and deliver for patients.

00:32:43.894 --> 00:32:45.600
And being able, again,
we've discussed it before,

00:32:45.600 --> 00:32:48.810
but being able to pass
a greater proportion

00:32:48.810 --> 00:32:51.057
of the NHS budget through to systems

00:32:51.057 --> 00:32:53.040
and the frontline of we've consistently,

00:32:53.040 --> 00:32:54.690
I've just mentioned it,
you know, been trying to do

00:32:54.690 --> 00:32:57.360
and have done over the last
few years is the right thing

00:32:57.360 --> 00:32:59.610
to do, particularly at time

00:32:59.610 --> 00:33:01.680
of such significant financial challenge.

00:33:01.680 --> 00:33:03.930
And that's, you know, a challenge

00:33:03.930 --> 00:33:06.900
that is a global challenge,
not just one for this country.

00:33:06.900 --> 00:33:10.770
And I hope that nobody in
NHS England would disagree

00:33:10.770 --> 00:33:12.630
with either of those two points.

00:33:12.630 --> 00:33:15.600
The value of avoiding
duplication, of making us as lean,

00:33:15.600 --> 00:33:16.770
as effective as possible

00:33:16.770 --> 00:33:19.320
or indeed the importance
of passing resource

00:33:19.320 --> 00:33:21.033
on to the front line.

00:33:22.380 --> 00:33:25.560
But saying that, changes of this magnitude

00:33:25.560 --> 00:33:29.640
clearly do affect kind of
everyone in the organisation.

00:33:29.640 --> 00:33:33.780
And I know that that will be
at the forefront of the minds

00:33:33.780 --> 00:33:36.150
of colleagues who are staying on

00:33:36.150 --> 00:33:37.980
as part of the leadership team

00:33:37.980 --> 00:33:41.640
for NHS England over
this transitional phase.

00:33:41.640 --> 00:33:44.390
And I do know, and you
may want to talk about it

00:33:44.390 --> 00:33:45.723
a bit later,

00:33:45.723 --> 00:33:48.207
Penny, Jim, but I do know that, you know,

00:33:49.075 --> 00:33:51.990
you're hugely personally
committed to making sure

00:33:51.990 --> 00:33:54.840
that things are done now
as swiftly, as fairly

00:33:54.840 --> 00:33:57.300
and as sensitively as possible
in terms of getting through

00:33:57.300 --> 00:33:58.983
that period of uncertainty.

00:33:59.970 --> 00:34:01.650
So that's important I think partly

00:34:01.650 --> 00:34:03.480
because you know, these are our colleagues

00:34:03.480 --> 00:34:06.330
and hugely valuable members of the team.

00:34:06.330 --> 00:34:08.010
We want to look after people.

00:34:08.010 --> 00:34:11.610
But it's also important because
we've still got a job to do

00:34:11.610 --> 00:34:14.250
and a big part of the job at the moment.

00:34:14.250 --> 00:34:16.950
Of course it's the usual stuff
we have at this time of year,

00:34:16.950 --> 00:34:21.243
which is both the operational
and financial delivery agenda.

00:34:22.984 --> 00:34:25.320
So the pressures of
today at managing today,

00:34:25.320 --> 00:34:27.753
but also planning for next year,

00:34:28.738 --> 00:34:30.870
which is a hugely important exercise.

00:34:30.870 --> 00:34:32.520
Again, both of those things,

00:34:32.520 --> 00:34:36.150
Emily will talk a bit about
the operational and delivery.

00:34:36.150 --> 00:34:37.740
Julian will talk about
the financial delivery

00:34:37.740 --> 00:34:39.390
and then Julian and Jim
will talk a bit about

00:34:39.390 --> 00:34:40.773
where we are on planning.

00:34:41.640 --> 00:34:43.110
The other thing that
we're doing at the moment,

00:34:43.110 --> 00:34:45.450
and this is really
exciting, is we've got lots

00:34:45.450 --> 00:34:47.250
of colleagues contributing on

00:34:47.250 --> 00:34:49.680
and working with the
Department on the development

00:34:49.680 --> 00:34:51.600
of the 10-year health plan.

00:34:51.600 --> 00:34:54.630
And that is genuinely
just a huge opportunity

00:34:54.630 --> 00:34:55.557
and not just for the NHS

00:34:55.557 --> 00:34:58.230
but for the country to set out a vision

00:34:58.230 --> 00:34:59.790
for the next 10 years

00:34:59.790 --> 00:35:01.770
and then a consistent delivery plan

00:35:01.770 --> 00:35:03.990
to support us actually getting there.

00:35:03.990 --> 00:35:06.150
So again, confident that Jim and Penny

00:35:06.150 --> 00:35:08.220
might want to say a bit more about that

00:35:08.220 --> 00:35:09.690
in their remarks.

00:35:09.690 --> 00:35:13.950
I should say, we have, I think
covered most of those things.

00:35:13.950 --> 00:35:16.920
In fact all of those things in
some way on our agenda today

00:35:16.920 --> 00:35:19.230
as we go through Richard

00:35:19.230 --> 00:35:21.480
and indeed we've spent some
time on all of those things

00:35:21.480 --> 00:35:24.993
at our Private Board
before this conversation.

00:35:26.010 --> 00:35:28.680
But I think we will
also just take a moment

00:35:28.680 --> 00:35:30.040
as you just did Richard

00:35:30.996 --> 00:35:34.590
and Emily and Julian will cover
as well just to reflect back

00:35:34.590 --> 00:35:36.240
'cause we are at year end now

00:35:36.240 --> 00:35:38.367
on what we have achieved
over the past year

00:35:38.367 --> 00:35:39.873
and what is still to do.

00:35:40.740 --> 00:35:43.050
Indeed, what has been achieved
over the past few years

00:35:43.050 --> 00:35:44.190
and where that leaves us.

00:35:44.190 --> 00:35:48.180
So there is an accompanying
paper for this item,

00:35:48.180 --> 00:35:50.970
which is a written paper

00:35:50.970 --> 00:35:54.570
but also slides which just
sort of try to tell the story

00:35:54.570 --> 00:35:58.740
of the last few years in
hopefully a way which is clear

00:35:58.740 --> 00:36:00.780
and helpful for colleagues.

00:36:00.780 --> 00:36:02.957
As I say, it's a bit of
a here you go for some

00:36:02.957 --> 00:36:05.250
of the things we've learned and done well,

00:36:05.250 --> 00:36:06.930
but also some of the challenges

00:36:06.930 --> 00:36:08.460
that I think we would all recognise.

00:36:08.460 --> 00:36:11.340
So some of the things
that we would call out,

00:36:11.340 --> 00:36:14.040
I think if I may, you've covered some

00:36:14.040 --> 00:36:15.720
of these already Richard, but you know,

00:36:15.720 --> 00:36:19.440
following the enormous
shock of the pandemic,

00:36:19.440 --> 00:36:22.770
we have virtually
eliminated the longest waits

00:36:22.770 --> 00:36:25.080
for patients waiting for elective care

00:36:25.080 --> 00:36:27.810
and half the number of
people waiting over a year.

00:36:27.810 --> 00:36:29.910
Under your leadership, Jim.

00:36:29.910 --> 00:36:32.190
GPs, you've talked about
the volume of activity

00:36:32.190 --> 00:36:34.230
that primary care
colleagues are now doing,

00:36:34.230 --> 00:36:37.950
but we've also got now
consistent improvements

00:36:37.950 --> 00:36:42.570
in the experience of
access to primary care.

00:36:42.570 --> 00:36:45.270
And we're beginning to see the numbers

00:36:45.270 --> 00:36:50.270
of fully qualified GPs really,
really increase this year.

00:36:50.730 --> 00:36:53.130
Lots of investment in training
over the last few years,

00:36:53.130 --> 00:36:57.960
but now actually converting
into the ability to appoint

00:36:57.960 --> 00:37:00.360
fully qualified colleagues in primary care

00:37:00.360 --> 00:37:02.700
making a huge difference.

00:37:02.700 --> 00:37:05.640
Productivity, we'll talk
about properly in a moment,

00:37:05.640 --> 00:37:08.700
but in the first nine months of this year,

00:37:08.700 --> 00:37:10.410
I think Julian will
correct me if I'm wrong,

00:37:10.410 --> 00:37:14.490
but we now think that
acute sector productivity

00:37:14.490 --> 00:37:16.013
is up by 2.7%.

00:37:17.400 --> 00:37:22.110
That is significantly more
than double rates pre-pandemic.

00:37:22.110 --> 00:37:24.150
So just three things,

00:37:24.150 --> 00:37:27.480
but three examples of
where I think real progress

00:37:27.480 --> 00:37:29.910
and more importantly sustained progress.

00:37:29.910 --> 00:37:30.930
And that's your point Andrew.

00:37:30.930 --> 00:37:32.970
This isn't just a one-off, this
is something we've been able

00:37:32.970 --> 00:37:36.723
to do over the past now two plus years,

00:37:38.121 --> 00:37:39.840
which is a much better foundation

00:37:39.840 --> 00:37:42.540
for the next phase than where we were

00:37:42.540 --> 00:37:44.760
when we picked up post-pandemic.

00:37:44.760 --> 00:37:48.240
Important reforms that
we've also delivered

00:37:48.240 --> 00:37:51.780
from mental health teams now
in half of schools in England

00:37:51.780 --> 00:37:53.340
to the thousands of virtual wards,

00:37:53.340 --> 00:37:55.110
meaning patients can get you know,

00:37:55.110 --> 00:37:57.420
hospital style care at home.

00:37:57.420 --> 00:38:00.600
Just want to call out a
couple more, Martha's Rule,

00:38:00.600 --> 00:38:03.600
which I think we would now say
has the potential to be one

00:38:03.600 --> 00:38:07.350
of the most important
patient safety interventions

00:38:07.350 --> 00:38:11.700
we've ever seen in the NHS
already saving hundreds of lives.

00:38:11.700 --> 00:38:15.300
And I think we'll undoubtedly
save countless more

00:38:15.300 --> 00:38:16.920
and it's changing the way we work

00:38:16.920 --> 00:38:18.180
in a really fundamental way

00:38:18.180 --> 00:38:20.880
'cause it's putting much
more power in the hands

00:38:20.880 --> 00:38:22.830
of patients and families.

00:38:22.830 --> 00:38:25.213
And you know, that is
something we talk about.

00:38:25.213 --> 00:38:27.300
I don't like the phrase
patient empowerment

00:38:27.300 --> 00:38:29.190
because I don't like the
idea that people should have

00:38:29.190 --> 00:38:32.640
to be empowered, actually
that the relationship needs

00:38:32.640 --> 00:38:34.650
to be the other way round
with the Health Service.

00:38:34.650 --> 00:38:35.730
But this is a great example

00:38:35.730 --> 00:38:37.830
of really living that in practise

00:38:37.830 --> 00:38:41.160
and seeing that shift
in the balance of power

00:38:41.160 --> 00:38:44.610
and the real benefits that
can drive both the patients

00:38:44.610 --> 00:38:46.830
and for clinicians.

00:38:46.830 --> 00:38:50.037
And then looking to the future
at the end of last year,

00:38:50.037 --> 00:38:54.270
end of 2023, I was really
pleased that I was able

00:38:54.270 --> 00:38:56.460
to announce a lifesaving

00:38:56.460 --> 00:38:59.550
and world-leading new ambition
for the NHS, which was

00:38:59.550 --> 00:39:03.870
to eliminate cervical
cancer in England by 2040,

00:39:03.870 --> 00:39:07.710
which was driven by a
combination of HPV vaccination

00:39:07.710 --> 00:39:11.280
and the highly effective
cervical screening programme.

00:39:11.280 --> 00:39:13.253
And tomorrow, I'm
looking at Steve on this,

00:39:13.253 --> 00:39:15.720
I think I'm right in saying it's tomorrow,

00:39:15.720 --> 00:39:17.760
we're going to be
publishing the delivery plan

00:39:17.760 --> 00:39:20.850
that sets out the next
steps towards that ambition,

00:39:20.850 --> 00:39:24.150
including ramping up
preparations to roll out

00:39:24.150 --> 00:39:25.980
or potentially roll out self sampling

00:39:25.980 --> 00:39:30.330
to support many more women
to access cervical screening.

00:39:30.330 --> 00:39:33.720
So we do need to do, we
know this, to do all we can

00:39:33.720 --> 00:39:35.850
to make those programmes
more convenient for women

00:39:35.850 --> 00:39:40.830
and to make sure that
those two things together,

00:39:40.830 --> 00:39:45.300
the screening and the
vaccination really do deliver

00:39:45.300 --> 00:39:47.340
what they clearly have,
which is the potential

00:39:47.340 --> 00:39:50.760
to make cervical cancer
genuinely a disease of the past.

00:39:50.760 --> 00:39:53.460
Which is a completely remarkable
thing to be able to say,

00:39:53.460 --> 00:39:57.300
but it is within our grasp to
be able to actually do that.

00:39:57.300 --> 00:39:59.580
So final words from me.

00:39:59.580 --> 00:40:02.220
I do think that the NHS has evidenced

00:40:02.220 --> 00:40:06.240
time and time again that it has
the determination, the skill

00:40:06.240 --> 00:40:10.500
and the innovative spirit
needed to deliver that future.

00:40:10.500 --> 00:40:11.787
All the things that we
are going to talk about,

00:40:11.787 --> 00:40:14.510
the 10-year plan, all the
things that we're on the cusp

00:40:14.510 --> 00:40:18.660
of now, it absolutely does
have the potential to do that

00:40:18.660 --> 00:40:21.660
supported by a leaner but stronger

00:40:21.660 --> 00:40:24.060
and more coherent centre.

00:40:24.060 --> 00:40:25.643
And I would like once again,

00:40:25.643 --> 00:40:28.920
just to thank all of you for your support

00:40:28.920 --> 00:40:32.670
but also your determination
to make the improvements

00:40:32.670 --> 00:40:35.280
that we know are necessary
for patients and for staff.

00:40:35.280 --> 00:40:38.820
Because it's not about
stopping now, it is about using

00:40:38.820 --> 00:40:42.090
where we are now as a real
launch pad for the future.

00:40:42.090 --> 00:40:45.000
And I would also like to thank
you, as I've said in advance

00:40:45.000 --> 00:40:47.457
for all the support I
know you will give Jim

00:40:47.457 --> 00:40:50.523
and Penny as they lead the NHS forward.

00:40:51.780 --> 00:40:52.613
- Thank you.

00:40:52.613 --> 00:40:53.490
And very well said.

00:40:54.420 --> 00:40:56.533
And that's quite a good segue in actually

00:40:56.533 --> 00:40:59.730
'cause I think Penny,
I'm going to hand to you

00:40:59.730 --> 00:41:02.670
and Jim I think just to share
some initial thoughts really

00:41:02.670 --> 00:41:04.650
as you come into the role.

00:41:04.650 --> 00:41:07.290
So from Penny and Jim together, I think,

00:41:07.290 --> 00:41:08.883
just to share those thoughts.

00:41:09.750 --> 00:41:12.033
- Okay, thank you Richard.

00:41:13.320 --> 00:41:15.090
Very pleased to be here.

00:41:15.090 --> 00:41:16.980
Just want to start by first of all echoing

00:41:16.980 --> 00:41:19.050
Amanda's thoughts and reflections.

00:41:19.050 --> 00:41:21.660
I think some very exciting
aspirations for the future,

00:41:21.660 --> 00:41:25.520
but also some real positive track record

00:41:25.520 --> 00:41:27.390
of performance over the last few years.

00:41:27.390 --> 00:41:30.000
And that's very much thanks
to you and to Richard

00:41:30.000 --> 00:41:32.350
and to wider colleagues
for delivering on that.

00:41:33.390 --> 00:41:36.360
I think three sort of things that Jim

00:41:36.360 --> 00:41:38.490
and I will be focusing on.

00:41:38.490 --> 00:41:43.290
First of all, changes to
the healthcare system,

00:41:43.290 --> 00:41:45.420
particularly through the 10-year
plan and we can come back

00:41:45.420 --> 00:41:47.490
and talk a bit more about that.

00:41:47.490 --> 00:41:51.030
Secondly, have already
been changes announced

00:41:51.030 --> 00:41:52.950
to Integrated Care Boards.

00:41:52.950 --> 00:41:55.440
So really working with
Integrated Care Boards

00:41:55.440 --> 00:41:59.700
and with regions as well as
with providers to think through

00:41:59.700 --> 00:42:03.000
how they collectively work
together to deliver the changes

00:42:03.000 --> 00:42:04.890
that we all want to see to the system.

00:42:04.890 --> 00:42:09.390
And then finally the integration
planning around NHS England

00:42:09.390 --> 00:42:11.910
and the Department of
Health and Social Care

00:42:11.910 --> 00:42:14.907
where we have now
established a Programme Board

00:42:14.907 --> 00:42:17.370
and we will be meeting for
the first time tomorrow

00:42:17.370 --> 00:42:20.010
and hopefully able to then out of that,

00:42:20.010 --> 00:42:22.680
describe what the process will look like

00:42:22.680 --> 00:42:25.380
and have some clear communication messages

00:42:25.380 --> 00:42:27.330
to support staff in particular,

00:42:27.330 --> 00:42:30.090
but also to reassure
members of the public about

00:42:30.090 --> 00:42:32.310
how we'll be making sure
that integration happens

00:42:32.310 --> 00:42:34.710
in a smooth and effective way.

00:42:34.710 --> 00:42:37.337
So those are the three things,
I'll stop at that point

00:42:37.337 --> 00:42:39.750
'cause I'm sure Jim wants
to say something as well.

00:42:39.750 --> 00:42:43.029
But again, thank you for all of your work

00:42:43.029 --> 00:42:44.880
and looking forward
though slightly daunted,

00:42:44.880 --> 00:42:47.043
to taking over the mantle.

00:42:48.360 --> 00:42:49.804
- Thanks Penny.

00:42:49.804 --> 00:42:51.060
And I'll just add my thanks as well

00:42:51.060 --> 00:42:53.370
to Amanda and colleagues for
all you've done over the years,

00:42:53.370 --> 00:42:55.020
but especially over this last few weeks,

00:42:55.020 --> 00:42:57.210
been an incredibly complicated period

00:42:57.210 --> 00:43:00.000
and you've handled it as a true pro

00:43:00.000 --> 00:43:01.770
as we would all expect.

00:43:01.770 --> 00:43:05.220
And you know, we spoke
several times a day at times

00:43:05.220 --> 00:43:07.920
just trying to stay on
top of the complexity

00:43:07.920 --> 00:43:09.810
and focused on what we have to do.

00:43:09.810 --> 00:43:11.460
We're here for patients and staff

00:43:12.751 --> 00:43:14.730
and that's been a key
theme throughout the day.

00:43:14.730 --> 00:43:18.360
A key theme of our work
in this next period,

00:43:18.360 --> 00:43:21.603
acknowledging we're in a tricky place,

00:43:23.130 --> 00:43:25.770
and March has been an
incredibly busy month

00:43:25.770 --> 00:43:27.810
with an awful lot of things happening.

00:43:27.810 --> 00:43:30.090
But I'll just touch on a couple of things

00:43:30.090 --> 00:43:31.770
to give people hopefully a bit of a sign

00:43:31.770 --> 00:43:34.863
of some glimmers of hope for the future.

00:43:36.060 --> 00:43:39.990
So in the first few sort
of changes in that week

00:43:39.990 --> 00:43:43.350
that led to the PM's
announcement on that day,

00:43:43.350 --> 00:43:46.470
we met with chairs and
chief execs across the NHS

00:43:46.470 --> 00:43:49.920
to discuss the planning
round and the concerns

00:43:49.920 --> 00:43:51.297
about the planning round and progress

00:43:51.297 --> 00:43:55.350
and the potential for having
a very difficult position

00:43:55.350 --> 00:43:56.820
going into this year.

00:43:56.820 --> 00:43:59.490
All of that was about
a leadership response

00:43:59.490 --> 00:44:02.250
and we absolutely have seen
that leadership response.

00:44:02.250 --> 00:44:04.200
So I think we should acknowledge

00:44:04.200 --> 00:44:06.180
and thank colleagues for all they've done

00:44:06.180 --> 00:44:07.863
in this last couple of weeks.

00:44:08.974 --> 00:44:09.807
Absolutely.

00:44:09.807 --> 00:44:10.640
Absolutely.

00:44:10.640 --> 00:44:11.473
So right across the NHS,

00:44:11.473 --> 00:44:15.180
people have pulled together
and we now have incomplete,

00:44:15.180 --> 00:44:19.020
but still much, much better
plans going in the next year.

00:44:19.020 --> 00:44:22.830
And over the next few weeks
we'll be working through that,

00:44:22.830 --> 00:44:23.980
the delivery risks

00:44:24.963 --> 00:44:26.760
and the things that we need to handle

00:44:26.760 --> 00:44:27.930
as we'll go through the year.

00:44:27.930 --> 00:44:30.090
But we're in a much stronger
position now as a result

00:44:30.090 --> 00:44:32.313
of that leadership response.

00:44:33.540 --> 00:44:34.740
As has already been said,

00:44:34.740 --> 00:44:38.640
we've got a huge change for NHS England,

00:44:38.640 --> 00:44:39.990
Department of Health and Social Care,

00:44:39.990 --> 00:44:41.070
Integrated Care Boards,

00:44:41.070 --> 00:44:44.910
but right throughout the NHS on the back

00:44:44.910 --> 00:44:46.680
of the announcements this month.

00:44:46.680 --> 00:44:48.780
And there's an awful lot
of people very anxious

00:44:48.780 --> 00:44:51.300
and uncertain about that change.

00:44:51.300 --> 00:44:54.450
So Penny and I met with
colleagues here today in Leeds.

00:44:54.450 --> 00:44:57.163
First thing this morning,
we've heard that,

00:44:57.163 --> 00:44:58.860
I just want to say publicly,

00:44:58.860 --> 00:45:00.783
we've heard and acknowledged.

00:45:01.955 --> 00:45:04.230
We do acknowledge the uncertainty,

00:45:04.230 --> 00:45:08.190
the concern from staff about
the change, the need for us

00:45:08.190 --> 00:45:09.420
to move with pace,

00:45:09.420 --> 00:45:14.420
but with fairness, with
clarity as quickly as we can

00:45:14.813 --> 00:45:18.150
and learn from previous
reorganisation as well

00:45:18.150 --> 00:45:19.680
where there's been a lot of that happening

00:45:19.680 --> 00:45:21.930
in recent years as been described.

00:45:21.930 --> 00:45:25.260
And make sure we get through
this period as quickly

00:45:25.260 --> 00:45:27.660
and as safely as we can
do, not dropping a ball

00:45:27.660 --> 00:45:29.250
'cause we've got to do
what we've got to do

00:45:29.250 --> 00:45:32.040
as well as get through the change process.

00:45:32.040 --> 00:45:33.420
And then hopefully,

00:45:33.420 --> 00:45:35.010
and a few of us have been involved

00:45:35.010 --> 00:45:36.960
in 10-year plan discussions
the last few weeks,

00:45:36.960 --> 00:45:39.548
Amanda and others before that.

00:45:39.548 --> 00:45:41.760
So we're a few weeks away
from it being published,

00:45:41.760 --> 00:45:45.090
but I think those of us
that have seen bits of it

00:45:45.090 --> 00:45:48.660
and had discussions about it,
it will be really energising.

00:45:48.660 --> 00:45:51.510
So when people are saying
to me, this is all,

00:45:51.510 --> 00:45:53.700
there's a lot of heavy lifting here.

00:45:53.700 --> 00:45:55.260
Where's the optimism?

00:45:55.260 --> 00:45:56.850
Where's the future?

00:45:56.850 --> 00:45:58.443
We've got that to look forward to.

00:45:58.443 --> 00:46:02.400
So I do think once we get
through this tricky phase,

00:46:02.400 --> 00:46:06.750
finalise plans, et cetera, we've
got more to look forward to

00:46:06.750 --> 00:46:11.070
and a much more community
oriented, prevention oriented,

00:46:11.070 --> 00:46:15.300
neighbourhood orientated,
liberated health system

00:46:15.300 --> 00:46:17.670
that we all want to work in.

00:46:17.670 --> 00:46:21.180
And there's been mentioned
several times today,

00:46:21.180 --> 00:46:22.980
let's just remember the
people involved in this,

00:46:22.980 --> 00:46:25.530
whether the patients that
we're looking after are,

00:46:25.530 --> 00:46:26.670
or our staff.

00:46:26.670 --> 00:46:29.550
And we will, Penny and
I committed that today.

00:46:29.550 --> 00:46:32.430
We'll continue to stay focused on that

00:46:32.430 --> 00:46:34.680
as you all have done
over the last few years.

00:46:36.109 --> 00:46:37.140
- Thank you.

00:46:37.140 --> 00:46:41.670
So let me just, I'm
going to say in advance

00:46:42.960 --> 00:46:44.490
what I'm going to say at
the end of the meeting,

00:46:44.490 --> 00:46:48.653
which is I give you, but the
whole Board, which is you Penny

00:46:48.653 --> 00:46:52.050
and you Jim, huge, huge
fortune, you know, good fortune,

00:46:52.050 --> 00:46:55.290
good luck, you need
luck, great commitment.

00:46:55.290 --> 00:46:57.150
You'll have the support,

00:46:57.150 --> 00:46:59.700
I know you'll have the support
from the Board and the execs,

00:46:59.700 --> 00:47:02.370
but all my best wishes

00:47:02.370 --> 00:47:05.190
and our best wishes as
you take on these roles.

00:47:05.190 --> 00:47:06.600
But I'm sorry, I'm going to
say that again at the end

00:47:06.600 --> 00:47:09.270
of the meeting as well actually
all words to that effect.

00:47:09.270 --> 00:47:12.483
But thank you very much
for those comments.

00:47:14.250 --> 00:47:16.410
Can I move us on,

00:47:16.410 --> 00:47:18.990
can we move into just an
update on performance?

00:47:18.990 --> 00:47:21.840
So both operational
performance to start with

00:47:21.840 --> 00:47:23.120
and then financial performance.

00:47:23.120 --> 00:47:25.377
So Emily, I think to you, I think.

00:47:25.377 --> 00:47:26.377
- Thank you.

00:47:27.463 --> 00:47:29.970
So this is my final report to the Board

00:47:29.970 --> 00:47:31.680
as chief operating officer

00:47:31.680 --> 00:47:36.000
and I will continue the theme
that I've been talking about

00:47:36.000 --> 00:47:39.120
for the last few Boards,
which is this is a report

00:47:39.120 --> 00:47:43.823
that shows both how impressive the NHS is

00:47:45.090 --> 00:47:47.280
at continually looking

00:47:47.280 --> 00:47:48.870
for improvements that matter

00:47:48.870 --> 00:47:50.640
to patients and delivering them.

00:47:50.640 --> 00:47:53.940
And that we still have
a hugely long way to go.

00:47:53.940 --> 00:47:56.250
And I will try and reflect
that in each section

00:47:56.250 --> 00:47:57.370
of this update

00:47:58.530 --> 00:48:01.560
because I can see that the
NHS is delivering every day,

00:48:01.560 --> 00:48:04.710
not perfectly, not
easily, but meaningfully

00:48:04.710 --> 00:48:07.263
and measurably in every
corner of the country.

00:48:08.610 --> 00:48:11.130
I'll look at the whole of of 24-25,

00:48:11.130 --> 00:48:13.260
just to put this in
context, if we think back

00:48:13.260 --> 00:48:14.610
to the first quarter,

00:48:14.610 --> 00:48:17.040
we were still managing industrial action.

00:48:17.040 --> 00:48:19.440
We had a late planning round

00:48:19.440 --> 00:48:21.690
and we had the ongoing
operational pressures

00:48:21.690 --> 00:48:23.730
that we have most days.

00:48:23.730 --> 00:48:26.010
And now as we close the
year, performance is better

00:48:26.010 --> 00:48:28.320
across the board in urgent care,

00:48:28.320 --> 00:48:30.120
in elective recovery, in cancer,

00:48:30.120 --> 00:48:32.940
in diagnostics, in
access to mental health.

00:48:32.940 --> 00:48:35.190
And that's not the end of the story,

00:48:35.190 --> 00:48:36.780
but it is a turning point,

00:48:36.780 --> 00:48:39.180
and primary care, sorry,
I should have said.

00:48:39.180 --> 00:48:40.980
So I want to give an overview of

00:48:40.980 --> 00:48:43.020
where we are and how we got there.

00:48:43.020 --> 00:48:45.690
And in doing so, I want
to pick out three themes

00:48:45.690 --> 00:48:47.670
I'll come back to briefly at the end

00:48:47.670 --> 00:48:49.860
around improvement around data

00:48:49.860 --> 00:48:51.560
and insight and around leadership.

00:48:52.740 --> 00:48:56.610
So if I start with elective care in 24-25,

00:48:56.610 --> 00:48:59.520
we've moved decisively
in the right direction.

00:48:59.520 --> 00:49:01.590
For the first time since 2019,

00:49:01.590 --> 00:49:05.340
the waiting list is shrinking,
it's now at 7.43 million,

00:49:05.340 --> 00:49:07.860
which is down over 35,000 since December

00:49:07.860 --> 00:49:11.790
and 147,000 lower than
this time last year.

00:49:11.790 --> 00:49:16.110
65 week waits have fallen
by 84% to just under 15,000.

00:49:16.110 --> 00:49:19.770
52 week waits have fallen
for eight consecutive months

00:49:19.770 --> 00:49:21.507
and we're forecasting that'll be at around

00:49:21.507 --> 00:49:24.600
195,000 by year end.

00:49:24.600 --> 00:49:26.790
We've delivered this
through surgical hubs,

00:49:26.790 --> 00:49:29.190
through seven day operating, mutual aid,

00:49:29.190 --> 00:49:30.960
specialty level recovery plans

00:49:30.960 --> 00:49:33.150
and thousands of clinical teams rethinking

00:49:33.150 --> 00:49:34.830
how they manage their lists.

00:49:34.830 --> 00:49:37.350
That's progress built on action.

00:49:37.350 --> 00:49:39.390
But the job is clearly not done.

00:49:39.390 --> 00:49:42.330
All of the numbers I've just
read out are still too high

00:49:42.330 --> 00:49:45.060
and returning to 18 week
performance will be harder,

00:49:45.060 --> 00:49:47.610
requires a real rethink
in many places about

00:49:47.610 --> 00:49:51.300
how local systems are managing
their elective waiting lists.

00:49:51.300 --> 00:49:55.560
And so in 25-26, we'll need
to go further into digital,

00:49:55.560 --> 00:49:57.900
into embedding productivity improvements

00:49:57.900 --> 00:50:00.660
and to do even more than we have already

00:50:00.660 --> 00:50:03.120
and into reproducible best practise

00:50:03.120 --> 00:50:05.793
and making the most of
what we already know works.

00:50:06.990 --> 00:50:11.990
In urgent and emergency care,
it always needs to be better,

00:50:12.360 --> 00:50:14.130
but performance is improving.

00:50:14.130 --> 00:50:17.550
So February's four hour
performance stood at 73.4%,

00:50:17.550 --> 00:50:20.700
which is up 2.4 percentage
points year on year.

00:50:20.700 --> 00:50:23.130
And category two ambulance response times

00:50:23.130 --> 00:50:25.380
averaged 31 minutes, 22 seconds,

00:50:25.380 --> 00:50:27.060
which is nearly five minutes faster

00:50:27.060 --> 00:50:28.590
than this time last year.

00:50:28.590 --> 00:50:31.260
Recognising that the flu peak
has come at a different time

00:50:31.260 --> 00:50:33.750
this year than it did the year before.

00:50:33.750 --> 00:50:36.780
And that's testament to
the work of thousands

00:50:36.780 --> 00:50:38.040
of people in local systems.

00:50:38.040 --> 00:50:39.210
And of course the work

00:50:39.210 --> 00:50:42.090
that Sarah-Jane's team have led nationally

00:50:42.090 --> 00:50:45.663
building on the two UEC improvement plans.

00:50:46.830 --> 00:50:49.890
Having said that, those
headline metrics have improved

00:50:49.890 --> 00:50:52.350
year on year and the
resilience of the system

00:50:52.350 --> 00:50:56.400
to cope against, you know,
particularly bad flu peaks,

00:50:56.400 --> 00:50:58.380
which we saw again this year.

00:50:58.380 --> 00:51:00.750
There are metrics where we are,

00:51:00.750 --> 00:51:02.610
we haven't seen the same
level of improvement.

00:51:02.610 --> 00:51:07.380
So 12 hour breaches fell 22%
between January and February,

00:51:07.380 --> 00:51:09.450
but is still too high.

00:51:09.450 --> 00:51:12.000
And this is a place where
I wanted to call out

00:51:12.000 --> 00:51:14.070
that variability in performance

00:51:14.070 --> 00:51:16.980
because if we look at the
last six months of data,

00:51:16.980 --> 00:51:21.390
17.6% of trusts have deteriorated
into 12-hour breaches,

00:51:21.390 --> 00:51:23.670
25.8% have improved

00:51:23.670 --> 00:51:26.640
and the rest have sustained
their 12-hour performance.

00:51:26.640 --> 00:51:28.920
So even just bearing in mind

00:51:28.920 --> 00:51:30.300
that when we look at the headline numbers,

00:51:30.300 --> 00:51:33.330
we often miss both the
improvement that's going on,

00:51:33.330 --> 00:51:35.280
but also some of the
tensions that are happening

00:51:35.280 --> 00:51:37.740
and needing to be managed locally.

00:51:37.740 --> 00:51:41.520
The gains in UEC performance
come from more core beds,

00:51:41.520 --> 00:51:43.620
at least in the previous year,

00:51:43.620 --> 00:51:46.710
but maintaining that capacity
this year, the expansion

00:51:46.710 --> 00:51:48.780
of same day emergency care, greater use

00:51:48.780 --> 00:51:51.210
of urgent treatment
centres and virtual wards

00:51:51.210 --> 00:51:54.150
and a much tighter focus
on flow and discharge

00:51:54.150 --> 00:51:57.630
and operational discipline
within UEC departments

00:51:57.630 --> 00:51:59.640
and into the rest of the hospital.

00:51:59.640 --> 00:52:02.040
And what we can see from
the productivity packs

00:52:02.040 --> 00:52:04.920
that were shared with systems
as part of the planning round

00:52:04.920 --> 00:52:07.830
is UEC still has some opportunity

00:52:07.830 --> 00:52:08.940
for productivity improvement,

00:52:08.940 --> 00:52:10.830
but much less thanks to all of the work

00:52:10.830 --> 00:52:13.430
that's already gone in to
improve performance there.

00:52:14.610 --> 00:52:16.800
We know what works, mostly,

00:52:16.800 --> 00:52:18.360
I'm sure there's more insight to come,

00:52:18.360 --> 00:52:21.120
but our task is now to
do what works everywhere

00:52:21.120 --> 00:52:23.610
and that requires capacity, capability,

00:52:23.610 --> 00:52:26.013
and brilliant communication.

00:52:27.960 --> 00:52:30.120
I'm going to not dwell on cancer

00:52:30.120 --> 00:52:32.460
because we've got a cancer
paper coming in a second

00:52:32.460 --> 00:52:34.230
and I'll leave the headlines for the team

00:52:34.230 --> 00:52:36.435
that are here to talk about that.

00:52:36.435 --> 00:52:38.610
I wanted to move on to
talk about diagnostics,

00:52:38.610 --> 00:52:42.540
which is a story of really
impressive scale and growth.

00:52:42.540 --> 00:52:46.320
In January alone, we
delivered 2.51 million tests,

00:52:46.320 --> 00:52:48.210
which is the highest ever monthly total.

00:52:48.210 --> 00:52:50.760
It's been increasing almost every month

00:52:50.760 --> 00:52:55.083
since I've been in the
job since November 23.

00:52:56.160 --> 00:52:59.460
Our six week breach rate was 22.4%

00:52:59.460 --> 00:53:02.370
and we forecast closing the year at 18%,

00:53:02.370 --> 00:53:06.060
which is a significant
improvement on last year's 25.3%.

00:53:06.060 --> 00:53:09.690
But clearly still a long way
to go to the 5% ambition,

00:53:09.690 --> 00:53:11.440
which was our target for this year.

00:53:13.020 --> 00:53:16.320
Really what's happened is
instead of the new capacity

00:53:16.320 --> 00:53:18.480
being able to bring down the waiting list,

00:53:18.480 --> 00:53:20.040
it's meant that we've kept pace

00:53:20.040 --> 00:53:23.190
with massively increased
demand in diagnostics.

00:53:23.190 --> 00:53:25.440
And those expanded CDCs have delivered

00:53:25.440 --> 00:53:26.970
much better patient convenience,

00:53:26.970 --> 00:53:29.070
longer opening hours, pooled reporting,

00:53:29.070 --> 00:53:31.140
and better demand management.

00:53:31.140 --> 00:53:34.920
But the unscheduled activity
demand has continued to rise

00:53:34.920 --> 00:53:37.530
and so we are going to need
to keep scaling capacity

00:53:37.530 --> 00:53:40.560
and digitising and modernising
the diagnostic infrastructure

00:53:40.560 --> 00:53:42.010
to keep pace with the demand.

00:53:43.260 --> 00:53:45.960
On mental health, learning
disability and autism services

00:53:45.960 --> 00:53:47.760
have massively expanded the reach,

00:53:47.760 --> 00:53:50.370
and again, pressures remain.

00:53:50.370 --> 00:53:52.920
For example, over two million
people were in contact

00:53:52.920 --> 00:53:55.260
with mental health
services as of December,

00:53:55.260 --> 00:53:58.170
which is up from 1.94 million in March.

00:53:58.170 --> 00:53:59.550
Talking therapy's exceeded

00:53:59.550 --> 00:54:02.490
the six-week access standard at 91%,

00:54:02.490 --> 00:54:07.490
although recovery is at
47.5% against a 48% target.

00:54:08.220 --> 00:54:11.160
CYP access rose 5.2% year-on-year,

00:54:11.160 --> 00:54:15.270
reaching over 817,000

00:54:15.270 --> 00:54:19.590
and dementia diagnosis
rate is holding at 65.4%.

00:54:19.590 --> 00:54:22.470
That's been achieved through
improving crisis lines,

00:54:22.470 --> 00:54:25.140
increasing perinatal
services, school-based teams

00:54:25.140 --> 00:54:30.140
and workforce growth, which
is now at over 155,000 FTEs.

00:54:30.300 --> 00:54:32.340
But we know that long waits in A&amp;E

00:54:32.340 --> 00:54:34.380
for patients in crisis remain too common,

00:54:34.380 --> 00:54:36.090
although there's a
brilliant improvement piece

00:54:36.090 --> 00:54:37.890
of work going on on that now.

00:54:37.890 --> 00:54:39.960
And out of area placements in particular

00:54:39.960 --> 00:54:42.210
have not reduced this
year at the pace we did

00:54:42.210 --> 00:54:44.070
last year and at the aspiration

00:54:44.070 --> 00:54:46.140
that we all had for this year.

00:54:46.140 --> 00:54:49.920
So 25-26 needs to be an
ongoing improvement year

00:54:49.920 --> 00:54:52.260
of consolidating crisis alternatives,

00:54:52.260 --> 00:54:54.060
reducing unnecessary admissions

00:54:54.060 --> 00:54:57.060
and driving consistent
quality across all services

00:54:57.060 --> 00:54:59.700
and geographies because
the 12-hour breaches

00:54:59.700 --> 00:55:02.910
that I review weekly are
not consistently placed

00:55:02.910 --> 00:55:03.860
across the country.

00:55:04.710 --> 00:55:07.710
In primary care, we also
have seen improvement

00:55:07.710 --> 00:55:09.870
despite increasing demand.

00:55:09.870 --> 00:55:13.740
And we can see that patients
now are feeling a sense

00:55:13.740 --> 00:55:15.180
of better access.

00:55:15.180 --> 00:55:17.220
So in January, general practise delivered

00:55:17.220 --> 00:55:21.030
33 million appointments, which
was 5% higher than last year,

00:55:21.030 --> 00:55:24.300
but perhaps even more
importantly, satisfaction

00:55:24.300 --> 00:55:26.370
by patients with ease of access has risen

00:55:26.370 --> 00:55:30.480
from 60.9% to 73.3%.

00:55:30.480 --> 00:55:32.703
Still a long way to go, but much improved.

00:55:33.990 --> 00:55:36.420
Pharmacy First has continued
to deliver this year

00:55:36.420 --> 00:55:38.550
with exceeding targets on contraception

00:55:38.550 --> 00:55:39.570
and blood pressure checks

00:55:39.570 --> 00:55:42.030
and clinical consultations arising.

00:55:42.030 --> 00:55:43.980
But as is widely reported

00:55:43.980 --> 00:55:47.730
and a source of much
discussion across NHS England

00:55:47.730 --> 00:55:50.400
and the Department of
Health, dental access

00:55:50.400 --> 00:55:53.070
remains a huge concern with 78%

00:55:53.070 --> 00:55:55.260
of activity delivered in December

00:55:55.260 --> 00:55:58.143
and continued difficulty
in new patient access.

00:55:59.280 --> 00:56:00.777
In the community, virtual ward capacity

00:56:00.777 --> 00:56:02.820
has now passed 10,000 beds

00:56:02.820 --> 00:56:05.610
and the urgent community
response is hitting 84%

00:56:05.610 --> 00:56:07.650
on its two-hour response targets.

00:56:07.650 --> 00:56:09.930
But the waiting list has
grown to over one million

00:56:09.930 --> 00:56:12.360
with long waits concentrated
in children's services,

00:56:12.360 --> 00:56:16.020
particularly worryingly
in community paediatrics.

00:56:16.020 --> 00:56:18.180
So the priority for
the next year is clear.

00:56:18.180 --> 00:56:21.450
We need to reduce variation
in access and experience.

00:56:21.450 --> 00:56:23.850
We need to expand integrated
neighbourhood models

00:56:23.850 --> 00:56:26.010
and continue the contractual
reform that's needed

00:56:26.010 --> 00:56:28.413
to unlock primary and community capacity.

00:56:29.400 --> 00:56:31.260
So where does that leave us?

00:56:31.260 --> 00:56:32.940
We've got elective waits coming down.

00:56:32.940 --> 00:56:34.650
We have urgent care improving.

00:56:34.650 --> 00:56:37.320
We have cancer progress
moving even faster.

00:56:37.320 --> 00:56:40.290
We have diagnostics delivering
at scale and mental health

00:56:40.290 --> 00:56:41.880
and primary care reaching more people

00:56:41.880 --> 00:56:43.680
more effectively than ever.

00:56:43.680 --> 00:56:46.320
We've got a system that's
recovering, not evenly,

00:56:46.320 --> 00:56:49.800
not completely, but steadily
in the right direction.

00:56:49.800 --> 00:56:51.900
And so 25-26 in my view,

00:56:51.900 --> 00:56:53.640
although it'll be leaving
it to other people

00:56:53.640 --> 00:56:56.100
to make it happen, is the year we need

00:56:56.100 --> 00:56:58.860
to go more precisely on actions

00:56:58.860 --> 00:57:00.120
in the way that I've just spoken

00:57:00.120 --> 00:57:03.240
and in those three categories
I spoke about earlier.

00:57:03.240 --> 00:57:06.930
First of all, in embedding
improvement capacity, capability

00:57:06.930 --> 00:57:10.500
and confidence locally moving
from knowledge of what works

00:57:10.500 --> 00:57:13.560
to actual consistent
delivery across the board.

00:57:13.560 --> 00:57:15.693
And we need to hardwire that
improvement into the way

00:57:15.693 --> 00:57:18.360
that we operate every day,
including in reporting

00:57:18.360 --> 00:57:20.220
and target setting.

00:57:20.220 --> 00:57:23.670
On data and insight, we know
a lot about performance.

00:57:23.670 --> 00:57:26.670
We track an immense amount of data.

00:57:26.670 --> 00:57:29.280
We are getting better at
getting insight from it.

00:57:29.280 --> 00:57:31.200
And this year, that included work

00:57:31.200 --> 00:57:32.730
across our analytical teams

00:57:32.730 --> 00:57:36.030
and with academics to identify
the actual root causes

00:57:36.030 --> 00:57:38.100
of underperformance as
opposed to what we tend

00:57:38.100 --> 00:57:40.440
to think it is based on experience.

00:57:40.440 --> 00:57:42.330
The work, there's work underway to build

00:57:42.330 --> 00:57:44.130
that understanding into a diagnostic

00:57:44.130 --> 00:57:47.310
that will allow more
effective, focused support

00:57:47.310 --> 00:57:48.630
to the most challenged systems

00:57:48.630 --> 00:57:49.830
and making sure we're addressing

00:57:49.830 --> 00:57:51.720
the real underlying root causes

00:57:51.720 --> 00:57:54.900
of underperformance, that needs
to link into the assurance

00:57:54.900 --> 00:57:56.997
and regulatory approaches that Steve

00:57:56.997 --> 00:57:59.043
and Adam will talk about in a minute.

00:58:00.120 --> 00:58:02.430
And then finally on leadership.

00:58:02.430 --> 00:58:04.350
A lot of this is about leadership.

00:58:04.350 --> 00:58:05.910
It's not all about leadership.

00:58:05.910 --> 00:58:10.910
It's a sometimes a bit
of a convenient phrase,

00:58:11.130 --> 00:58:14.730
but we know from talking
to CEOs across the NHS,

00:58:14.730 --> 00:58:17.130
that taking on the most
challenged systems,

00:58:17.130 --> 00:58:20.760
the most challenged organisations
is not always attractive

00:58:20.760 --> 00:58:22.113
or rarely attractive.

00:58:23.040 --> 00:58:26.280
And so I'm looking forward
to the work that's been done

00:58:26.280 --> 00:58:30.060
by teams across the system
to identify what does it take

00:58:30.060 --> 00:58:33.330
to get the right mix of leadership skills

00:58:33.330 --> 00:58:34.920
into those most challenged systems

00:58:34.920 --> 00:58:37.890
so that we can support staff
to make the improvements

00:58:37.890 --> 00:58:40.980
that they can see need to be
made and that they want to make

00:58:40.980 --> 00:58:43.290
and make sure those systems
are improving in the way

00:58:43.290 --> 00:58:45.440
that we would all desire
for service users.

00:58:46.470 --> 00:58:49.053
I want to close, you'll
be grateful too Richard,

00:58:50.730 --> 00:58:53.310
by thanking colleagues across NHS England,

00:58:53.310 --> 00:58:56.340
including the regions and
above all on the front line

00:58:56.340 --> 00:58:58.920
because the recovery we've
seen this year is thanks

00:58:58.920 --> 00:59:01.110
to everything that they've done.

00:59:01.110 --> 00:59:04.410
I'm handing over with
confidence and optimism.

00:59:04.410 --> 00:59:07.320
There's more to do, but
we know where we're going

00:59:07.320 --> 00:59:09.990
and I have confidence in
the people that have done

00:59:09.990 --> 00:59:11.820
all of that work that they can see

00:59:11.820 --> 00:59:12.990
where more needs to be done

00:59:12.990 --> 00:59:15.630
and they'll be busy delivering
it today as every day.

00:59:15.630 --> 00:59:16.463
Thank you.

00:59:16.463 --> 00:59:20.400
- Emily, thank you very
much for a fulsome report.

00:59:20.400 --> 00:59:22.770
Actually it's important
sometimes I think you

00:59:22.770 --> 00:59:24.893
and I have talked about it to share

00:59:24.893 --> 00:59:27.150
some of the detailed factual evidence

00:59:27.150 --> 00:59:28.350
behind some of the statements.

00:59:28.350 --> 00:59:30.030
And thank you for doing that.

00:59:30.030 --> 00:59:33.390
So clearly I'm going to
ask Sarah-Jane in a moment

00:59:33.390 --> 00:59:35.820
just to reflect on winter
the lessons from the last,

00:59:35.820 --> 00:59:37.110
the last couple of winters actually,

00:59:37.110 --> 00:59:38.880
and then looking forward.

00:59:38.880 --> 00:59:41.460
But before I do that, any comments

00:59:41.460 --> 00:59:42.560
or questions for Emily

00:59:44.457 --> 00:59:46.380
on the operational performance?

00:59:46.380 --> 00:59:47.220
Andrew?

00:59:47.220 --> 00:59:50.940
- So if you just stand back, you know,

00:59:50.940 --> 00:59:55.080
we're trying to aim for a kind
of step change in performance

00:59:55.080 --> 00:59:56.820
on four hours and 12 hours,

00:59:56.820 --> 00:59:59.290
12 hours I think is more important

01:00:00.180 --> 01:00:02.430
from a kind of risk
management point of view.

01:00:03.390 --> 01:00:07.950
And if we don't do something
very, very different,

01:00:07.950 --> 01:00:12.030
we'll never hit the
elective target downstream

01:00:12.030 --> 01:00:14.760
because you need that capacity freed up

01:00:14.760 --> 01:00:17.580
to do more elective work potentially.

01:00:17.580 --> 01:00:21.150
And I'm just, I mean we put
in a hell of a lot of resource

01:00:21.150 --> 01:00:23.610
to support organisations

01:00:23.610 --> 01:00:27.243
and just going forward,
you know, what is it

01:00:27.243 --> 01:00:29.550
that is going to give us that step change?

01:00:29.550 --> 01:00:31.530
Because I think at some
point we're going to have

01:00:31.530 --> 01:00:35.850
to talk about a 5%
betterment per annum, right?

01:00:35.850 --> 01:00:38.763
Which is where we were at
the beginning of, you know,

01:00:39.650 --> 01:00:40.750
of the century, right?

01:00:42.263 --> 01:00:44.220
When, you know, we were
faced with, you know,

01:00:44.220 --> 01:00:47.280
the 95% target for four hours.

01:00:47.280 --> 01:00:49.470
So it is just trying to
understand, you know,

01:00:49.470 --> 01:00:53.340
that kind of landscape and
what's required going forward.

01:00:53.340 --> 01:00:55.890
'Cause without that vision,
we're not going to get there.

01:00:55.890 --> 01:00:58.020
We're going to be still
talking about, you know,

01:00:58.020 --> 01:01:01.290
a one or 2% kind of incremental growth.

01:01:01.290 --> 01:01:04.310
And I think that's the challenge
we've got at NHS England

01:01:04.310 --> 01:01:07.620
is just trying to set
the scene for the future

01:01:07.620 --> 01:01:09.780
and be clear about what are those

01:01:09.780 --> 01:01:12.303
kind of big step moves
that we need to make.

01:01:14.310 --> 01:01:16.290
- Well I was wondering if
it was worth holding that

01:01:16.290 --> 01:01:17.580
until Sarah-Jane's had a chance

01:01:17.580 --> 01:01:20.003
to talk about lessons
learned and looking forward.

01:01:21.244 --> 01:01:23.128
- Sarah-Jane, do you
want to comment about--

01:01:23.128 --> 01:01:24.319
- Yeah, absolutely.
- Past winter

01:01:24.319 --> 01:01:26.139
and the winter before?

01:01:26.139 --> 01:01:27.553
- That's the big question I think in all

01:01:27.553 --> 01:01:30.510
of this sort obviously is that
Andrew's always spot on with,

01:01:30.510 --> 01:01:31.740
yeah, so in terms of the winter,

01:01:31.740 --> 01:01:34.080
I mean Emily covered I think really well,

01:01:34.080 --> 01:01:35.400
the sort of picture overall.

01:01:35.400 --> 01:01:38.490
I think the areas that
we have seen progress in

01:01:38.490 --> 01:01:43.490
is that consistency and just
ability to be able to step up

01:01:43.500 --> 01:01:46.950
and respond particularly to
acute needs in the community.

01:01:46.950 --> 01:01:50.460
So our ambulance response times

01:01:50.460 --> 01:01:53.730
and also being able to get
alternatives to conveyance

01:01:53.730 --> 01:01:55.920
and admission in the community,
which has really started

01:01:55.920 --> 01:01:57.870
to make a difference.

01:01:57.870 --> 01:01:59.880
I think the area that
we've really struggled

01:01:59.880 --> 01:02:01.230
with over winter, and again,

01:02:01.230 --> 01:02:03.570
it's been very visible in the data

01:02:03.570 --> 01:02:05.190
that as we've really driven

01:02:05.190 --> 01:02:08.700
to get our ambulances out back
on the road, that has meant

01:02:08.700 --> 01:02:11.250
that some of our emergency departments

01:02:11.250 --> 01:02:14.310
and then wider parts of the hospital

01:02:14.310 --> 01:02:17.820
have been overcrowded at times.

01:02:17.820 --> 01:02:20.820
And that has meant to patients
to receive care in corridors

01:02:20.820 --> 01:02:23.970
and other areas that are
absolutely suboptimal.

01:02:23.970 --> 01:02:25.740
And I agree with Andrew
that whilst we need

01:02:25.740 --> 01:02:29.040
to achieve the four hours going
forwards, that is the thing

01:02:29.040 --> 01:02:34.040
that we need to provide a
huge focus on every day.

01:02:34.590 --> 01:02:36.120
But obviously it becomes more acute

01:02:36.120 --> 01:02:37.800
and challenging in winter.

01:02:37.800 --> 01:02:42.800
We will be sharing a UEC
delivery plan in coming weeks,

01:02:42.840 --> 01:02:45.870
which will be our short-term
approach essentially

01:02:45.870 --> 01:02:47.668
while we wait for the 10-year plan

01:02:47.668 --> 01:02:50.160
'cause I think many of
the sustainable solutions

01:02:50.160 --> 01:02:53.700
that answer your
challenge sit in that plan

01:02:53.700 --> 01:02:55.950
and how we think very
differently about community

01:02:55.950 --> 01:02:58.110
and neighbourhood and our relationships

01:02:58.110 --> 01:03:00.360
with social care and so on.

01:03:00.360 --> 01:03:03.000
But we've got a probably a
whole year of delivery here

01:03:03.000 --> 01:03:04.950
where we're not going to have that

01:03:04.950 --> 01:03:07.620
and therefore we need
to get really focused.

01:03:07.620 --> 01:03:12.360
If you take the, Emily
described the organisations

01:03:12.360 --> 01:03:15.930
that have improved and certainly
the ones we've worked with,

01:03:15.930 --> 01:03:20.430
there isn't one thing you
can do, even if you load lots

01:03:20.430 --> 01:03:23.460
of new beds into a system,
if they're not part

01:03:23.460 --> 01:03:27.120
of a whole integrated flow,
they make a difference

01:03:27.120 --> 01:03:30.687
for like about seven, 10 days
and then everything silts up.

01:03:30.687 --> 01:03:32.640
And in a way they make the problem worse

01:03:32.640 --> 01:03:34.710
because there's even more patients

01:03:34.710 --> 01:03:37.110
for an infrastructure that's overwhelmed.

01:03:37.110 --> 01:03:41.910
So the key things there are
every part playing their role.

01:03:41.910 --> 01:03:46.910
So from primary care,
111, ambulance services

01:03:47.310 --> 01:03:48.750
not taking patients to hospital

01:03:48.750 --> 01:03:51.300
unless completely necessary,
community services

01:03:51.300 --> 01:03:53.100
being scaled appropriately.

01:03:53.100 --> 01:03:57.300
We often see absolutely
beautiful examples of what a UCR

01:03:57.300 --> 01:04:00.660
or a virtual ward or a
neighbourhood team can do,

01:04:00.660 --> 01:04:02.130
but they don't do it consistently

01:04:02.130 --> 01:04:05.490
for every patient every time,
all through the day and night.

01:04:05.490 --> 01:04:07.950
And we need to build that consistency.

01:04:07.950 --> 01:04:10.230
But we do also need to
be honest with ourselves

01:04:10.230 --> 01:04:13.200
about what is going on
in hospitals themselves

01:04:13.200 --> 01:04:17.100
and the organisations that
deliver the lowest 12 hours

01:04:17.100 --> 01:04:20.790
and the best four-hour
and 12-hour performance.

01:04:20.790 --> 01:04:22.350
The medical model of care

01:04:22.350 --> 01:04:25.260
inside the hospital has a huge focus

01:04:25.260 --> 01:04:28.050
and priority on urgent and emergency care.

01:04:28.050 --> 01:04:31.980
And patients are reviewed
consistently with continuity

01:04:31.980 --> 01:04:33.210
and decisions are taken

01:04:33.210 --> 01:04:37.230
that take those patients
forward in their care every day.

01:04:37.230 --> 01:04:39.930
In some of our most challenge
that we have gone to

01:04:39.930 --> 01:04:44.310
as part of our improvement,
it can sometimes be days

01:04:44.310 --> 01:04:48.210
before a patient is seen by the specialty

01:04:48.210 --> 01:04:49.800
that is the one that's going to be able

01:04:49.800 --> 01:04:51.690
to take their care forward and resolve.

01:04:51.690 --> 01:04:54.270
So this is really linked to productivity,

01:04:54.270 --> 01:04:57.420
it's linked to the relationship
between UEC and elective.

01:04:57.420 --> 01:05:00.390
But if there was one thing
that was going to unlock this

01:05:00.390 --> 01:05:02.820
for the winter coming, it will be that.

01:05:02.820 --> 01:05:05.193
But I do not want to lose the broader,

01:05:06.240 --> 01:05:07.440
the broader landscape here

01:05:07.440 --> 01:05:09.870
because this is definitely
about what happens in community

01:05:09.870 --> 01:05:11.490
and discharge as well.

01:05:11.490 --> 01:05:12.900
- It's back to Emily's point

01:05:12.900 --> 01:05:15.390
around the leadership challenge
in certain communities

01:05:15.390 --> 01:05:17.700
where you get leaders working together

01:05:17.700 --> 01:05:20.670
and you know, re-gluing pathways,

01:05:20.670 --> 01:05:23.580
you get a lift in performance potentially

01:05:23.580 --> 01:05:26.313
where it's a bit more kind of fractious,

01:05:27.480 --> 01:05:29.285
you just don't get it.

01:05:29.285 --> 01:05:31.320
- But we are looking for that drumbeat.

01:05:31.320 --> 01:05:32.397
We've got a saying in ECIST,

01:05:32.397 --> 01:05:35.100
the improvement team,
that consistency beats

01:05:35.100 --> 01:05:36.630
brilliance every time.

01:05:36.630 --> 01:05:38.100
And there's so many people doing

01:05:38.100 --> 01:05:41.010
these really brilliant
things inconsistently,

01:05:41.010 --> 01:05:43.590
we just need every day

01:05:43.590 --> 01:05:46.470
to do the steps that
everybody takes in their care.

01:05:46.470 --> 01:05:48.570
And that's why our handover delays

01:05:48.570 --> 01:05:50.580
and there are more patients
corridors at the beginning

01:05:50.580 --> 01:05:52.480
of the week than there are at the end.

01:05:53.700 --> 01:05:55.470
That's because we don't
have the consistency,

01:05:55.470 --> 01:05:58.050
we don't have the clinical
and operational standards

01:05:58.050 --> 01:05:59.340
and execution that we need.

01:05:59.340 --> 01:06:01.690
So that will be a big focus of

01:06:03.514 --> 01:06:04.830
the delivery plan moving forwards

01:06:04.830 --> 01:06:07.620
and then what we'll do to support trusts

01:06:07.620 --> 01:06:10.500
and systems to get in
the best place they can.

01:06:10.500 --> 01:06:13.387
- Julian, you want to make a comment?

01:06:13.387 --> 01:06:15.376
- I've got a reflection and a question.

01:06:15.376 --> 01:06:18.513
I mean I think the debate
about over the medium term,

01:06:19.580 --> 01:06:21.210
how you achieve those step changes.

01:06:21.210 --> 01:06:23.070
I think it still has to play out.

01:06:23.070 --> 01:06:24.960
I think there is a question

01:06:24.960 --> 01:06:27.510
where you might need some more capacity

01:06:27.510 --> 01:06:29.430
even with like a significant left shift

01:06:29.430 --> 01:06:30.300
and a change in model

01:06:30.300 --> 01:06:32.940
because nowhere in the country

01:06:32.940 --> 01:06:35.820
is delivering against the
constitutional standard

01:06:35.820 --> 01:06:37.590
and with an older population,
I just can't believe

01:06:37.590 --> 01:06:40.890
that some extra capacity
is not going to be needed

01:06:40.890 --> 01:06:44.070
to provide some level of
kind of decompression.

01:06:44.070 --> 01:06:46.380
But that's the debate
absolutely that needs

01:06:46.380 --> 01:06:48.870
to be had I think as part
of the spending round.

01:06:48.870 --> 01:06:52.380
But I am really struck by
the fact that if you look

01:06:52.380 --> 01:06:56.850
over the last five years
when you look at demand

01:06:56.850 --> 01:06:58.527
sort of emergency demand

01:06:58.527 --> 01:07:00.870
and you look at admissions,
we are basically

01:07:00.870 --> 01:07:02.940
sort of flatlined against

01:07:02.940 --> 01:07:06.450
even with the sort of increase we've seen

01:07:06.450 --> 01:07:10.137
and you know, against the level in 1920.

01:07:10.137 --> 01:07:13.140
Now if someone had said
that's what would happen

01:07:13.140 --> 01:07:15.690
back in 2019-20 would you've thought,

01:07:15.690 --> 01:07:16.590
well that's extraordinary.

01:07:16.590 --> 01:07:19.020
Now I know we had a, you know,
big change through COVID,

01:07:19.020 --> 01:07:21.180
big shock to the system,

01:07:21.180 --> 01:07:22.556
but over the last six months,

01:07:22.556 --> 01:07:24.840
we've sort of seen a flattening in demand.

01:07:24.840 --> 01:07:28.890
And my question is do we
understand why that is

01:07:28.890 --> 01:07:33.810
and is it actually the work
that's already been done

01:07:33.810 --> 01:07:38.040
to achieve some of that left
shift in some of our systems

01:07:38.040 --> 01:07:40.740
or indeed the work we've led nationally

01:07:40.740 --> 01:07:42.810
through things like urgent crisis response

01:07:42.810 --> 01:07:45.870
and the virtual wards, even
if they can still work better

01:07:45.870 --> 01:07:48.810
or is something else going on?

01:07:48.810 --> 01:07:52.380
Because similarly, if you
look at elective referrals,

01:07:52.380 --> 01:07:54.930
I think one of the
striking things this year

01:07:54.930 --> 01:07:57.356
is clock starts have not seen
the growth you would typically

01:07:57.356 --> 01:08:01.740
expect to see a relatively flat.

01:08:01.740 --> 01:08:06.180
And again is that evidence of, you know,

01:08:06.180 --> 01:08:08.820
the stuff we have worked
on over the last few years

01:08:08.820 --> 01:08:10.590
around pre-referral advice and guidance

01:08:10.590 --> 01:08:13.320
and so on, there is something else

01:08:13.320 --> 01:08:14.823
going on and what's our view.

01:08:15.870 --> 01:08:18.483
- I mean can I just reinforce
that as a question to,

01:08:20.580 --> 01:08:23.400
Sarah-Jane and others, you
and or clearly to Emily

01:08:23.400 --> 01:08:26.640
and also if you can pick
up Andrew's comment more,

01:08:26.640 --> 01:08:30.400
which is that question, is
it, 'cause when I go into

01:08:31.260 --> 01:08:35.700
acute hospitals and I go through ED

01:08:35.700 --> 01:08:38.910
and it is the profile of the
patients predominantly elderly

01:08:38.910 --> 01:08:42.090
and frail actually are waiting

01:08:42.090 --> 01:08:43.200
and they're often the ones

01:08:43.200 --> 01:08:46.290
who are either in the
cubicles waiting in admission

01:08:46.290 --> 01:08:48.420
or on trolleys.

01:08:48.420 --> 01:08:51.990
And yet at the same time,
Julian's asking about this

01:08:51.990 --> 01:08:54.480
flattening demand and have we got data

01:08:54.480 --> 01:08:57.210
or clues that would say
it is the introduction

01:08:57.210 --> 01:08:58.859
of urgent community response.

01:08:58.859 --> 01:09:01.080
So it's the cat 2B, elderly
falls categorization

01:09:01.080 --> 01:09:02.733
with ambulance call outs.

01:09:04.470 --> 01:09:05.997
The virtual wards which
have been around now

01:09:05.997 --> 01:09:07.320
for a couple of years,

01:09:07.320 --> 01:09:09.060
the utilisation of the
virtual wards, are they

01:09:09.060 --> 01:09:13.380
by the elderly and the frail
more in step up and step down?

01:09:13.380 --> 01:09:17.763
And are these the things
beginning to make the difference?

01:09:19.290 --> 01:09:20.603
What do you think?

01:09:20.603 --> 01:09:23.040
- I don't have a well
researched academic answer

01:09:23.040 --> 01:09:26.550
for the whole country, but I
do know that wherever we go

01:09:26.550 --> 01:09:29.910
'cause we try to take the DNA
of the high performing system

01:09:29.910 --> 01:09:33.720
as well and that is
absolutely the case Richard.

01:09:33.720 --> 01:09:36.600
They are focused on frail older people,

01:09:36.600 --> 01:09:38.490
they've got some sort of approach

01:09:38.490 --> 01:09:41.200
to segmenting the population

01:09:42.630 --> 01:09:45.180
and it's that every time approach

01:09:45.180 --> 01:09:46.380
to that group of patients.

01:09:46.380 --> 01:09:48.510
And there are some places,
I know you visited one

01:09:48.510 --> 01:09:50.700
that have arrested the
increase in admissions

01:09:50.700 --> 01:09:55.260
for over 65s by way of example
I was at Washwood Heath

01:09:55.260 --> 01:09:56.640
last Thursday in Birmingham

01:09:56.640 --> 01:09:59.192
where in East Birmingham they've got

01:09:59.192 --> 01:10:01.110
a population cohort that they're managing.

01:10:01.110 --> 01:10:03.960
And again they've seen a big
reduction in admissions there.

01:10:03.960 --> 01:10:07.860
So I definitely think this
isn't just, you know, something

01:10:07.860 --> 01:10:10.200
that we say and we don't
really know whether it impacts,

01:10:10.200 --> 01:10:11.820
I go back to the corridors.

01:10:11.820 --> 01:10:15.090
A third of the patients in
the corridors that are waiting

01:10:15.090 --> 01:10:17.790
for care are frail older people

01:10:17.790 --> 01:10:20.703
that should have had a better
response in the community

01:10:21.723 --> 01:10:23.340
and they're over 80 years old

01:10:23.340 --> 01:10:27.330
and many of those patients
go on to be delayed discharge

01:10:27.330 --> 01:10:29.640
and need to leave hospital
with a higher level

01:10:29.640 --> 01:10:31.410
of need than when they arrived.

01:10:31.410 --> 01:10:33.480
And if we can get it right for that group,

01:10:33.480 --> 01:10:35.460
we are not just helping capacity and flow

01:10:35.460 --> 01:10:36.570
and everything else,

01:10:36.570 --> 01:10:40.260
but we're also helping
massively with social care

01:10:40.260 --> 01:10:42.600
and continuing healthcare spend as well.

01:10:42.600 --> 01:10:44.580
And some of our best examples,

01:10:44.580 --> 01:10:47.670
there's not just been huge
productivity gains for the NHS

01:10:47.670 --> 01:10:50.940
but there's been huge savings
for social care as well.

01:10:50.940 --> 01:10:53.010
So there is a way to get this right,

01:10:53.010 --> 01:10:54.387
but you do have to make the shift

01:10:54.387 --> 01:10:57.420
and the investment in the
frailty pathway upfront

01:10:57.420 --> 01:10:59.130
for this to work.

01:10:59.130 --> 01:11:02.160
It's not that that patients
don't need anything,

01:11:02.160 --> 01:11:04.920
they're just better served in
their care home or at home.

01:11:04.920 --> 01:11:07.080
So we should have confidence

01:11:07.080 --> 01:11:09.150
that we can do this from
the examples that we've got,

01:11:09.150 --> 01:11:12.393
but it's a big cultural shift
as well as everything else.

01:11:13.598 --> 01:11:16.262
- Emily, your response,
actually just a reaction

01:11:16.262 --> 01:11:19.095
to the conversation we are having.

01:11:21.030 --> 01:11:22.440
- I would wanted to pick up something

01:11:22.440 --> 01:11:25.960
that Sarah-Jane said which is

01:11:27.330 --> 01:11:28.710
when we look at systems

01:11:28.710 --> 01:11:30.990
that are consistently higher performing

01:11:30.990 --> 01:11:32.790
and the ones that have
made the biggest difference

01:11:32.790 --> 01:11:36.330
where often Sarah-Jane's
got teams in there doing

01:11:36.330 --> 01:11:40.950
some of the work, it isn't just
one thing, it is everything

01:11:40.950 --> 01:11:44.490
because this is a system
collectively across the country

01:11:44.490 --> 01:11:46.380
which is at its limits.

01:11:46.380 --> 01:11:48.570
So there isn't a magic bullet

01:11:48.570 --> 01:11:50.640
because if there was, we'd
have done it already, right?

01:11:50.640 --> 01:11:52.290
This is about flow through the hospital,

01:11:52.290 --> 01:11:55.290
it's about the relationship
with social care,

01:11:55.290 --> 01:11:57.960
it's about the right frailty
services in the community,

01:11:57.960 --> 01:12:01.410
it's about primary care and
making sure people have access.

01:12:01.410 --> 01:12:03.750
It's about the right level
of hear and treat and see

01:12:03.750 --> 01:12:06.540
and treat and the ambulance
trust, I won't keep on

01:12:06.540 --> 01:12:09.690
listing it 'cause mostly a
test except from Sarah-Jane

01:12:09.690 --> 01:12:11.913
to see if I've internalised it all.

01:12:13.890 --> 01:12:15.870
So that is where it comes back

01:12:15.870 --> 01:12:17.970
to Andrew's point because some of that

01:12:17.970 --> 01:12:19.220
can be achieved within
a single organisation,

01:12:19.220 --> 01:12:21.150
a lot of it can't.

01:12:21.150 --> 01:12:24.180
So it is about the right
leadership inside the organisation.

01:12:24.180 --> 01:12:25.590
If I think about one of the systems

01:12:25.590 --> 01:12:26.910
that was perhaps the most troubling

01:12:26.910 --> 01:12:29.673
when I arrived in November 23,

01:12:30.810 --> 01:12:33.600
what made the biggest difference
there was actually getting

01:12:33.600 --> 01:12:35.550
the clinical leadership of the trust

01:12:35.550 --> 01:12:36.500
and the managerial leadership

01:12:36.500 --> 01:12:39.480
of the trust working together
in a completely different way.

01:12:39.480 --> 01:12:40.410
That was the first step.

01:12:40.410 --> 01:12:42.870
But the second step was
getting the rest of the system

01:12:42.870 --> 01:12:44.730
to pull its weight to make sure

01:12:44.730 --> 01:12:47.280
that particular part
wasn't being overloaded.

01:12:47.280 --> 01:12:48.900
And within that, there are all of those

01:12:48.900 --> 01:12:50.430
sort of specific operational improvements

01:12:50.430 --> 01:12:51.380
we've talked about.

01:12:52.567 --> 01:12:55.680
So that goes back to Julian's
point, which is to say

01:12:55.680 --> 01:12:59.310
I can't see a sunlit upland
where you can do all of this

01:12:59.310 --> 01:13:02.550
and not put extra capacity
into this part of the system.

01:13:02.550 --> 01:13:05.190
But I think it remains
to be seen, you know,

01:13:05.190 --> 01:13:08.010
the way in which the 10-year
plan, not just what it says

01:13:08.010 --> 01:13:10.410
but how it's planned to
work through the system.

01:13:10.410 --> 01:13:13.000
Because we can definitely do
more to keep people from having

01:13:13.000 --> 01:13:16.680
to go to A&amp;E and yet
lots of people are going

01:13:16.680 --> 01:13:17.760
to end up at A&amp;E

01:13:17.760 --> 01:13:18.930
and we have to make sure we've got

01:13:18.930 --> 01:13:20.313
the systems to manage both.

01:13:21.240 --> 01:13:22.073
- Thank you.

01:13:22.073 --> 01:13:24.750
So, okay, so I'm going to go

01:13:24.750 --> 01:13:27.180
Mary then Mark and then Steve

01:13:27.180 --> 01:13:28.800
and then I'll move us on to finance.

01:13:28.800 --> 01:13:30.450
I think that's good, so Mary?

01:13:30.450 --> 01:13:31.500
- Thank you very much.

01:13:31.500 --> 01:13:34.800
I mean I completely agree
that a lot of people

01:13:34.800 --> 01:13:36.090
are being admitted to hospital

01:13:36.090 --> 01:13:37.830
who'd be much better to be at home

01:13:37.830 --> 01:13:41.280
and their homes are their
care homes quite often.

01:13:41.280 --> 01:13:45.630
So I wonder whether in
the next few months,

01:13:45.630 --> 01:13:49.620
we might do something
about really focusing

01:13:49.620 --> 01:13:54.520
on a handful of people who've
managed to stay at home

01:13:57.060 --> 01:14:00.420
and learn what it is in more detail

01:14:00.420 --> 01:14:04.950
to share best practise to show others

01:14:04.950 --> 01:14:06.390
what can be achieved.

01:14:06.390 --> 01:14:09.450
And I think we talked briefly
this morning about there are

01:14:09.450 --> 01:14:12.780
quite a lot of care homes that
are very nervous for example,

01:14:12.780 --> 01:14:16.950
of having an infusion for a period of time

01:14:16.950 --> 01:14:20.970
maybe to give intravenous antibiotics.

01:14:20.970 --> 01:14:22.830
And yet a lot of people

01:14:22.830 --> 01:14:24.780
who live in their own
home are quite comfortable

01:14:24.780 --> 01:14:27.360
with that if they've got
a district nurse coming.

01:14:27.360 --> 01:14:30.630
So there's something about
showing what we can achieve

01:14:30.630 --> 01:14:34.437
to the public so that their
first response isn't actually,

01:14:34.437 --> 01:14:36.810
and quite often this is people's response,

01:14:36.810 --> 01:14:39.360
I want somebody to go to hospital

01:14:39.360 --> 01:14:40.890
'cause then I don't have to worry

01:14:40.890 --> 01:14:44.040
and we should somehow make it
that they don't have to worry

01:14:44.040 --> 01:14:46.440
because we're giving
enough support in the home.

01:14:46.440 --> 01:14:47.828
- Thank you.

01:14:47.828 --> 01:14:48.978
Mark?

01:14:48.978 --> 01:14:52.350
- It's sort of talking
about this flattening

01:14:52.350 --> 01:14:53.730
of the line and the flattening of demand.

01:14:53.730 --> 01:14:58.560
The one bit that is not flattening is

01:14:58.560 --> 01:15:01.080
patients in contact with
mental health services.

01:15:01.080 --> 01:15:03.840
Which seems to be on this--

01:15:03.840 --> 01:15:06.660
- Fast rising.
- Not quite exponential

01:15:06.660 --> 01:15:08.070
but not ending growth.

01:15:08.070 --> 01:15:09.360
And I suppose there are two questions.

01:15:09.360 --> 01:15:14.130
One, do we understand what
the underlying driver is

01:15:14.130 --> 01:15:16.290
and do we have any hope
this will tail off?

01:15:16.290 --> 01:15:19.080
Or do we think we are
facing into sustained

01:15:19.080 --> 01:15:21.000
and systemic long-term demand?

01:15:21.000 --> 01:15:24.600
And two, given shortage
of long-term investment

01:15:24.600 --> 01:15:26.130
in this area, what effect is

01:15:26.130 --> 01:15:30.963
that happening on UEC
and other work pathways?

01:15:32.425 --> 01:15:33.480
- So if we can hold that question, Steve,

01:15:33.480 --> 01:15:34.906
do you want to come in?

01:15:34.906 --> 01:15:35.739
- Yeah, just very briefly Richard,

01:15:35.739 --> 01:15:38.700
it's like a minor fly the flag moment.

01:15:38.700 --> 01:15:41.790
So Amanda mentioned the
RSV vaccination programme

01:15:41.790 --> 01:15:44.253
that was introduced this winter.

01:15:45.120 --> 01:15:46.230
Colleagues may or may not have seen it

01:15:46.230 --> 01:15:49.980
but I thought it was worth
a couple of seconds just to,

01:15:49.980 --> 01:15:52.320
to sort of underscore the point
that Sarah-Jane often makes,

01:15:52.320 --> 01:15:55.230
which it's about getting the basics right,

01:15:55.230 --> 01:15:57.870
that applies both in
hospital and out of hospital.

01:15:57.870 --> 01:16:00.120
And one of the basics is vaccination.

01:16:00.120 --> 01:16:02.310
So we've managed to vaccinate

01:16:02.310 --> 01:16:06.410
since the start of the programme
about 1.8 million people

01:16:06.410 --> 01:16:09.360
of which about one and a half million are

01:16:09.360 --> 01:16:12.120
the older adult cohort.

01:16:12.120 --> 01:16:15.713
And UKHSA published a early evaluation

01:16:15.713 --> 01:16:18.120
early this week, which I thought

01:16:18.120 --> 01:16:20.250
might just be of interest to colleagues.

01:16:20.250 --> 01:16:23.615
And what that showed is that in the 75

01:16:23.615 --> 01:16:28.615
to 79-year-old group,
hospitalisation was 30% lower

01:16:28.950 --> 01:16:31.320
than in the other age categories.

01:16:31.320 --> 01:16:33.810
Now that's not going to
flatten the entire demand curve

01:16:33.810 --> 01:16:35.744
because the population of 75

01:16:35.744 --> 01:16:39.030
to 79-year-old is not the
entire population of England.

01:16:39.030 --> 01:16:41.490
But it is a group of people who make up

01:16:41.490 --> 01:16:44.280
a significant proportion of the population

01:16:44.280 --> 01:16:48.630
and often to Sarah-Jane point
have other comorbidities.

01:16:48.630 --> 01:16:51.510
So the reason that I guess
that highlighting that is,

01:16:51.510 --> 01:16:56.070
although that is good news,
there will be more people

01:16:56.070 --> 01:16:57.850
who turn 75 this year

01:16:58.950 --> 01:17:01.590
and we need to make sure
that all of those people

01:17:01.590 --> 01:17:03.990
that turn 75 this year are offered

01:17:03.990 --> 01:17:06.540
the most convenient access to vaccination.

01:17:06.540 --> 01:17:08.460
It's something that everybody thinks about

01:17:08.460 --> 01:17:11.130
'cause if we get all of those new entrants

01:17:11.130 --> 01:17:13.050
to the cohort vaccinated

01:17:13.050 --> 01:17:17.700
before winter, that will
mean their chance is 30% less

01:17:17.700 --> 01:17:19.410
of being admitted to hospital.

01:17:19.410 --> 01:17:22.350
And similarly, we've still
got about half a million,

01:17:22.350 --> 01:17:24.330
six, 700,000 people

01:17:24.330 --> 01:17:26.460
who were eligible at the
start of the programme

01:17:26.460 --> 01:17:28.510
who didn't have a vaccination

01:17:30.900 --> 01:17:32.190
by sort of the end of January.

01:17:32.190 --> 01:17:34.110
So that was maybe a little bit longer

01:17:34.110 --> 01:17:36.210
than you thought I was going to say.

01:17:36.210 --> 01:17:37.350
It's worth highlighting.

01:17:37.350 --> 01:17:38.400
- Worth highlighting.

01:17:39.928 --> 01:17:41.839
Actually there's one,

01:17:41.839 --> 01:17:42.672
it's the question on mental health

01:17:42.672 --> 01:17:43.950
I know there's questions for Mark,

01:17:43.950 --> 01:17:46.822
but before that, Jane you
want to come in I think?

01:17:46.822 --> 01:17:48.069
- Just super quick actually.

01:17:48.069 --> 01:17:49.555
Really just to reflect,

01:17:49.555 --> 01:17:51.930
I mean there's an opportunity
coming up with the publication

01:17:51.930 --> 01:17:54.900
of the 10-year plan to
also have that conversation

01:17:54.900 --> 01:17:57.623
with the public about how
they interact with the NHS

01:17:57.623 --> 01:18:00.330
and the things they can
do around their own health

01:18:00.330 --> 01:18:01.830
and all of that sort of thing.

01:18:01.830 --> 01:18:06.090
And I think that's such a great
example of where, you know,

01:18:06.090 --> 01:18:08.107
something is being offered
to people that I know from,

01:18:08.107 --> 01:18:11.310
you know, my background WHO,
millions and millions of people

01:18:11.310 --> 01:18:13.500
around the world don't have access to

01:18:13.500 --> 01:18:16.596
and you know, and die as a result.

01:18:16.596 --> 01:18:17.580
- Yeah.
- So I think it's just,

01:18:17.580 --> 01:18:19.140
I hope we take that opportunity.

01:18:19.140 --> 01:18:21.120
I'm sure it will be taken
when the 10-year plan

01:18:21.120 --> 01:18:22.380
is published to also have that

01:18:22.380 --> 01:18:25.080
conversation with the
public that these are things

01:18:25.080 --> 01:18:26.520
that as I say, the vast majority

01:18:26.520 --> 01:18:28.650
of the world's population
don't have access to

01:18:28.650 --> 01:18:31.980
and to encourage people to do
everything possible to help us

01:18:31.980 --> 01:18:35.190
to support them in the
way that we want to.

01:18:35.190 --> 01:18:36.023
- Well said.

01:18:36.023 --> 01:18:38.517
So Emily or Simon, do
you want to have a go

01:18:38.517 --> 01:18:39.437
at Mark's question?

01:18:39.437 --> 01:18:41.010
- I come from an operational perspective

01:18:41.010 --> 01:18:44.460
and Simon may want to, if we
do it the other way around?

01:18:44.460 --> 01:18:46.104
- What's driving this demand?

01:18:46.104 --> 01:18:46.937
- Well I think,

01:18:49.027 --> 01:18:51.600
actually why don't you talk about demand

01:18:51.600 --> 01:18:53.633
and I'll come back in on
what we're doing about it.

01:18:54.930 --> 01:18:56.430
- Charming, thank you.

01:18:56.430 --> 01:18:58.350
Well I mean there are two things.

01:18:58.350 --> 01:19:00.360
One you can see that
more people are accessing

01:19:00.360 --> 01:19:02.070
that's been going on for quite a long time

01:19:02.070 --> 01:19:04.320
and it's still steadily increasing

01:19:04.320 --> 01:19:06.450
but it's not increasing enough to cope

01:19:06.450 --> 01:19:08.700
with increasing demand.

01:19:08.700 --> 01:19:10.620
And I don't think it ever will be

01:19:10.620 --> 01:19:11.730
because I think it's made up

01:19:11.730 --> 01:19:14.970
of several different aspects
as we've talked before.

01:19:14.970 --> 01:19:18.810
One is genuinely better
recognition of some disorders.

01:19:18.810 --> 01:19:22.290
Other is changing diagnoses,
which you do from time-to-time.

01:19:22.290 --> 01:19:23.580
Very irritating for people.

01:19:23.580 --> 01:19:24.900
But we do.

01:19:24.900 --> 01:19:28.863
But the other I suspect is
admission creep as well.

01:19:29.820 --> 01:19:33.030
That's going on in
relabeling of other issues

01:19:33.030 --> 01:19:35.130
and disorders as well, which is something

01:19:35.130 --> 01:19:37.080
that we won't ever catch up with

01:19:37.080 --> 01:19:39.690
and you might possibly
argue perhaps we shouldn't

01:19:39.690 --> 01:19:41.850
because it's not necessarily
going to be solved

01:19:41.850 --> 01:19:45.900
by antidepressants or
start talking treatments.

01:19:45.900 --> 01:19:50.010
But quite how we unravel
that is a major problem.

01:19:50.010 --> 01:19:51.180
- Yeah.

01:19:51.180 --> 01:19:54.270
So we talked earlier in
the year about the strategy

01:19:54.270 --> 01:19:55.860
around that which needs further work

01:19:55.860 --> 01:19:58.440
and I'm sure that will
be in the 10-year plan

01:19:58.440 --> 01:20:03.000
at least partly, I think
what the NHS in terms of

01:20:03.000 --> 01:20:05.670
what we're delivering
every day we are seeing

01:20:05.670 --> 01:20:07.403
increasing delivery and
I mentioned, you know,

01:20:07.403 --> 01:20:09.210
some of the targets earlier

01:20:09.210 --> 01:20:13.770
and I'm just looking in the
longer paper at the children,

01:20:13.770 --> 01:20:16.500
young people receiving
more than one contact

01:20:16.500 --> 01:20:18.489
and then the children young people's

01:20:18.489 --> 01:20:20.640
eating disorder pathways where in December

01:20:20.640 --> 01:20:24.630
we saw 81.9% within four weeks.

01:20:24.630 --> 01:20:27.240
That's a big improvement
on the previous year

01:20:27.240 --> 01:20:29.580
but it's still nearly
20% of children in need

01:20:29.580 --> 01:20:30.630
of eating tool services

01:20:30.630 --> 01:20:32.220
who are not being seen within four weeks.

01:20:32.220 --> 01:20:33.300
Which I can only imagine

01:20:33.300 --> 01:20:36.090
what it's like if you are
that child or the parent.

01:20:36.090 --> 01:20:38.730
So this is a case of we do clearly

01:20:38.730 --> 01:20:41.220
have to do more on capacity

01:20:41.220 --> 01:20:43.830
but that is a combination of
more capacity in the place

01:20:43.830 --> 01:20:45.450
where it's actually needed combined

01:20:45.450 --> 01:20:47.940
with the clinical judgement
that Simon's mentioning.

01:20:47.940 --> 01:20:50.400
And also making better use of
the capacity that we've got.

01:20:50.400 --> 01:20:53.130
So we know for example,

01:20:53.130 --> 01:20:58.130
that our length of stay
in mental health beds

01:20:58.140 --> 01:20:59.227
is much longer than it used to be.

01:20:59.227 --> 01:21:02.580
It's also much longer than other countries

01:21:02.580 --> 01:21:04.620
with similar populations.

01:21:04.620 --> 01:21:06.570
So there is an opportunity
to make the most

01:21:06.570 --> 01:21:08.520
of our capacity while we figure out

01:21:08.520 --> 01:21:10.530
what is the right mix of capacity growth

01:21:10.530 --> 01:21:13.080
and clinical decision making in order

01:21:13.080 --> 01:21:15.540
to address the huge pent up need

01:21:15.540 --> 01:21:17.490
that we're seeing from the population.

01:21:17.490 --> 01:21:18.570
- Thank you.

01:21:18.570 --> 01:21:20.910
I'm going to slightly
conscious of time move us on,

01:21:20.910 --> 01:21:23.280
but thank you for the
update and Simon thank you

01:21:23.280 --> 01:21:25.503
and Sarah-Jane, thank you very much.

01:21:26.820 --> 01:21:29.400
And it was just to welcome Steve Powis.

01:21:29.400 --> 01:21:31.440
I know you only arrived late,

01:21:31.440 --> 01:21:32.700
you had the Public Accounts Committee.

01:21:32.700 --> 01:21:33.840
So I do understand you had a--

01:21:33.840 --> 01:21:35.250
- [Steve] I had the pleasure
of public accounting

01:21:35.250 --> 01:21:36.396
this morning, ow I have the pleasure

01:21:36.396 --> 01:21:38.340
of the Board this afternoon.

01:21:38.340 --> 01:21:40.040
- What a lovely day you're having.

01:21:42.750 --> 01:21:44.093
So Julian, financial performance.

01:21:45.127 --> 01:21:46.350
- So I'll take the papers read,

01:21:46.350 --> 01:21:48.100
but the headlines are

01:21:50.160 --> 01:21:52.800
as of the 27th of March,
we are forecasting

01:21:52.800 --> 01:21:54.690
to deliver within the NHS mandate.

01:21:54.690 --> 01:21:55.923
As I've said we would.

01:21:57.090 --> 01:21:58.170
At the moment, we forecast

01:21:58.170 --> 01:22:01.410
to underspend it by 25 to 50 million.

01:22:01.410 --> 01:22:03.360
There's a few things
we'll no doubt go through

01:22:03.360 --> 01:22:05.760
as we complete the accounts
the end of the year.

01:22:07.320 --> 01:22:11.550
With respect to the kind
of the NHS as a service.

01:22:11.550 --> 01:22:12.790
We will end the year

01:22:13.737 --> 01:22:17.910
with integrated care systems
600 million off plan,

01:22:20.088 --> 01:22:23.640
that compares to last
year when we were closer

01:22:23.640 --> 01:22:25.440
to one and a half billion off plan.

01:22:25.440 --> 01:22:27.900
And I think shows there's a long way to go

01:22:27.900 --> 01:22:32.850
but like the sort of super
tanker is turning in terms of,

01:22:32.850 --> 01:22:36.540
I mean just kind of the real hard yards

01:22:36.540 --> 01:22:39.810
of financial discipline, the
recovery of productivity,

01:22:39.810 --> 01:22:44.220
25 ICBs will be on plan

01:22:44.220 --> 01:22:46.770
but I mean, you know, the residual aren't

01:22:46.770 --> 01:22:50.523
and that is the detail of
that is set out in Annex A.

01:22:52.140 --> 01:22:55.200
I think what is striking is,

01:22:55.200 --> 01:22:57.720
you know we have seen
significant headcount growth

01:22:57.720 --> 01:22:59.160
this year.

01:22:59.160 --> 01:23:03.360
When you take the total WTE position

01:23:03.360 --> 01:23:04.950
and I include in that both substantive

01:23:04.950 --> 01:23:06.900
and temporary staffing.

01:23:06.900 --> 01:23:08.820
We've seen an increase
in substantive staffing

01:23:08.820 --> 01:23:12.300
but in total, the pay bill is down.

01:23:12.300 --> 01:23:15.480
So year-on-year, the
pay bill is down 0.7%.

01:23:15.480 --> 01:23:18.660
That is actually a shift
compared to previous years.

01:23:18.660 --> 01:23:23.250
And that is basically because
as people have recruited,

01:23:23.250 --> 01:23:27.060
they are taking out the the
temporary staffing costs.

01:23:27.060 --> 01:23:30.450
We are seeing that particularly
again on the agency bill

01:23:30.450 --> 01:23:33.690
this year where we are
forecasting total agency costs

01:23:33.690 --> 01:23:35.070
this year of about 2.1 billion

01:23:35.070 --> 01:23:36.690
by the way compared to two years ago.

01:23:36.690 --> 01:23:39.150
That is like where it was like more like

01:23:39.150 --> 01:23:41.130
three and a half billion pounds

01:23:41.130 --> 01:23:42.390
that, you know, really is a shift.

01:23:42.390 --> 01:23:44.880
And I think probably about the
lowest it's almost ever been.

01:23:44.880 --> 01:23:47.040
Not just as a percentage of payroll

01:23:47.040 --> 01:23:50.163
but actually in cash terms as well.

01:23:51.150 --> 01:23:54.183
But look, more hard yards
to be done next year.

01:23:55.290 --> 01:23:56.373
Productivity.

01:24:03.033 --> 01:24:05.580
Yeah, so we've published
a paper today as part

01:24:05.580 --> 01:24:10.290
of this report that shows
that consistent with

01:24:10.290 --> 01:24:12.690
what we have said all the way through

01:24:12.690 --> 01:24:14.100
and despite the fact that I think

01:24:14.100 --> 01:24:15.120
that people have been concerned

01:24:15.120 --> 01:24:17.230
that NHS productivity is still falling

01:24:18.180 --> 01:24:21.570
after, you know, the sharp fall in 2021,

01:24:21.570 --> 01:24:24.879
a reasonably sharp
rebound in 21-22, the ONS

01:24:24.879 --> 01:24:27.870
as you have said, have
published their latest

01:24:27.870 --> 01:24:30.600
sort of gold standard analysis
of public sector productivity

01:24:30.600 --> 01:24:33.690
and health productivity
this morning that shows

01:24:33.690 --> 01:24:38.670
that the NHS in England saw
an improvement in productivity

01:24:38.670 --> 01:24:42.840
of just around 2% in
the calendar year 22-23.

01:24:42.840 --> 01:24:45.210
By the way, that almost precisely matches

01:24:45.210 --> 01:24:49.053
what we have said in the
measurement we were making,

01:24:50.970 --> 01:24:53.942
our data would say we
have continued to recover

01:24:53.942 --> 01:24:56.190
through 23-24 as Amanda said earlier,

01:24:56.190 --> 01:25:00.960
acute sector productivity
is up year-on-year 24-25

01:25:00.960 --> 01:25:04.983
by 2.7% through to the end of December,

01:25:06.150 --> 01:25:09.513
actually almost three
times the historic rate.

01:25:10.890 --> 01:25:13.410
We are publishing for the first time

01:25:13.410 --> 01:25:17.550
non-acute sector productivity
data covering mental health,

01:25:17.550 --> 01:25:19.200
community and ambulance trusts.

01:25:19.200 --> 01:25:24.200
And again that is showing
we think around, you know,

01:25:24.240 --> 01:25:27.150
the same level of productivity
improvement year-on-year

01:25:27.150 --> 01:25:31.410
23-24 to 24-25.

01:25:31.410 --> 01:25:34.890
Now the ONS data by the
way shows that at the end

01:25:34.890 --> 01:25:39.890
of 22-23, the gap to 2019-20 was about 5%.

01:25:41.820 --> 01:25:44.670
Now there are one or two technical
things which will change,

01:25:44.670 --> 01:25:48.060
but I think broadly if you
were to look at that today

01:25:48.060 --> 01:25:50.700
for the total NHS, I think
that would probably show you

01:25:50.700 --> 01:25:55.650
around three to 4% gap today

01:25:55.650 --> 01:25:57.420
and therefore a real
chance to kind of close it

01:25:57.420 --> 01:26:02.303
as we go through next year,
again, with the right focus.

01:26:03.510 --> 01:26:06.180
- So that's recovering from
the productivity challenge

01:26:06.180 --> 01:26:07.620
of dealing with the pandemic?

01:26:07.620 --> 01:26:08.580
- Yeah.
- Yeah.

01:26:08.580 --> 01:26:09.413
- Yeah.

01:26:10.914 --> 01:26:13.920
And look, as I said, you can see

01:26:13.920 --> 01:26:16.680
the kind of just getting back
the kind of basic discipline,

01:26:16.680 --> 01:26:18.630
I mean it, it's worth
remembering that even

01:26:18.630 --> 01:26:20.613
before the pandemic,

01:26:21.690 --> 01:26:24.030
NHS finances were significantly stressed.

01:26:24.030 --> 01:26:27.240
I mean it's, you know, in a previous life

01:26:27.240 --> 01:26:32.220
when I was in the Treasury.

01:26:32.220 --> 01:26:34.500
- On the dark side.
- Yeah on the dark side.

01:26:34.500 --> 01:26:37.380
And we're working with Jim
to kind of wrestle, you know,

01:26:37.380 --> 01:26:40.470
at the time, provider finances
underlyingly, you know,

01:26:40.470 --> 01:26:42.570
had sort of deficits of
around three billion pounds.

01:26:42.570 --> 01:26:45.340
So it shows this is a long standing issue

01:26:46.560 --> 01:26:51.120
but you know, I would say green shoots

01:26:51.120 --> 01:26:56.120
of recovery but a lot further
to go, now looking ahead,

01:26:56.910 --> 01:27:00.040
we have had back today

01:27:02.220 --> 01:27:05.193
the sort of returns about
people's plans for next year.

01:27:06.630 --> 01:27:09.483
On the positive side,

01:27:10.410 --> 01:27:12.610
I think we've got 37 systems

01:27:13.689 --> 01:27:16.980
who are showing plans to
kind of live within the money

01:27:16.980 --> 01:27:21.450
that we have said is available.

01:27:21.450 --> 01:27:25.350
We have five systems,
Cheshire, Devon, Sussex,

01:27:25.350 --> 01:27:27.210
Southwest London, Greater Manchester

01:27:27.210 --> 01:27:30.330
who are still showing
they have a gap to plan

01:27:30.330 --> 01:27:33.480
of about 300 million pounds, now I think

01:27:33.480 --> 01:27:36.300
that is better than we, you
know, had thought we might be.

01:27:36.300 --> 01:27:39.273
And I think that's testament
to a lot of the work of,

01:27:41.310 --> 01:27:44.214
well people throughout the
NHS including in NHS England.

01:27:44.214 --> 01:27:48.792
And I think it's also a
reflection of the work that,

01:27:48.792 --> 01:27:51.510
you know, Jim has also been doing

01:27:51.510 --> 01:27:53.700
and some of the stuff that's
happened over the last three

01:27:53.700 --> 01:27:55.080
or four weeks where
people really have clocked

01:27:55.080 --> 01:27:59.430
nearly the money is the money,
now I think when we really go

01:27:59.430 --> 01:28:01.363
through those plans
we're going to discover

01:28:01.363 --> 01:28:03.446
a couple of things.

01:28:03.446 --> 01:28:04.770
One is there is still further

01:28:04.770 --> 01:28:08.940
to go in turning things
written down on the spreadsheet

01:28:08.940 --> 01:28:11.010
into an actual plan

01:28:11.010 --> 01:28:14.790
and that there is still further work to do

01:28:14.790 --> 01:28:17.250
because I suspect what it'll
show is people think they can

01:28:17.250 --> 01:28:20.220
sort of keep emergency
performance improvements

01:28:20.220 --> 01:28:23.910
sort of steady rather than
the sort of shift Andrew,

01:28:23.910 --> 01:28:25.740
that you were talking about.

01:28:25.740 --> 01:28:27.810
And I think we will still find

01:28:27.810 --> 01:28:31.350
there is a way to go to
really nail down with people

01:28:31.350 --> 01:28:34.140
how they can show the improvement
in elective performance

01:28:34.140 --> 01:28:36.513
that the government is clear is a must do.

01:28:38.130 --> 01:28:42.150
And I think that is the work
that the team have planned

01:28:42.150 --> 01:28:45.153
with Jim in particular
over the next month.

01:28:45.153 --> 01:28:46.470
And I think the critical
thing is getting away from

01:28:46.470 --> 01:28:49.200
negotiating about the
money to now nailing down

01:28:49.200 --> 01:28:50.127
what is the actual action.

01:28:50.127 --> 01:28:53.640
And I think there's a chance
to do that in particular

01:28:53.640 --> 01:28:58.200
over the next month in support of that

01:28:58.200 --> 01:29:02.100
actually a real focus on
productivity is the key.

01:29:02.100 --> 01:29:04.410
We were talking in the
Private Board about, you know,

01:29:04.410 --> 01:29:06.450
the challenges of the future

01:29:06.450 --> 01:29:09.150
and you can see a way through

01:29:09.150 --> 01:29:13.980
if pay settlements are
sensible with, you know,

01:29:13.980 --> 01:29:15.840
real terms growth in NHS funding

01:29:15.840 --> 01:29:19.110
as long as we can deliver
kind of 2% year-on-year

01:29:19.110 --> 01:29:19.943
productivity plan.

01:29:19.943 --> 01:29:24.943
Now for next year, 25-26, you know we had

01:29:25.260 --> 01:29:28.890
a set of proposals, well we have a plan

01:29:28.890 --> 01:29:33.890
which is to focus on
training 20,000 managers

01:29:33.990 --> 01:29:37.290
in just the kind of basic
operational management skills

01:29:37.290 --> 01:29:40.090
in providing and investing in improvement

01:29:41.735 --> 01:29:44.567
senior clinically led improvement support

01:29:45.690 --> 01:29:48.510
that could potentially cover,
you know, in different levels

01:29:48.510 --> 01:29:50.790
of intensity up to 50% of our trusts.

01:29:50.790 --> 01:29:54.900
The ongoing work on technology

01:29:54.900 --> 01:29:57.250
and as I said just a real
focus on improvement.

01:29:59.250 --> 01:30:01.950
If we could, as I said
on the Private Board,

01:30:01.950 --> 01:30:04.680
the one thing I would invest in

01:30:04.680 --> 01:30:07.650
and make happen if it is at
all possible is basically

01:30:07.650 --> 01:30:09.887
rolling out ambient voice technology

01:30:09.887 --> 01:30:12.540
'cause I think that we know is actually,

01:30:12.540 --> 01:30:14.190
it is not a complicated thing Mark

01:30:14.190 --> 01:30:16.620
as you were describing earlier,

01:30:16.620 --> 01:30:19.680
it can be done and we know
it can be transformational

01:30:19.680 --> 01:30:23.010
both in the way that clinicians
can interact with patients

01:30:23.010 --> 01:30:26.160
but also it's just time saving

01:30:26.160 --> 01:30:28.953
just to automate the basic admin tasks.

01:30:30.150 --> 01:30:32.400
But anyway, that's where we
are as we look, you know,

01:30:32.400 --> 01:30:33.630
as we close out this year.

01:30:33.630 --> 01:30:34.463
So

01:30:36.390 --> 01:30:39.180
solid improvement, further to go,

01:30:39.180 --> 01:30:42.480
people taking seriously what
they need to do for next year.

01:30:42.480 --> 01:30:45.810
But I think again further
to go to turn it from

01:30:45.810 --> 01:30:48.873
ideas on a page to actions
we can all be confident in.

01:30:50.310 --> 01:30:51.143
- Jeremy?

01:30:52.110 --> 01:30:52.980
- First of all, thanks Julian

01:30:52.980 --> 01:30:55.350
for all your hard work
while we work together.

01:30:55.350 --> 01:30:57.840
Secondly, just further to field,

01:30:57.840 --> 01:30:59.610
I was going to ask a question
on short-term productivity,

01:30:59.610 --> 01:31:03.570
longer-term, any input to Penny, Jim

01:31:03.570 --> 01:31:06.390
and your successor Julian
on long term productivity

01:31:06.390 --> 01:31:08.430
going into the 10-year plan opportunities.

01:31:08.430 --> 01:31:11.100
And thirdly, would you like
a job with Mark and Spencer's

01:31:11.100 --> 01:31:12.367
on forecasting?

01:31:12.367 --> 01:31:14.013
'Cause it's more accurate.

01:31:17.610 --> 01:31:19.811
On the second question you could answer.

01:31:19.811 --> 01:31:20.644
- No.

01:31:21.780 --> 01:31:24.033
I won't say which question I'm answering.

01:31:25.500 --> 01:31:27.840
I mean look on the sort of longer term,

01:31:27.840 --> 01:31:30.090
I actually think it is the,

01:31:30.090 --> 01:31:32.970
you've got to get the basics
right, you've got to get people

01:31:32.970 --> 01:31:35.340
with the right skills,
with the right support.

01:31:35.340 --> 01:31:38.760
Without all the fancy stuff
I think doesn't really work.

01:31:38.760 --> 01:31:41.455
And it goes back to some of the stuff

01:31:41.455 --> 01:31:43.440
that we were just discussing about

01:31:43.440 --> 01:31:46.920
how we can honestly improve
urgent and emergency care,

01:31:46.920 --> 01:31:50.370
a real focus on whole system working.

01:31:50.370 --> 01:31:52.140
Because in the end that is the theme

01:31:52.140 --> 01:31:54.870
of joining it upright across the piece

01:31:54.870 --> 01:31:57.780
and the role that I think
commissioners have to really make

01:31:57.780 --> 01:31:59.883
that work is incredibly important.

01:32:01.269 --> 01:32:04.500
And then if we can do those things,

01:32:04.500 --> 01:32:06.660
and by the way there's
huge potential, you know,

01:32:06.660 --> 01:32:08.400
I mean if you look at some
of the productivity data

01:32:08.400 --> 01:32:11.130
we have given even around mental health

01:32:11.130 --> 01:32:14.760
and community services,
there really are gains,

01:32:14.760 --> 01:32:17.160
huge gains that can be made.

01:32:17.160 --> 01:32:21.005
But then if we can get the
basics on the tech right, I think

01:32:21.005 --> 01:32:23.470
that is the thing that shifts the paradigm

01:32:25.260 --> 01:32:29.010
and you know, half the gain
of the future needs to come

01:32:29.010 --> 01:32:32.070
through investment there consistent.

01:32:32.070 --> 01:32:35.853
It's like consistent repeated
investment in that area.

01:32:38.738 --> 01:32:40.655
- So Julian, thank you.

01:32:41.520 --> 01:32:45.010
So it sounds like the initial submissions

01:32:45.960 --> 01:32:48.750
which are pretty high levels

01:32:48.750 --> 01:32:51.153
of overspend in initial submissions,

01:32:52.050 --> 01:32:55.558
although similar to
last year's submissions

01:32:55.558 --> 01:32:59.451
when they first came in, I
mean the work done by you

01:32:59.451 --> 01:33:03.180
and with Jim's, huge, huge, huge support

01:33:03.180 --> 01:33:05.426
and call on it looks like

01:33:05.426 --> 01:33:06.259
that those numbers have come within

01:33:06.259 --> 01:33:08.733
a much more reasonable
range at least as a plan.

01:33:10.080 --> 01:33:13.080
So it's a few hundred million more to do

01:33:13.080 --> 01:33:14.100
and then much more to do

01:33:14.100 --> 01:33:15.570
as we talked this morning at the Board

01:33:15.570 --> 01:33:19.440
and Jim talked to the Board
making sure it's not easy

01:33:19.440 --> 01:33:21.780
to write the plan but you've
then got to deliver the plan.

01:33:21.780 --> 01:33:24.090
So actually that will
take some very real work.

01:33:24.090 --> 01:33:28.410
And we also know, and
Jim you are very sort of

01:33:28.410 --> 01:33:30.120
strong on this morning
and wholly appropriately

01:33:30.120 --> 01:33:31.500
strong on this morning with Julian

01:33:31.500 --> 01:33:33.360
that you know, there'll be
some plans in there actually

01:33:33.360 --> 01:33:35.313
where there's a, you know,

01:33:35.313 --> 01:33:38.010
some real credibility issues
we've got to get after

01:33:38.010 --> 01:33:39.570
and get after really quite quickly.

01:33:39.570 --> 01:33:41.970
So it's helpful that the initial step

01:33:41.970 --> 01:33:44.670
is much more reasonable to start with,

01:33:44.670 --> 01:33:47.430
but now that step's got
to be sort of concreted in

01:33:47.430 --> 01:33:51.453
and confidence in delivery
increased and enhanced I think.

01:33:52.554 --> 01:33:54.323
And then the productivity challenge,

01:33:54.323 --> 01:33:56.190
4% in the planning guidance.

01:33:56.190 --> 01:33:59.340
So that's a hard challenge.

01:33:59.340 --> 01:34:00.687
It's an appropriate level of challenge

01:34:00.687 --> 01:34:04.230
and it's good actually that
we're just running at 2.7%

01:34:04.230 --> 01:34:05.640
in this latest year.

01:34:05.640 --> 01:34:07.500
But 4% is a step up.

01:34:07.500 --> 01:34:12.500
And if I had another sort of just concern,

01:34:12.600 --> 01:34:14.670
it's also that a number of the measures

01:34:14.670 --> 01:34:17.250
that the various trust
systems were taking last year

01:34:17.250 --> 01:34:21.180
may have taken, it's unfair
to call the low hanging fruit

01:34:21.180 --> 01:34:24.480
but you know, as you
go is it harder to find

01:34:24.480 --> 01:34:27.510
sources essentially of
effectiveness and efficiency?

01:34:27.510 --> 01:34:29.940
And it may well be said
it's a steep challenge still

01:34:29.940 --> 01:34:32.280
I think.
- Definitely, absolutely.

01:34:32.280 --> 01:34:34.260
So what we'll do in the
next few weeks is really key

01:34:34.260 --> 01:34:36.480
to get under the plans
work, work with people,

01:34:36.480 --> 01:34:38.725
understand the risk and how it's managed.

01:34:38.725 --> 01:34:40.225
- Yeah, very good.

01:34:41.175 --> 01:34:42.008
Go on, yeah Amanda.

01:34:42.008 --> 01:34:44.440
- On that, sorry but just because this is

01:34:45.540 --> 01:34:46.773
Julian's last meeting,

01:34:47.816 --> 01:34:49.710
and you've mentioned fantasy plans

01:34:49.710 --> 01:34:51.150
'cause there was I think quite a lot

01:34:51.150 --> 01:34:54.090
of anxiety over the course
of the year we're in now.

01:34:54.090 --> 01:34:56.490
So thinking about 24-25

01:34:56.490 --> 01:34:59.610
about the kind of confidence
in plans that ICBs

01:34:59.610 --> 01:35:00.810
and providers have put forward.

01:35:00.810 --> 01:35:03.210
I think Julian, I can't quite, sorry,

01:35:03.210 --> 01:35:05.580
I can't quite put my finger
on it, but the plans,

01:35:05.580 --> 01:35:06.930
the variance from plan

01:35:06.930 --> 01:35:08.790
that you're describing
obviously does include

01:35:08.790 --> 01:35:11.070
the agreed deficit
support and other things

01:35:11.070 --> 01:35:14.130
that were in people's start positions.

01:35:14.130 --> 01:35:15.900
But it feels to me looking at this

01:35:15.900 --> 01:35:19.140
that more systems are coming
in on plan than you had perhaps

01:35:19.140 --> 01:35:21.270
anticipated earlier in the year.

01:35:21.270 --> 01:35:23.280
So I just wanted to check
that I had interpreted

01:35:23.280 --> 01:35:25.530
that correctly 'cause that
would suggest that actually

01:35:25.530 --> 01:35:29.130
some of that anxiety about
plans not being as robust

01:35:29.130 --> 01:35:32.190
as we might want, you
know, have all wanted them

01:35:32.190 --> 01:35:34.680
to be including local leaders themselves

01:35:34.680 --> 01:35:35.970
and fact proved unfounded

01:35:35.970 --> 01:35:37.650
and people have come in on their plans,

01:35:37.650 --> 01:35:39.390
which thinking about this
year would give us perhaps

01:35:39.390 --> 01:35:41.400
a little bit more confidence.

01:35:41.400 --> 01:35:43.230
- Yeah, I mean, I thought,

01:35:43.230 --> 01:35:45.930
I've always thought we would
do better than last year.

01:35:47.460 --> 01:35:48.630
I think we've probably managed

01:35:48.630 --> 01:35:50.100
to do slightly better than the better

01:35:50.100 --> 01:35:54.930
that I thought we might manage to do.

01:35:54.930 --> 01:35:58.680
You can still see in fact in Annex A,

01:35:58.680 --> 01:36:00.615
like you can see the places

01:36:00.615 --> 01:36:02.640
that like are really materially off,

01:36:02.640 --> 01:36:05.223
all right, and where you can see,

01:36:08.370 --> 01:36:09.750
well either, you know, they had a plan,

01:36:09.750 --> 01:36:12.371
they just haven't delivered on it

01:36:12.371 --> 01:36:15.540
or there wasn't really a plan
there in the first place.

01:36:15.540 --> 01:36:19.467
I think the good thing looking into 25-26

01:36:19.467 --> 01:36:22.200
and last year was complicated
by just how late everything,

01:36:22.200 --> 01:36:25.200
you know, like the total
funding was agreed.

01:36:25.200 --> 01:36:29.190
And so it's a better place to be

01:36:29.190 --> 01:36:30.810
because I think it does mean you can focus

01:36:30.810 --> 01:36:33.630
not on discussions about money, et cetera.

01:36:33.630 --> 01:36:35.760
You can focus on what's actually needed

01:36:35.760 --> 01:36:37.377
to be done to deliver it.

01:36:37.377 --> 01:36:39.450
And I think in the
conversations I've had with some

01:36:39.450 --> 01:36:42.721
of the systems, I think
they're more realistic about

01:36:42.721 --> 01:36:45.330
what still needs to be done

01:36:45.330 --> 01:36:47.310
even over the next one,
two and three months.

01:36:47.310 --> 01:36:50.613
But it's like seeing that
plan just really get,

01:36:51.776 --> 01:36:53.970
what's the word I'm looking for?

01:36:53.970 --> 01:36:54.813
Made real.

01:36:56.070 --> 01:36:58.740
But as we've discussed elsewhere

01:36:58.740 --> 01:37:00.390
with Jim, I think there are four

01:37:00.390 --> 01:37:03.030
or five places, some of the
ones I mentioned at the start

01:37:03.030 --> 01:37:05.130
where I think right now.

01:37:05.130 --> 01:37:07.500
- Early engagement.
- Well I'd say actually

01:37:07.500 --> 01:37:08.333
stronger than that.

01:37:08.333 --> 01:37:10.890
I think a turnaround approach is needed

01:37:10.890 --> 01:37:12.930
and it's needed from
the start of the year.

01:37:12.930 --> 01:37:14.733
It's like don't even wait.

01:37:16.170 --> 01:37:18.870
But again I think we're
in a better place to know

01:37:18.870 --> 01:37:21.887
where those places are
to take the action now.

01:37:21.887 --> 01:37:25.083
But it is plenty of challenge ahead.

01:37:26.520 --> 01:37:30.240
- Okay, so let's move on to the next item.

01:37:30.240 --> 01:37:31.560
Thank you for that Julian

01:37:31.560 --> 01:37:36.560
and also that's your last
CFO's report at the NHS.

01:37:36.870 --> 01:37:37.703
What a relief, well done.

01:37:37.703 --> 01:37:38.680
Thank you very much.

01:37:43.108 --> 01:37:45.720
So we got to go on, the
performance assessment framework.

01:37:45.720 --> 01:37:48.720
I think Steve, it's you and Adam I think.

01:37:48.720 --> 01:37:49.553
- Thank you Richard.

01:37:49.553 --> 01:37:50.760
We'll do a bit of a double act.

01:37:50.760 --> 01:37:52.650
- I assume the paper's are read.

01:37:52.650 --> 01:37:55.135
I assume they are read,
let's get the main point.

01:37:55.135 --> 01:37:58.629
- So conscious of time, we'll
just step through some of

01:37:58.629 --> 01:38:00.600
the key points in here 'cause
it's a while since the Board

01:38:00.600 --> 01:38:03.180
last had this in front of them.

01:38:03.180 --> 01:38:05.670
So just to orientate
colleagues, you'll be aware

01:38:05.670 --> 01:38:07.443
that we've currently got
an oversight framework

01:38:07.443 --> 01:38:12.443
that is in place, that
was last updated in 2022.

01:38:14.335 --> 01:38:17.760
And we've previously
agreed with the system

01:38:17.760 --> 01:38:19.410
and within NHS England

01:38:19.410 --> 01:38:23.640
that we would review the
framework and update it.

01:38:23.640 --> 01:38:26.250
And we did quite a bit
of engagement at the time

01:38:26.250 --> 01:38:29.550
and then colleagues may
recall that we brought

01:38:29.550 --> 01:38:33.840
a proposed framework, an updated framework

01:38:33.840 --> 01:38:38.610
to the Board in about
June 2024, which responded

01:38:38.610 --> 01:38:42.423
or sought to respond to some
of the feedback that we'd had.

01:38:43.860 --> 01:38:47.340
Some of that feedback was then built on

01:38:47.340 --> 01:38:51.180
by a formal consultation
exercise that was run

01:38:51.180 --> 01:38:53.230
after the Board considered the framework.

01:38:54.120 --> 01:38:56.940
The feedback from that
indicated broad support

01:38:56.940 --> 01:38:59.670
for the proposals at the
time, there were lots

01:38:59.670 --> 01:39:00.810
of different bits of feedback,

01:39:00.810 --> 01:39:04.773
but a couple of the groups were from ICBs

01:39:04.773 --> 01:39:07.890
that there was insufficient
focus on the long-term

01:39:07.890 --> 01:39:11.490
and from community and mental
health trusts in particular

01:39:11.490 --> 01:39:13.750
that there was less focus on

01:39:15.000 --> 01:39:18.633
on their contribution
compared to the acute sector.

01:39:19.530 --> 01:39:23.100
And at the time, there
was a set of proposals

01:39:23.100 --> 01:39:27.903
that effectively bound
providers and ICBs together.

01:39:28.950 --> 01:39:30.690
And obviously

01:39:30.690 --> 01:39:34.173
after that point, the context
changed quite considerably.

01:39:35.190 --> 01:39:37.470
We had an election and a new government

01:39:37.470 --> 01:39:40.800
and the government
issued an updated mandate

01:39:40.800 --> 01:39:44.250
to the NHS focusing on recovery

01:39:44.250 --> 01:39:47.220
and accelerated recovery around
the constitutional standards

01:39:47.220 --> 01:39:50.970
and financial performance in
particular alongside a desire

01:39:50.970 --> 01:39:53.160
to really make progress
on the three shifts

01:39:53.160 --> 01:39:56.520
and to move to neighbourhood health

01:39:56.520 --> 01:39:58.380
and have also been were

01:39:58.380 --> 01:40:01.620
and continue to be keen to
make sure there is sufficient

01:40:01.620 --> 01:40:05.160
and appropriate public
information about the performance

01:40:05.160 --> 01:40:08.730
of the local NHS from a
transparency perspective

01:40:08.730 --> 01:40:13.560
and that there's good
accountability to local people

01:40:13.560 --> 01:40:16.560
for the performance of the NHS.

01:40:16.560 --> 01:40:20.670
So in support of that, we updated

01:40:20.670 --> 01:40:23.440
how we were describing the
operating model for the NHS

01:40:23.440 --> 01:40:25.680
after that time in around November.

01:40:25.680 --> 01:40:26.850
It was to kind of try

01:40:26.850 --> 01:40:31.110
and clarify the key
contributions of NHS providers

01:40:31.110 --> 01:40:33.810
and Integrated Care Boards
recognising that of course

01:40:33.810 --> 01:40:35.430
that was a point in time

01:40:35.430 --> 01:40:38.190
and no doubt that will continue to evolve.

01:40:38.190 --> 01:40:40.350
But that basically focus set out

01:40:40.350 --> 01:40:42.600
that providers would
have a particular focus

01:40:42.600 --> 01:40:45.840
on high quality care, value for money

01:40:45.840 --> 01:40:48.030
and a strong, strong culture

01:40:48.030 --> 01:40:51.150
and staff engagement within organisations.

01:40:51.150 --> 01:40:54.600
And the ICBs would focus on
what we described at the time

01:40:54.600 --> 01:40:56.190
as strategic commissioning.

01:40:56.190 --> 01:40:58.050
So a big focus on understanding

01:40:58.050 --> 01:41:00.600
of population health,
arranging services in a way

01:41:00.600 --> 01:41:03.540
that would start to move
towards that left shift

01:41:03.540 --> 01:41:05.670
and implement neighbourhood health

01:41:05.670 --> 01:41:08.040
and would have particular
focus on utilisation

01:41:08.040 --> 01:41:09.440
and care management as well.

01:41:10.590 --> 01:41:13.080
We published at the time two documents

01:41:13.080 --> 01:41:16.620
called the Insightful Board
to try and help providers

01:41:16.620 --> 01:41:21.240
and ICBs focus and strengthen
their governance arrangements

01:41:21.240 --> 01:41:23.550
in that context.

01:41:23.550 --> 01:41:25.320
And all of that said, it is really

01:41:25.320 --> 01:41:26.430
therefore clearly important

01:41:26.430 --> 01:41:30.510
that whatever assessment
framework is in place for the NHS,

01:41:30.510 --> 01:41:32.790
it reflects all of those changes

01:41:32.790 --> 01:41:35.033
that have occurred since
we were last with you.

01:41:36.330 --> 01:41:39.720
And so we've continued to do
work with the Department to try

01:41:39.720 --> 01:41:42.030
and make sure that the framework
which you've got in front

01:41:42.030 --> 01:41:45.030
of you today or in your papers rather,

01:41:45.030 --> 01:41:49.574
reflects some of those shifts
and changes that we've seen,

01:41:49.574 --> 01:41:52.839
and in the recent last couple
of weeks we've been able

01:41:52.839 --> 01:41:54.030
to and had the benefit
of engaging with Penny

01:41:54.030 --> 01:41:56.370
and Jim about where we'd
got to and then therefore

01:41:56.370 --> 01:41:59.100
what the right set of next steps were.

01:41:59.100 --> 01:42:01.230
So what you've got in the pack

01:42:01.230 --> 01:42:04.320
is the proposed assessment
framework for 25-26

01:42:05.537 --> 01:42:07.260
so that the NHS has got clarity about

01:42:07.260 --> 01:42:10.770
how it will be assessed,
how we'll identify areas

01:42:10.770 --> 01:42:13.200
for improvement and to guide

01:42:13.200 --> 01:42:16.770
where enforcement action
might need to be taken.

01:42:16.770 --> 01:42:20.220
And it also sets out how
we'll assess the capability

01:42:20.220 --> 01:42:22.833
of Integrated Care Boards and providers.

01:42:23.790 --> 01:42:26.070
Adam's going to run through
just a bit about the mechanics

01:42:26.070 --> 01:42:29.013
saying it's important that
colleagues are aware of that,

01:42:30.360 --> 01:42:32.820
but it's also important to
just highlight to the Board

01:42:32.820 --> 01:42:36.450
that of course what is
with Board colleagues today

01:42:36.450 --> 01:42:38.910
is quite different to what
we consulted on before.

01:42:38.910 --> 01:42:40.380
And of course the environment

01:42:40.380 --> 01:42:43.620
and circumstances continue
to shape and change.

01:42:43.620 --> 01:42:46.620
And I'll say a little bit
about next steps at the end,

01:42:46.620 --> 01:42:48.390
but there are a couple of
points just to highlight.

01:42:48.390 --> 01:42:50.160
So one is that the changes

01:42:50.160 --> 01:42:53.340
or key changes that have been
made are set out in the annex

01:42:53.340 --> 01:42:55.113
so that colleagues can see those.

01:42:56.250 --> 01:42:59.430
And the second thing is,
this is an important part

01:42:59.430 --> 01:43:02.130
of the puzzle in the NHS in some respects

01:43:02.130 --> 01:43:03.930
because quite a lot of
things might hang off

01:43:03.930 --> 01:43:07.390
the oversight framework as
they have indeed in the past

01:43:08.359 --> 01:43:10.770
and recognising that it is different

01:43:10.770 --> 01:43:14.130
and that the environment
continues to change.

01:43:14.130 --> 01:43:15.990
We'll come back to this at the
end, but what we're proposing

01:43:15.990 --> 01:43:17.640
here in agreement with Penny and Jim

01:43:17.640 --> 01:43:21.060
and colleagues at the
Department is that subject

01:43:21.060 --> 01:43:25.050
to Board approval, the
framework would be tested

01:43:25.050 --> 01:43:27.030
with the service and would be subject

01:43:27.030 --> 01:43:29.850
to a further formal consultation.

01:43:29.850 --> 01:43:31.380
So it's just important to say that then

01:43:31.380 --> 01:43:33.780
I'll maybe just frame
that point at the end when

01:43:33.780 --> 01:43:36.450
Adam's done his bit, if that's all right.

01:43:36.450 --> 01:43:37.680
- Thanks Steve.

01:43:37.680 --> 01:43:39.257
Good afternoon, colleagues.

01:43:39.257 --> 01:43:41.850
So the framework works
in a way where each ICB

01:43:41.850 --> 01:43:44.850
and each provider is allocated a segment,

01:43:44.850 --> 01:43:46.080
a form of segmentation.

01:43:46.080 --> 01:43:50.370
So one highly performing,
four poorly performing

01:43:50.370 --> 01:43:53.070
and there's an additional
segment five introduced

01:43:53.070 --> 01:43:55.500
where we'll perform a
diagnostic in all segment four

01:43:55.500 --> 01:43:57.900
organisations to ascertain whether or not

01:43:57.900 --> 01:44:01.173
we should place them into
recovery support going forward.

01:44:02.220 --> 01:44:03.900
The framework looks at a balanced set

01:44:03.900 --> 01:44:07.082
of measures across a number of domains

01:44:07.082 --> 01:44:08.940
and it's been interesting
listening to the discussion today

01:44:08.940 --> 01:44:11.430
because you can almost tick in the annex

01:44:11.430 --> 01:44:13.770
the kind of measures that
people have been talking about

01:44:13.770 --> 01:44:17.430
when they're describing
keeping people out of hospital,

01:44:17.430 --> 01:44:19.350
non-conveyance to ED, all those areas.

01:44:19.350 --> 01:44:21.390
So it feels pretty prescient when we look

01:44:21.390 --> 01:44:23.493
at the discussion of the Board today.

01:44:24.570 --> 01:44:27.180
Also I would say though
each of the organisations,

01:44:27.180 --> 01:44:28.380
they are very different.

01:44:28.380 --> 01:44:30.690
So we've tried to allocate
the kind of measures

01:44:30.690 --> 01:44:32.430
that you could directly say

01:44:32.430 --> 01:44:35.730
that is a kind of ICB
driven measure versus

01:44:35.730 --> 01:44:38.880
what would be an ambulance
trust measure going forward.

01:44:38.880 --> 01:44:40.770
Important to note that primary care

01:44:40.770 --> 01:44:42.270
is an accessory of the ICB

01:44:42.270 --> 01:44:44.340
because the of the particular relationship

01:44:44.340 --> 01:44:48.210
that we have with delegated
commissioning into that area.

01:44:48.210 --> 01:44:49.950
But the measures were selected also

01:44:49.950 --> 01:44:53.520
by a clinical advisory
group co-chaired by our CNO

01:44:53.520 --> 01:44:56.490
and one of our incoming
co-medical directors.

01:44:56.490 --> 01:44:57.900
And they've worked really, really hard

01:44:57.900 --> 01:45:01.410
within the organisation and
through the system to make sure

01:45:01.410 --> 01:45:03.660
that the measures were
right and appropriate.

01:45:05.100 --> 01:45:06.810
The framework allows for an annual update

01:45:06.810 --> 01:45:09.480
and I think Steve's earlier
point, if things change

01:45:09.480 --> 01:45:11.340
with the 10-year plan, there's the ability

01:45:11.340 --> 01:45:12.570
to put different things in

01:45:12.570 --> 01:45:14.700
and out depending on the pressures

01:45:14.700 --> 01:45:16.600
or the requirements in any given year.

01:45:18.150 --> 01:45:21.060
But the key part for this is the last time

01:45:21.060 --> 01:45:22.890
we put this to the Board, we talked about

01:45:22.890 --> 01:45:25.230
how the system was pulled together

01:45:25.230 --> 01:45:27.570
in how we assess how things operate.

01:45:27.570 --> 01:45:29.520
In this model, all of the providers

01:45:29.520 --> 01:45:31.020
will be given a score

01:45:31.020 --> 01:45:33.510
regardless of what happens around them.

01:45:33.510 --> 01:45:36.780
However, the ICBs because of
their strategic responsibility

01:45:36.780 --> 01:45:39.840
to allocate resources,
if they're in a system

01:45:39.840 --> 01:45:42.630
where they may be with
their score performing well

01:45:42.630 --> 01:45:44.820
but a provider in that system is not,

01:45:44.820 --> 01:45:46.710
they will have their score moderated

01:45:46.710 --> 01:45:49.680
because we would expect them
as the leader of the system

01:45:49.680 --> 01:45:52.330
to therefore be held to
account for making that work.

01:45:53.910 --> 01:45:57.120
Our aim is that the organisations
in segment one are given

01:45:57.120 --> 01:46:01.170
as much autonomy as possible
and that's quite clear to us.

01:46:01.170 --> 01:46:03.840
But also it's really,
really clear that in three

01:46:03.840 --> 01:46:07.620
and four, we will give a
targeted level of support

01:46:07.620 --> 01:46:10.140
but also enforcement
actions where required

01:46:10.140 --> 01:46:11.850
for those organisations.

01:46:11.850 --> 01:46:13.080
And as I said earlier,

01:46:13.080 --> 01:46:16.440
segment five falls into recovery support.

01:46:16.440 --> 01:46:20.460
We aim to test these operational
plans as they come through.

01:46:20.460 --> 01:46:22.080
So Julian's covered that earlier.

01:46:22.080 --> 01:46:25.140
We do want to make sure that
all systems know in the plan

01:46:25.140 --> 01:46:27.180
that they've submitted this would be

01:46:27.180 --> 01:46:30.360
where we think if you deliver
that plan to what you've said,

01:46:30.360 --> 01:46:32.460
that's where we think
you will land this year.

01:46:32.460 --> 01:46:34.920
We just think it's important
that they know in submitting

01:46:34.920 --> 01:46:37.590
what you submitted, we would
put you at a segment three.

01:46:37.590 --> 01:46:40.470
Do you want to do something
now to kind of intervene

01:46:40.470 --> 01:46:41.973
to improve that going forward?

01:46:43.020 --> 01:46:44.280
In terms of leadership,

01:46:44.280 --> 01:46:45.960
the last time we discussed
this with you as well,

01:46:45.960 --> 01:46:47.070
there was the mechanic

01:46:47.070 --> 01:46:49.830
where we could use the
leadership score to moderate

01:46:49.830 --> 01:46:51.990
an organisation up and down.

01:46:51.990 --> 01:46:53.370
In this model, we have removed

01:46:53.370 --> 01:46:55.470
that quite formally,

01:46:55.470 --> 01:46:57.120
but we've all now put in a formal way

01:46:57.120 --> 01:46:59.820
of assessing the capability of ICBs

01:46:59.820 --> 01:47:02.370
and providers to give a leadership rating

01:47:02.370 --> 01:47:05.613
for both those organisations
in quite a formalised way.

01:47:06.510 --> 01:47:11.220
We're using the four main purposes of ICBs

01:47:11.220 --> 01:47:12.900
to assess that and we are using

01:47:12.900 --> 01:47:14.730
the Insightful Provider Board model

01:47:14.730 --> 01:47:16.923
to therefore assess providers.

01:47:18.120 --> 01:47:20.370
Finally and really quite important

01:47:20.370 --> 01:47:22.380
for us is there are two things we know

01:47:22.380 --> 01:47:24.360
will happen and come forward.

01:47:24.360 --> 01:47:25.800
One is the 10-year health plan.

01:47:25.800 --> 01:47:28.560
So that will therefore need
some adjustment going forward.

01:47:28.560 --> 01:47:31.260
But also we know there'll be
further reviews coming out

01:47:31.260 --> 01:47:34.740
in short order in relation to
quality and also oversight.

01:47:34.740 --> 01:47:37.560
So it gives the ability to
look at any of the domains

01:47:37.560 --> 01:47:39.900
going forward to therefore get that right.

01:47:39.900 --> 01:47:41.520
So as we do this testing

01:47:41.520 --> 01:47:43.950
and consultation phase,
it gives us the ability

01:47:43.950 --> 01:47:45.900
to make some adjustments along the way

01:47:45.900 --> 01:47:47.820
to therefore get it right at that point.

01:47:47.820 --> 01:47:49.613
And with that, I'll hand back to Steve.

01:47:51.450 --> 01:47:52.410
- I can be super brief

01:47:52.410 --> 01:47:54.661
'cause Adam's just said
what I was going to say.

01:47:54.661 --> 01:47:57.330
So I think the key bit is to say

01:47:57.330 --> 01:47:58.860
what the Board's being asked to do

01:47:58.860 --> 01:48:00.610
is to recognise the environment

01:48:02.040 --> 01:48:06.060
and the context for the changes,
to approve the framework.

01:48:06.060 --> 01:48:09.900
Noting particularly that
it'll be subject to testing

01:48:09.900 --> 01:48:13.440
with the service on plans
as well as more broadly

01:48:13.440 --> 01:48:16.740
and that it'll be subject
to a formal consultation

01:48:16.740 --> 01:48:19.770
and that then a final
framework would be agreed

01:48:19.770 --> 01:48:22.410
by the end of quarter one

01:48:22.410 --> 01:48:26.490
and the first formal segmentation
decisions would be taken

01:48:26.490 --> 01:48:29.880
at that point and then published in July.

01:48:29.880 --> 01:48:32.010
- So I'm going to hold
us and just ask the Board

01:48:32.010 --> 01:48:33.647
if they would support that.

01:48:33.647 --> 01:48:37.353
I mean the key points
co-created, widely consulted on,

01:48:38.220 --> 01:48:39.633
clinically consulted on,

01:48:42.720 --> 01:48:45.387
so autonomy given for strong performance

01:48:45.387 --> 01:48:48.880
and the great NHS phase
of support given to those

01:48:50.070 --> 01:48:52.200
where there are real challenges

01:48:52.200 --> 01:48:53.970
and it's going to be further
consulted on, which is,

01:48:53.970 --> 01:48:55.440
I know is something that you

01:48:55.440 --> 01:48:58.320
and the DH want to think about

01:48:58.320 --> 01:49:01.920
and particularly helpful I
think is that you really engage

01:49:01.920 --> 01:49:03.900
with Penny and Jim on this

01:49:03.900 --> 01:49:07.830
and it can flex going forward
as things might change

01:49:07.830 --> 01:49:10.080
with future policy changes
or other programmes.

01:49:10.080 --> 01:49:11.070
Do you want to say anything on it, Jim?

01:49:11.070 --> 01:49:12.090
- Yeah, just a couple of things.

01:49:12.090 --> 01:49:14.810
So there's a huge amount
of work gone into this.

01:49:14.810 --> 01:49:16.140
So Steve, Adam, Miranda

01:49:16.140 --> 01:49:19.080
or other colleagues been
working on it for a long while

01:49:19.080 --> 01:49:21.540
and this is very complicated.

01:49:21.540 --> 01:49:24.870
So there's a desire out there

01:49:24.870 --> 01:49:27.060
for all of us for this to be as simple

01:49:27.060 --> 01:49:29.310
and as clean as it used
to be in the old days.

01:49:29.310 --> 01:49:31.230
But the world we're in isn't as simple

01:49:31.230 --> 01:49:33.330
and as clean as it was.

01:49:33.330 --> 01:49:35.040
So there'll absolutely
be things that will have

01:49:35.040 --> 01:49:36.870
to tweak and adjust.

01:49:36.870 --> 01:49:40.260
So this consultation
process is really important

01:49:40.260 --> 01:49:42.327
that people actually do come back to us

01:49:42.327 --> 01:49:44.805
and point out the wrinkles
that need to change.

01:49:44.805 --> 01:49:47.970
And then as Adam said, when
the 10-year plans published,

01:49:47.970 --> 01:49:49.470
there'll be a bit of correction there

01:49:49.470 --> 01:49:52.830
as well and we'll get better
with it through the year.

01:49:52.830 --> 01:49:56.400
But you know, let's understand
this isn't something

01:49:56.400 --> 01:49:59.795
that can be perfect at the first go.

01:49:59.795 --> 01:50:01.440
- But clarity will be good.

01:50:01.440 --> 01:50:03.843
So I'm going to move us on.

01:50:05.610 --> 01:50:07.610
- I think it's quite important actually.

01:50:08.518 --> 01:50:09.690
In the mental health, I just wanted

01:50:09.690 --> 01:50:13.470
to ask whether we had thought through this

01:50:13.470 --> 01:50:16.397
discharge within 60 days
being the responsibility

01:50:16.397 --> 01:50:18.510
of the mental health trust

01:50:18.510 --> 01:50:20.730
because the proportion of people,

01:50:20.730 --> 01:50:24.930
those are in mental health
beds in the independent sector

01:50:24.930 --> 01:50:27.060
funded by us that is staying

01:50:27.060 --> 01:50:30.390
for more than 60 days is
actually quite significant.

01:50:30.390 --> 01:50:33.330
And I wonder whether we should
also hold the ICBs to account

01:50:33.330 --> 01:50:35.850
for that because that's
their responsibility

01:50:35.850 --> 01:50:38.460
rather than the mental health trust.

01:50:38.460 --> 01:50:40.683
- So this is page 93 of 147, I think,

01:50:41.803 --> 01:50:43.830
but is that something
you want to respond to?

01:50:43.830 --> 01:50:45.030
- Mary's point's very valid.

01:50:45.030 --> 01:50:46.530
We probably wouldn't change the framework

01:50:46.530 --> 01:50:48.300
because the framework doesn't apply

01:50:48.300 --> 01:50:53.300
to NHS independent
providers of NHS services.

01:50:53.520 --> 01:50:55.080
However, Mary, you are absolutely correct

01:50:55.080 --> 01:50:57.360
that you would expect a good commissioner

01:50:57.360 --> 01:51:01.440
faced with a provider that
had those circumstances,

01:51:01.440 --> 01:51:02.880
if they're an independent
sector organisation

01:51:02.880 --> 01:51:04.560
to be using their commissioning levers.

01:51:04.560 --> 01:51:06.053
Oops, I was hitting his phone,

01:51:06.960 --> 01:51:08.010
to leave a change with that.

01:51:08.010 --> 01:51:11.436
So that's what you would
expect to happen in practise.

01:51:11.436 --> 01:51:14.782
- Great, thank you.
- Is that all right?

01:51:14.782 --> 01:51:15.615
Okay.

01:51:16.762 --> 01:51:18.060
Of course you can.

01:51:18.060 --> 01:51:19.470
- It'll be quick.

01:51:19.470 --> 01:51:22.623
So it's terrific and I think
it's almost comprehensive.

01:51:23.485 --> 01:51:26.187
There's, to my mind, one absence

01:51:26.187 --> 01:51:29.100
and that is, there's
no mention of research,

01:51:29.100 --> 01:51:30.570
there's no mention of research

01:51:30.570 --> 01:51:33.270
and I would say research
is not just a nice to have.

01:51:33.270 --> 01:51:35.670
I think it's in the
constitution, it's good

01:51:35.670 --> 01:51:37.198
for patients to be cared for

01:51:37.198 --> 01:51:38.940
and the researcher to
find better outcomes,

01:51:38.940 --> 01:51:41.070
but it's also key to sustaining innovation

01:51:41.070 --> 01:51:43.343
that we're going to badly need.

01:51:43.343 --> 01:51:45.651
So I just wonder if that could?

01:51:45.651 --> 01:51:48.000
- Richard, are you happy if
I quickly respond to that?

01:51:48.000 --> 01:51:49.833
- Yes.
- Prepared, not happy.

01:51:51.232 --> 01:51:52.623
So Robert,

01:51:53.640 --> 01:51:57.540
one of the domains is
wider economic development

01:51:57.540 --> 01:51:59.910
and social contribution

01:51:59.910 --> 01:52:04.080
and that includes some measures around

01:52:04.080 --> 01:52:07.650
greener responsibilities
and also on research.

01:52:07.650 --> 01:52:11.790
But because of the sort of
nascence of the data flows

01:52:11.790 --> 01:52:15.420
and various other factors,
although they're in the framework

01:52:15.420 --> 01:52:16.560
and we will publish the data,

01:52:16.560 --> 01:52:18.570
it doesn't inform the formal score.

01:52:18.570 --> 01:52:20.670
So it's like a contextual
piece at this point.

01:52:20.670 --> 01:52:23.730
But there is a metric on
research that we agreed

01:52:23.730 --> 01:52:26.430
with Vin's team after the Board
Development Session, sorry,

01:52:26.430 --> 01:52:28.590
the Exec Development Session a while back.

01:52:28.590 --> 01:52:31.834
- So Penny, do you want
to comment on this at all?

01:52:31.834 --> 01:52:32.700
At this stage, I mean Jim?

01:52:32.700 --> 01:52:33.533
- Great start.

01:52:33.533 --> 01:52:35.790
I think it needs a bit
of tweaking, working on

01:52:35.790 --> 01:52:37.440
which you already shared a bit with Steve.

01:52:37.440 --> 01:52:39.153
I think we do that over the next,

01:52:40.170 --> 01:52:41.700
however long the consultation period is.

01:52:41.700 --> 01:52:43.680
I think we can input at that stage.

01:52:43.680 --> 01:52:44.610
- Very good.

01:52:44.610 --> 01:52:45.443
Thank you.

01:52:46.560 --> 01:52:49.803
I'm now going to move us on,
having tried a couple times.

01:52:50.790 --> 01:52:52.563
So cancer programme update.

01:52:53.880 --> 01:52:56.343
Peter, welcome Rob, welcome.

01:52:57.300 --> 01:52:58.133
Are you going to lead in,

01:52:58.133 --> 01:52:59.703
Peter or Emily going to lead in?

01:53:01.464 --> 01:53:03.366
- I'll just say very briefly.

01:53:03.366 --> 01:53:05.340
Yeah, so this is the biannual update

01:53:05.340 --> 01:53:06.720
to the Board on delivery progress

01:53:06.720 --> 01:53:08.403
for the National Cancer Programme.

01:53:09.360 --> 01:53:12.750
It is a summary of the latest
performance progress on,

01:53:12.750 --> 01:53:14.730
for example, the earlier diagnosis agenda

01:53:14.730 --> 01:53:16.110
and some of the national initiatives

01:53:16.110 --> 01:53:18.180
that have been driving improvements.

01:53:18.180 --> 01:53:20.160
The report details some
fantastic achievements,

01:53:20.160 --> 01:53:21.297
which I didn't spoil earlier

01:53:21.297 --> 01:53:24.930
and I won't spoil now so that
the team can talk about them

01:53:24.930 --> 01:53:27.990
with the main result being
more patients being seen

01:53:27.990 --> 01:53:30.540
more quickly and diagnosed earlier

01:53:30.540 --> 01:53:34.050
and focused on the people in
communities who need it most.

01:53:34.050 --> 01:53:36.030
And the paper also outlines the work

01:53:36.030 --> 01:53:37.830
to develop a national cancer plan,

01:53:37.830 --> 01:53:40.830
which is being jointly
developed by NHSE and DHSE

01:53:40.830 --> 01:53:43.170
which will be published
later next this year.

01:53:43.170 --> 01:53:44.580
So over to you guys.

01:53:44.580 --> 01:53:45.480
- Thank you Emily.

01:53:45.480 --> 01:53:46.590
And thank you chair.

01:53:46.590 --> 01:53:47.910
My name's Rob Stones, I'm director

01:53:47.910 --> 01:53:50.070
of delivery for the
National Cancer Programme.

01:53:50.070 --> 01:53:51.900
I'm joined by Professor Johnson,

01:53:51.900 --> 01:53:54.453
who's our National Clinical
Director for Cancer.

01:53:55.350 --> 01:53:57.600
I'll take the paper as read.

01:53:57.600 --> 01:54:00.450
I'll give a brief summary of
the key points in relation

01:54:00.450 --> 01:54:02.520
to operational performance for cancer

01:54:02.520 --> 01:54:04.740
and on early diagnosis.

01:54:04.740 --> 01:54:07.230
Peter will then go on to
describe a bit of the how

01:54:07.230 --> 01:54:11.610
of how we've achieved some of
these areas of improvement.

01:54:11.610 --> 01:54:13.110
I'll start with early diagnosis.

01:54:13.110 --> 01:54:15.420
So after the best part of a decade,

01:54:15.420 --> 01:54:20.370
we've had fairly flat
performance on early diagnosis.

01:54:20.370 --> 01:54:21.420
That is the proportion

01:54:21.420 --> 01:54:25.050
of cancers being diagnosed
at stages one or two.

01:54:25.050 --> 01:54:29.760
It's stayed very constant
at around 55 to 56%.

01:54:29.760 --> 01:54:31.080
We've seen some real

01:54:31.080 --> 01:54:34.410
and steady improvement of
around 2.8 percentage points

01:54:34.410 --> 01:54:38.730
on that since 2019 in
the most recent data.

01:54:38.730 --> 01:54:42.600
So that takes the overall
rate up to around 58.7%.

01:54:42.600 --> 01:54:45.450
And if we convert that into patients,

01:54:45.450 --> 01:54:50.310
that's over 7,300 more patients
per year being diagnosed

01:54:50.310 --> 01:54:52.560
at stage one and two
than before the pandemic.

01:54:52.560 --> 01:54:56.343
So very real impacts on real people.

01:54:57.360 --> 01:54:59.250
Lung cancer screening has been

01:54:59.250 --> 01:55:02.190
a really crucial driver in this,

01:55:02.190 --> 01:55:06.300
that is using mobile
imaging diagnostic capacity,

01:55:06.300 --> 01:55:08.460
IE trucks to target

01:55:08.460 --> 01:55:12.390
lung CT scans at populations
that need them the most,

01:55:12.390 --> 01:55:14.220
as well as helping to drive up

01:55:14.220 --> 01:55:17.280
that overall early diagnosis rate.

01:55:17.280 --> 01:55:20.760
It's been a huge success
story in also being able

01:55:20.760 --> 01:55:22.900
to target some of the most deprived areas

01:55:24.150 --> 01:55:25.230
versus the least deprived.

01:55:25.230 --> 01:55:29.070
And we've seen a narrowing
of the gap in that story.

01:55:29.070 --> 01:55:31.020
So that's been really, really positive.

01:55:32.550 --> 01:55:34.530
Moving on to operational performance then

01:55:34.530 --> 01:55:38.250
for the current financial year
we're in, we set two targets

01:55:38.250 --> 01:55:42.900
for access through 24-25
both to be achieved

01:55:42.900 --> 01:55:47.280
by March, 2025, which we're
nearly at the end of said month.

01:55:47.280 --> 01:55:48.570
The first of those two was

01:55:48.570 --> 01:55:51.600
for the 28 day faster diagnosis standard

01:55:51.600 --> 01:55:55.994
that we would improve performance to 77%.

01:55:55.994 --> 01:55:58.620
And the second was for the 62 day referral

01:55:58.620 --> 01:56:01.770
to first treatment standard,
we set the ambition

01:56:01.770 --> 01:56:04.560
that we'll get to 70% on that.

01:56:04.560 --> 01:56:09.060
We are on track nationally
to hit both of those targets.

01:56:09.060 --> 01:56:13.080
In the third quarter of the
financial year, we were at 77.5%

01:56:13.080 --> 01:56:14.910
for the faster diagnosis standard.

01:56:14.910 --> 01:56:17.932
That's just over 5% higher than last year.

01:56:17.932 --> 01:56:21.900
And on the 62 day target we were at 69.6%.

01:56:21.900 --> 01:56:25.680
So 4.6 percentage points
higher than last year.

01:56:25.680 --> 01:56:28.080
Both of those indicators are subject

01:56:28.080 --> 01:56:31.440
to quite considerable seasonal
variation through the year.

01:56:31.440 --> 01:56:35.130
And while performance tends
to dip typically in January,

01:56:35.130 --> 01:56:37.110
and we see that in the
very latest data we have

01:56:37.110 --> 01:56:40.200
in particular for the
faster diagnosis standard,

01:56:40.200 --> 01:56:42.420
we typically see that very quickly recover

01:56:42.420 --> 01:56:43.470
into February and March.

01:56:43.470 --> 01:56:46.560
So we're seeing the typical
seasonal trends play out here.

01:56:46.560 --> 01:56:50.010
Things like skin cancer has
a very seasonal pattern to it

01:56:50.010 --> 01:56:52.083
as you would expect.

01:56:53.130 --> 01:56:54.960
We've set some further stretch ambitions

01:56:54.960 --> 01:56:56.580
for the coming financial year

01:56:56.580 --> 01:56:58.230
through the operational planning guidance.

01:56:58.230 --> 01:57:02.310
So for 25-26, we have
a new ambition of 80%

01:57:02.310 --> 01:57:03.870
for the faster diagnosis standard

01:57:03.870 --> 01:57:07.770
and 75% for the 62 day standard.

01:57:07.770 --> 01:57:09.270
That's on our path as we hope

01:57:09.270 --> 01:57:11.280
to recover towards full recovery

01:57:11.280 --> 01:57:14.133
of the constitution standards
for cancer patients.

01:57:15.090 --> 01:57:18.900
We have currently 13
trusts who are in tier one

01:57:18.900 --> 01:57:21.390
and our approach to tiering helps us

01:57:21.390 --> 01:57:22.830
to provide enhanced oversight

01:57:22.830 --> 01:57:26.160
and support to the
systems that need it most.

01:57:26.160 --> 01:57:27.900
That's proven to be quite a powerful lever

01:57:27.900 --> 01:57:31.860
for us in driving focused
performance improvement.

01:57:31.860 --> 01:57:34.890
And paragraph five of the
paper demonstrates that.

01:57:34.890 --> 01:57:36.870
Here, I just want to
put on record my thanks

01:57:36.870 --> 01:57:40.320
to the 20 cancer alliances
across the country.

01:57:40.320 --> 01:57:42.210
They do incredible work

01:57:42.210 --> 01:57:43.830
and they really help to provide some of

01:57:43.830 --> 01:57:48.720
that targeted local support to
places that need it the most.

01:57:48.720 --> 01:57:50.460
I'll pass over to Peter
now to describe some

01:57:50.460 --> 01:57:52.110
of our work on the cancer pathways

01:57:52.110 --> 01:57:53.820
and some of the innovation
and as we look forward

01:57:53.820 --> 01:57:56.550
to the cancer plan, as Emily mentioned.

01:57:56.550 --> 01:57:57.990
- Thanks Rob, the Board will recall

01:57:57.990 --> 01:58:01.440
that we've done a lot of
work to try and streamline

01:58:01.440 --> 01:58:02.610
and to increase the efficiency

01:58:02.610 --> 01:58:05.520
and productivity for people

01:58:05.520 --> 01:58:07.950
with urgent suspected cancer referrals.

01:58:07.950 --> 01:58:11.190
And we talked a bit about
this at our last session,

01:58:11.190 --> 01:58:14.790
faecal immunochemical testing
to triage people who present

01:58:14.790 --> 01:58:17.940
with possible bowel cancer
symptoms is something

01:58:17.940 --> 01:58:19.140
which has made a huge difference

01:58:19.140 --> 01:58:21.270
to our colonoscopy capacity.

01:58:21.270 --> 01:58:23.730
We've now got 80% of
those referrals coming

01:58:23.730 --> 01:58:27.000
with a FIT test from a
pretty much a standing start

01:58:27.000 --> 01:58:29.820
a few years ago, which has
been enormously helpful

01:58:29.820 --> 01:58:31.680
in freeing up capacity in that area.

01:58:31.680 --> 01:58:32.820
We're doing similar things

01:58:32.820 --> 01:58:37.650
with tele dermatology using
remote capture of images

01:58:37.650 --> 01:58:40.620
for a review by a
consultant dermatologist.

01:58:40.620 --> 01:58:44.940
We are at just over 40% of
referrals accompanied by an image

01:58:44.940 --> 01:58:47.130
at the moment and we'll
continue to push on that.

01:58:47.130 --> 01:58:49.650
And I think the readout from that has been

01:58:49.650 --> 01:58:52.410
that we saw a much lower
seasonal drop in performance

01:58:52.410 --> 01:58:54.570
with skin cancer than
we'd seen previously.

01:58:54.570 --> 01:58:57.780
The kind of post summer
holiday bump that we always get

01:58:57.780 --> 01:59:00.150
was significantly attenuated this year.

01:59:00.150 --> 01:59:01.950
We've got significant challenges still

01:59:01.950 --> 01:59:03.420
in prostate cancer pathways.

01:59:03.420 --> 01:59:05.370
We're working hard to drive up the rates

01:59:05.370 --> 01:59:08.610
of local anaesthetic outpatient biopsies

01:59:08.610 --> 01:59:11.850
and training up the non-medical
workforce to deliver those.

01:59:11.850 --> 01:59:14.937
We've also got pressures
in gynaecological pathways,

01:59:14.937 --> 01:59:17.070
particularly driven

01:59:17.070 --> 01:59:19.410
by the huge rise in hormone
replacement treatment,

01:59:19.410 --> 01:59:21.780
which results in abnormal bleeding

01:59:21.780 --> 01:59:24.330
in women past the menopause.

01:59:24.330 --> 01:59:26.520
And again, we're working with pathways up

01:59:26.520 --> 01:59:29.310
and down the country to put
in place alternative routes

01:59:29.310 --> 01:59:31.290
to diagnosis and management of people

01:59:31.290 --> 01:59:34.530
with abnormal uterine
bleeding to make sure

01:59:34.530 --> 01:59:36.090
that we can preserve the efficiency

01:59:36.090 --> 01:59:38.370
of the endometrial cancer pathways.

01:59:38.370 --> 01:59:41.190
So a lot of work going
on to improve efficiency.

01:59:41.190 --> 01:59:43.380
But I think the overall message is

01:59:43.380 --> 01:59:45.330
that we are progressively pivoting from

01:59:47.146 --> 01:59:51.300
a continuing increase in
unselected cancer referrals

01:59:51.300 --> 01:59:53.490
where we've seen a 10% year-on-year rise

01:59:53.490 --> 01:59:55.350
over the last decade to trying

01:59:55.350 --> 01:59:57.270
to be more selective in our case finding.

01:59:57.270 --> 01:59:59.011
And the lung cancer screening programme

01:59:59.011 --> 02:00:00.119
is an example of that.

02:00:00.119 --> 02:00:03.420
Other things such as looking
at people at greatest risk

02:00:03.420 --> 02:00:05.550
of liver cancers by screening people

02:00:05.550 --> 02:00:08.160
with fatty liver disease,
alcoholic liver disease,

02:00:08.160 --> 02:00:12.090
viral liver disease, we've
referred 45,000 people

02:00:12.090 --> 02:00:14.730
for fibro scans to look at their risk

02:00:14.730 --> 02:00:16.980
of liver cancer development

02:00:16.980 --> 02:00:19.110
and 3000 of them are now on high risk

02:00:19.110 --> 02:00:21.030
follow up pathways to do that.

02:00:21.030 --> 02:00:24.090
Similarly, trying to
understand hereditary risk.

02:00:24.090 --> 02:00:26.490
Lots of work has already
gone into finding people

02:00:26.490 --> 02:00:28.710
with DNA mismatch repair deficiencies,

02:00:28.710 --> 02:00:30.300
so-called Lynch syndrome,

02:00:30.300 --> 02:00:33.210
where we now have pretty
much every case of bowel

02:00:33.210 --> 02:00:36.540
or endometrial cancer tested
for mismatch repair deficiency

02:00:36.540 --> 02:00:38.187
to enable us to find those kindreds.

02:00:38.187 --> 02:00:41.190
And the database for that
is expanding rapidly,

02:00:41.190 --> 02:00:43.590
similar programmes in detecting

02:00:43.590 --> 02:00:45.240
families with BRCA mutations.

02:00:45.240 --> 02:00:48.120
I think we've sent out 26,000 odd tests

02:00:48.120 --> 02:00:50.280
as part of the case finding programme.

02:00:50.280 --> 02:00:53.640
We've done primarily aimed
at the Jewish population

02:00:53.640 --> 02:00:56.670
and have found an extra 300
people with BRCA mutations

02:00:56.670 --> 02:00:59.430
who were unaware of that,
which again will have

02:00:59.430 --> 02:01:01.533
important consequences for their families.

02:01:03.090 --> 02:01:05.940
In thinking about innovation and returning

02:01:05.940 --> 02:01:09.420
to Richard's point about the
cancer vaccine programme,

02:01:09.420 --> 02:01:12.210
our partnership in the
cancer vaccine launchpad

02:01:12.210 --> 02:01:13.230
has been very effective.

02:01:13.230 --> 02:01:16.650
We've got 55 sites across
the country now potentially

02:01:16.650 --> 02:01:18.240
offering people access to trials,

02:01:18.240 --> 02:01:20.790
which would only historically
have been run in about a dozen

02:01:20.790 --> 02:01:24.390
trial sites as part of the
experimental medicine network.

02:01:24.390 --> 02:01:27.600
So we've gone from covering
around 15% of the population

02:01:27.600 --> 02:01:29.730
with access to trials
of this type of thing

02:01:29.730 --> 02:01:33.630
to somewhere north of 60%
as things currently stand.

02:01:33.630 --> 02:01:36.840
And the consequence of that
is that the first trial

02:01:36.840 --> 02:01:39.030
we supported through this in bowel cancer,

02:01:39.030 --> 02:01:42.360
adjuvant vaccination closed
about six months earlier

02:01:42.360 --> 02:01:44.310
than anybody had anticipated

02:01:44.310 --> 02:01:47.240
because the UK was recruiting
faster than the rest

02:01:47.240 --> 02:01:49.890
of the world put together
to that particular trial.

02:01:49.890 --> 02:01:52.980
And the consequence of that
is that we are now attracting

02:01:52.980 --> 02:01:56.100
a great deal more interest
from different companies

02:01:56.100 --> 02:01:57.990
with personalised vaccines to test.

02:01:57.990 --> 02:02:00.660
We've got another two trials
opening in the next couple

02:02:00.660 --> 02:02:03.270
of months and a progressively
expanding portfolio,

02:02:03.270 --> 02:02:07.170
which I think is very important
not only for the development

02:02:07.170 --> 02:02:10.050
of research, but also
for the setup of business

02:02:10.050 --> 02:02:11.760
as usual type pathways to support

02:02:11.760 --> 02:02:13.980
these kind of innovative treatments.

02:02:13.980 --> 02:02:16.230
And very importantly
for the penumbral effect

02:02:16.230 --> 02:02:20.100
that it's had on bringing
together our histopathology

02:02:20.100 --> 02:02:22.380
and genomic services to
streamline that process,

02:02:22.380 --> 02:02:25.380
which has historically been
something of a bottleneck

02:02:25.380 --> 02:02:26.613
for genomics.

02:02:27.570 --> 02:02:30.660
The work on the cancer plan
is actually extremely helpful.

02:02:30.660 --> 02:02:33.090
The close working that we've got now

02:02:33.090 --> 02:02:34.470
with the Department of Health team,

02:02:34.470 --> 02:02:37.680
we've set up the various
structures very much jointly

02:02:37.680 --> 02:02:39.090
to make sure that we're delivering that.

02:02:39.090 --> 02:02:40.800
And the great majority of clinical advice

02:02:40.800 --> 02:02:43.917
to the Cancer Plan will come from

02:02:43.917 --> 02:02:47.193
our NHS England Clinical Advisory Groups,

02:02:48.150 --> 02:02:50.250
a big focus obviously on the strategies

02:02:50.250 --> 02:02:53.490
which will most improve
survival from cancers.

02:02:53.490 --> 02:02:54.540
And we anticipate

02:02:54.540 --> 02:02:57.690
that there will be a continued
focus on earlier diagnosis,

02:02:57.690 --> 02:02:59.550
more effective pathways to diagnosis

02:02:59.550 --> 02:03:01.620
and innovative treatment.

02:03:01.620 --> 02:03:04.620
And I think in thinking
about that, we are anxious

02:03:04.620 --> 02:03:07.770
to make sure that we retain a degree

02:03:07.770 --> 02:03:11.760
of system facing national
oversight for the process

02:03:11.760 --> 02:03:14.520
linked to the very successful
work of the cancer alliances.

02:03:14.520 --> 02:03:16.740
And a lot of the things
that we've talked about

02:03:16.740 --> 02:03:18.960
have critically depended on the presence

02:03:18.960 --> 02:03:21.240
of locality based cancer alliances

02:03:21.240 --> 02:03:22.680
who understand their populations

02:03:22.680 --> 02:03:25.860
and who are capable of enacting
both the national programmes

02:03:25.860 --> 02:03:29.103
and local initiatives to drive
up performance and delivery.

02:03:30.442 --> 02:03:33.090
So those, I think will be key
priorities for our development

02:03:33.090 --> 02:03:35.340
of the cancer plan as it goes ahead

02:03:35.340 --> 02:03:37.320
and in the year ahead,
obviously we will continue

02:03:37.320 --> 02:03:39.420
to focus on driving up the performances

02:03:39.420 --> 02:03:41.340
as Rob has outlined back towards

02:03:41.340 --> 02:03:44.703
the constitutional standards
and to continue stretching

02:03:44.703 --> 02:03:46.980
the faster diagnosis standard
where we've already gone above

02:03:46.980 --> 02:03:50.520
what we set as our baseline
and we can see that in order

02:03:50.520 --> 02:03:53.310
to meet the 62 day wait standard,
we will need to continue

02:03:53.310 --> 02:03:57.094
to push harder on that to squeeze
down the diagnostic times.

02:03:57.094 --> 02:03:58.650
So we will continue to do that.

02:03:58.650 --> 02:04:00.210
We will continue to deliver the programmes

02:04:00.210 --> 02:04:02.035
and very importantly the rollout of the

02:04:02.035 --> 02:04:03.343
lung cancer programme,

02:04:03.343 --> 02:04:06.840
which is now just under 40%
of the population covered.

02:04:06.840 --> 02:04:09.390
And we need to continue to accelerate

02:04:09.390 --> 02:04:11.310
and expand that in
order to get the benefit

02:04:11.310 --> 02:04:13.380
that we've already seen as we've started.

02:04:13.380 --> 02:04:15.060
And of course to continue to drive some

02:04:15.060 --> 02:04:16.320
of these innovative programmes.

02:04:16.320 --> 02:04:17.850
And we've talked previously about

02:04:17.850 --> 02:04:19.860
some of the diagnostic innovations

02:04:19.860 --> 02:04:21.810
and we're waiting to see,
for example, a result

02:04:21.810 --> 02:04:26.237
of the big multi cancer
early detection test study

02:04:26.237 --> 02:04:28.920
which will emerge in
the early part of 2026.

02:04:30.591 --> 02:04:31.424
- That's very good.

02:04:31.424 --> 02:04:34.350
So let me open to, so I've got Andrew

02:04:34.350 --> 02:04:36.930
and yeah, and Duncan,
Andrew first and Duncan.

02:04:36.930 --> 02:04:39.570
- Fantastic progress, really is fantastic

02:04:39.570 --> 02:04:42.510
and it's great to see some step change

02:04:42.510 --> 02:04:46.800
in terms of improvement,
on the 62 day standard

02:04:46.800 --> 02:04:50.640
though, Peter, what's
the big constraint now?

02:04:50.640 --> 02:04:54.330
Is it physical bed
capacity or is it manpower

02:04:54.330 --> 02:04:56.430
or is it a combination?

02:04:56.430 --> 02:04:58.410
Just to understand, what are the things

02:04:58.410 --> 02:05:01.800
that you are doing to lift a further 5%

02:05:01.800 --> 02:05:03.968
improvement in performance?

02:05:03.968 --> 02:05:04.801
- And we're going to
do the normal technique

02:05:04.801 --> 02:05:06.420
of batching up the
questions if that's alright.

02:05:06.420 --> 02:05:09.073
And then so then I think
it was Duncan then Jane.

02:05:09.073 --> 02:05:10.569
I think Duncan?

02:05:10.569 --> 02:05:12.480
- Yeah look some amazing stuff in here.

02:05:12.480 --> 02:05:14.710
I spent a bit of time in the Maggie Centre

02:05:16.033 --> 02:05:18.480
in Charing Cross a couple of months ago

02:05:18.480 --> 02:05:21.720
and I was meeting with women who had gone

02:05:21.720 --> 02:05:23.100
through crash menopause as a result

02:05:23.100 --> 02:05:24.090
of their cancer treatment.

02:05:24.090 --> 02:05:26.250
And the thing that really
struck me was how important

02:05:26.250 --> 02:05:29.970
that relationship with
clinical nurse specialists was,

02:05:29.970 --> 02:05:34.020
but also that peer support
from other patients.

02:05:34.020 --> 02:05:38.460
And I just wondered whether in
your prioritisation of things

02:05:38.460 --> 02:05:42.540
for the coming plan areas
around improving that experience

02:05:42.540 --> 02:05:45.300
and then also thoughts on the
importance of the workforce

02:05:45.300 --> 02:05:48.360
'cause there is a whole, as
you well know, army of people

02:05:48.360 --> 02:05:53.360
that are there for patients
at all hours of the day

02:05:54.810 --> 02:05:56.340
at some of the most difficult
times of their life.

02:05:56.340 --> 02:05:58.290
And just, you know, in the midst of all

02:05:58.290 --> 02:05:59.820
of these brilliant pieces of work.

02:05:59.820 --> 02:06:01.293
It's just, that is the thing

02:06:01.293 --> 02:06:04.830
that often people talk about
in terms of their experience.

02:06:04.830 --> 02:06:06.090
- So I'm going to quickly gather,

02:06:06.090 --> 02:06:08.035
there are three more questions coming

02:06:08.035 --> 02:06:09.120
and then the interest of
time you'll be batching

02:06:09.120 --> 02:06:11.483
and then allocating.

02:06:11.483 --> 02:06:13.470
So it was Jane first and then Robert

02:06:13.470 --> 02:06:15.390
and then Julian.

02:06:15.390 --> 02:06:16.223
- Thank you chair.

02:06:16.223 --> 02:06:18.930
And I refer to my declaration
of interest as a trustee

02:06:18.930 --> 02:06:20.880
of Action on Smoking and Health.

02:06:20.880 --> 02:06:24.510
So I guess that with the
age of sale legislation,

02:06:24.510 --> 02:06:26.730
you know, on its way through parliament,

02:06:26.730 --> 02:06:28.230
there's a feeling I think from a lot

02:06:28.230 --> 02:06:31.470
of the sort of people who've worked to

02:06:31.470 --> 02:06:33.540
sort of decrease smoking over the years,

02:06:33.540 --> 02:06:34.800
that there'll be a sense

02:06:34.800 --> 02:06:36.690
of a lot of people think job done,

02:06:36.690 --> 02:06:38.820
but actually the sense is,

02:06:38.820 --> 02:06:41.100
I think rightly people think we will see

02:06:41.100 --> 02:06:43.710
that greater concentration,
particularly in areas

02:06:43.710 --> 02:06:46.350
that have already got a
lot of health inequalities

02:06:46.350 --> 02:06:47.730
and disadvantaged communities.

02:06:47.730 --> 02:06:50.190
So I just wondered if you
think in the years ahead

02:06:50.190 --> 02:06:52.890
there's more opportunity
to join up the NHS

02:06:52.890 --> 02:06:57.330
around the prevention agenda
in as much as sort of a lot

02:06:57.330 --> 02:07:00.510
of the people will be people
as you're already showing,

02:07:00.510 --> 02:07:02.190
who if you like, can be identified

02:07:02.190 --> 02:07:03.990
by some of those other risk factors.

02:07:05.580 --> 02:07:07.140
- Do you mind if I keep
batching up for you?

02:07:07.140 --> 02:07:09.870
Well, I'm not batching you,
Robert and then Julian.

02:07:09.870 --> 02:07:12.277
- Yeah, no, great paper

02:07:12.277 --> 02:07:13.320
and lots of terrific stuff happening.

02:07:13.320 --> 02:07:14.734
And you refer to innovation

02:07:14.734 --> 02:07:17.130
and you talked about the
cancer vaccine Peter,

02:07:17.130 --> 02:07:19.260
which is really very exciting.

02:07:19.260 --> 02:07:21.720
You are well aware there
are other innovations

02:07:21.720 --> 02:07:22.950
and checkpoint inhibitors

02:07:22.950 --> 02:07:24.900
and CAR T cells.

02:07:24.900 --> 02:07:27.570
CAR T cells purely in trials
at the moment I think,

02:07:27.570 --> 02:07:29.370
checkpoint inhibitors licenced

02:07:29.370 --> 02:07:31.560
for use in melanoma only.

02:07:31.560 --> 02:07:34.440
Is there an affordability
challenge coming our way

02:07:34.440 --> 02:07:37.023
if more of these things
get approved for wider use?

02:07:39.720 --> 02:07:41.760
- I think our cancer programme is one

02:07:41.760 --> 02:07:44.850
where we should really
learn some lessons about

02:07:44.850 --> 02:07:47.430
how you can make some change happen

02:07:47.430 --> 02:07:49.260
and change that people
have tried to deliver

02:07:49.260 --> 02:07:52.170
and haven't delivered actually
for quite a long time.

02:07:52.170 --> 02:07:55.080
And the early cancer diagnosis
is a really good example

02:07:55.080 --> 02:07:58.650
and it's where targeted funding,

02:07:58.650 --> 02:08:02.850
nationally targeted funding
can really make a difference,

02:08:02.850 --> 02:08:07.440
carefully directed,
well programme managed.

02:08:07.440 --> 02:08:10.860
Two, where, you know, how you learn,

02:08:10.860 --> 02:08:13.020
how you take innovation within the NHS

02:08:13.020 --> 02:08:16.590
and actually the FIT test I
think is a really good example,

02:08:16.590 --> 02:08:18.120
but it's national impetus

02:08:18.120 --> 02:08:20.910
that gets it scaled and rolled out.

02:08:20.910 --> 02:08:23.370
And that like a simple thing
that's made a huge difference

02:08:23.370 --> 02:08:28.140
as Peter's said, to, you
know, broader capacity

02:08:28.140 --> 02:08:30.030
and were well placed rules.

02:08:30.030 --> 02:08:32.220
I mean we have hit the
faster diagnosis standard,

02:08:32.220 --> 02:08:35.490
which we were struggling
to do pre-pandemic

02:08:35.490 --> 02:08:37.290
and it's where some of
the additional investment,

02:08:37.290 --> 02:08:39.240
but the kind of rules we have set,

02:08:39.240 --> 02:08:41.198
I think have made a difference.

02:08:41.198 --> 02:08:46.198
And I do think, you know,
it's easy to get the argument

02:08:46.350 --> 02:08:47.790
don't ring-fence anything,

02:08:47.790 --> 02:08:49.680
careful how much national direction.

02:08:49.680 --> 02:08:52.770
But I think there are some
lessons to be learned,

02:08:52.770 --> 02:08:56.640
which carefully applied
can make a huge difference

02:08:56.640 --> 02:08:59.070
as we think about how we
deliver the 10-year plan.

02:08:59.070 --> 02:09:00.240
- So you've got the question.

02:09:00.240 --> 02:09:02.243
Emily, do you want to come in either?

02:09:02.243 --> 02:09:05.430
- Just very briefly I
would've said some of

02:09:05.430 --> 02:09:06.960
what Julian said, but he
said it much better than me.

02:09:06.960 --> 02:09:08.490
The thing I wanted to mention,
which I'm sure the team

02:09:08.490 --> 02:09:10.200
would've done, is to recognise the work

02:09:10.200 --> 02:09:12.030
of Cally Palmer in delivery.

02:09:12.030 --> 02:09:14.070
So Cally's just stepped
away from this role

02:09:14.070 --> 02:09:17.850
after 10 years and we were at
her leaving drinks last night

02:09:17.850 --> 02:09:20.250
talking about the extraordinary
impact that she's had

02:09:20.250 --> 02:09:21.750
in leading and spearheading that.

02:09:21.750 --> 02:09:24.000
And I wanted to recognise that
as part of this discussion.

02:09:24.000 --> 02:09:25.440
- No, absolutely.

02:09:25.440 --> 02:09:28.414
Thank you, I was going to
come to Cally at the end,

02:09:28.414 --> 02:09:29.247
but fantastic.

02:09:29.247 --> 02:09:30.080
Thank you.

02:09:30.924 --> 02:09:32.700
And thank you Julian for the comments.

02:09:32.700 --> 02:09:35.520
I think whilst ring-fenced funding

02:09:35.520 --> 02:09:37.650
is clearly problematic in many ways,

02:09:37.650 --> 02:09:39.390
actually having a cadre of people,

02:09:39.390 --> 02:09:41.340
particularly at local level

02:09:41.340 --> 02:09:43.860
who get out of bed each day working out

02:09:43.860 --> 02:09:45.390
how you deliver better cancer services,

02:09:45.390 --> 02:09:46.467
how you deliver better performance.

02:09:46.467 --> 02:09:49.560
And one of the things that we
did in the last few years was

02:09:49.560 --> 02:09:54.030
to switch alliances from being
what I previously referred to

02:09:54.030 --> 02:09:57.660
as gloriously strategic to
focusing more on delivery

02:09:57.660 --> 02:10:00.030
and performance has had a
huge impact on the ground

02:10:00.030 --> 02:10:01.350
because they understand their providers

02:10:01.350 --> 02:10:03.000
and they understand their populations

02:10:03.000 --> 02:10:05.820
and also of course they can
put in some targeted funding

02:10:05.820 --> 02:10:06.990
to the bottlenecks.

02:10:06.990 --> 02:10:09.090
So I think that has been very important.

02:10:09.090 --> 02:10:11.940
It also speaks to Duncan's point

02:10:11.940 --> 02:10:13.290
about the patient experience.

02:10:13.290 --> 02:10:16.350
And again, the alliance is on
a huge amount of development.

02:10:16.350 --> 02:10:18.210
The ACCEND programme for the training

02:10:18.210 --> 02:10:21.390
of clinical nurse specialists
has been really important

02:10:21.390 --> 02:10:23.970
to make sure that we continue
to grow that cadre of people

02:10:23.970 --> 02:10:27.390
where we see quite high
turnover, people come in train

02:10:27.390 --> 02:10:29.310
and then move on to other
things in their lives.

02:10:29.310 --> 02:10:32.670
But it's really important that
we continue to develop that

02:10:32.670 --> 02:10:33.900
because you're absolutely right.

02:10:33.900 --> 02:10:36.090
It's the thing that patients
notice most of all is

02:10:36.090 --> 02:10:38.940
the clinical nurse
specialist to whom they talk,

02:10:38.940 --> 02:10:40.140
who deliver their chemotherapy

02:10:40.140 --> 02:10:42.750
and who do all the other
things that they do.

02:10:42.750 --> 02:10:44.520
So really important and living with

02:10:44.520 --> 02:10:45.870
and beyond cancer is clearly going

02:10:45.870 --> 02:10:48.720
to be an important
component of the thinking

02:10:48.720 --> 02:10:50.040
and the cancer plan.

02:10:50.040 --> 02:10:52.320
The difficulty, of course
is to make it strategic

02:10:52.320 --> 02:10:56.160
and effective and not try
to do absolutely everything

02:10:56.160 --> 02:10:57.063
with the plan.

02:10:59.070 --> 02:11:02.340
To Andrew's point about
the 62 day wait question,

02:11:02.340 --> 02:11:04.170
the investment in diagnostics has clearly

02:11:04.170 --> 02:11:05.970
been hugely important to helping us

02:11:05.970 --> 02:11:08.520
to deliver the faster diagnosis standard.

02:11:08.520 --> 02:11:12.873
And squeezing down diagnostic
time has helped enormously.

02:11:13.950 --> 02:11:16.140
The bit that we are now
seeing is the next point

02:11:16.140 --> 02:11:16.973
in the chain,

02:11:16.973 --> 02:11:18.150
which is the time to treatment.

02:11:18.150 --> 02:11:21.810
And particularly what we
see is a stretching out

02:11:21.810 --> 02:11:25.020
of operating time for cancer surgery,

02:11:25.020 --> 02:11:26.280
which of course is about half

02:11:26.280 --> 02:11:28.770
of all initial episodes of treatment.

02:11:28.770 --> 02:11:31.950
And there is a tension
between elective recovery

02:11:31.950 --> 02:11:33.570
and use of operating theatres for that

02:11:33.570 --> 02:11:35.970
and getting the cancer operations done.

02:11:35.970 --> 02:11:37.649
We are starting to see

02:11:37.649 --> 02:11:39.600
some helpfulness from the surgical hubs

02:11:39.600 --> 02:11:42.510
which do a relatively small
amount of cancer operating.

02:11:42.510 --> 02:11:45.870
They mostly only do skin and
breast cancer operations.

02:11:45.870 --> 02:11:49.350
But what that does do is free
up time in the acute units

02:11:49.350 --> 02:11:51.810
to get more cancer operations done.

02:11:51.810 --> 02:11:53.820
And that's again going to thinking about

02:11:53.820 --> 02:11:55.530
how we improve theatre productivity

02:11:55.530 --> 02:11:58.560
for cancer surgery is
going to be important.

02:11:58.560 --> 02:12:00.060
Radiotherapy is the other area

02:12:00.060 --> 02:12:03.510
where we've seen waiting
time start to edge up.

02:12:03.510 --> 02:12:06.417
And again the renewal of the
Linear Accelerated programme

02:12:06.417 --> 02:12:08.880
and we've put significant
capital investment

02:12:08.880 --> 02:12:10.740
into that recently and plans to continue

02:12:10.740 --> 02:12:12.840
to do more is going to be really important

02:12:12.840 --> 02:12:14.400
to continue to do that.

02:12:14.400 --> 02:12:19.400
We are hoping to implement AI contouring

02:12:19.530 --> 02:12:21.480
to speed up radiotherapy planning

02:12:21.480 --> 02:12:24.330
because as radiotherapy
becomes more sophisticated,

02:12:24.330 --> 02:12:26.580
you need longer time in medical physics

02:12:26.580 --> 02:12:29.250
and clinical oncologists
to plan the fields.

02:12:29.250 --> 02:12:33.060
Having AI to do that
significantly shortens the time

02:12:33.060 --> 02:12:36.450
and frees up specialist time to do more.

02:12:36.450 --> 02:12:38.253
The budget challenges have
meant that we haven't rolled

02:12:38.253 --> 02:12:41.640
that out as fast as we
had previously intended,

02:12:41.640 --> 02:12:44.460
but nonetheless, we've
got suppliers lined up

02:12:44.460 --> 02:12:46.620
to help us with that and
we'll continue to try

02:12:46.620 --> 02:12:48.270
and push on that during the year.

02:12:49.200 --> 02:12:52.500
Prevention absolutely is
going to be critical again

02:12:52.500 --> 02:12:53.997
to the Cancer Plan and one of

02:12:53.997 --> 02:12:57.900
the non NHS England
domains of the Cancer Plan

02:12:57.900 --> 02:12:59.970
where there is a specific work stream

02:12:59.970 --> 02:13:02.190
and is in prevention, really interesting

02:13:02.190 --> 02:13:05.850
to see recently some projected
reductions in mortality

02:13:05.850 --> 02:13:08.520
where it looks as though the UK's

02:13:08.520 --> 02:13:11.610
projected mortality reductions
from cancer are going faster

02:13:11.610 --> 02:13:13.530
than other European countries.

02:13:13.530 --> 02:13:15.240
And I think a lot of
that probably has to do

02:13:15.240 --> 02:13:18.030
with the faster change in
smoking habits that we've seen

02:13:18.030 --> 02:13:21.173
as a consequence of previous legislation

02:13:21.173 --> 02:13:22.800
and things that have
been done in that area.

02:13:22.800 --> 02:13:24.450
But very encouraging to see that.

02:13:25.343 --> 02:13:28.380
And finally, yes, in terms of delivery

02:13:28.380 --> 02:13:30.000
of sophisticated therapeutics,

02:13:30.000 --> 02:13:31.800
CAR T cells are now in routine use

02:13:31.800 --> 02:13:34.650
for acute lymphoblastic leukaemia

02:13:34.650 --> 02:13:38.100
and high grade B cell
lymphomas on a routine basis

02:13:38.100 --> 02:13:40.620
and in fact are coming forward
in the treatment algorithm

02:13:40.620 --> 02:13:41.970
for high grade lymphoma.

02:13:41.970 --> 02:13:44.310
So they're being used
in first recurrence now.

02:13:44.310 --> 02:13:47.550
So significant pressures of delivery.

02:13:47.550 --> 02:13:49.770
The next thing coming down
the line is CAR T cells

02:13:49.770 --> 02:13:52.650
for multiple myeloma where the prevalence

02:13:52.650 --> 02:13:53.483
is very much higher

02:13:53.483 --> 02:13:55.140
because there are many lines of treatment

02:13:55.140 --> 02:13:57.930
and we're examining how
we continue to expand

02:13:57.930 --> 02:14:02.280
that moving to more ambulatory
models of care experience

02:14:02.280 --> 02:14:04.050
with those technologies increases

02:14:04.050 --> 02:14:07.200
and checkpoint inhibitors
similarly, we've got a huge number

02:14:07.200 --> 02:14:09.480
of indications for the use
of checkpoint inhibitors now

02:14:09.480 --> 02:14:11.760
in multiple different cancer types.

02:14:11.760 --> 02:14:14.460
The challenge there is the uncommon

02:14:14.460 --> 02:14:16.620
but important toxicities that you see

02:14:16.620 --> 02:14:17.970
and what we are in the process

02:14:17.970 --> 02:14:20.040
of doing is setting up
a network of expertise

02:14:20.040 --> 02:14:22.650
around the country so that when you get

02:14:22.650 --> 02:14:26.250
your autoimmune enteritis or thyroiditis

02:14:26.250 --> 02:14:29.130
or pituitary failure,
you've got the relevant team

02:14:29.130 --> 02:14:31.470
that can manage it without
you necessarily having

02:14:31.470 --> 02:14:33.900
to travel a long way to get that support.

02:14:33.900 --> 02:14:35.820
So again, lots of work to do

02:14:35.820 --> 02:14:38.463
to make sure we put the
systems around the patient.

02:14:39.570 --> 02:14:42.600
- So I'm going to close this item off.

02:14:42.600 --> 02:14:46.410
I mean, whenever you come to the Board,

02:14:46.410 --> 02:14:47.700
I mean Cally will come to the Board

02:14:47.700 --> 02:14:51.420
and Rob, it's always an extremely,

02:14:51.420 --> 02:14:55.240
I would say rich topic where
we see amazing progress

02:14:56.130 --> 02:15:01.130
but battling a huge
scourge on our population.

02:15:01.830 --> 02:15:04.320
But actually the innovation
that you talk about

02:15:04.320 --> 02:15:06.420
as well as actually it is innovation

02:15:06.420 --> 02:15:09.420
but it's the rollout of
things like the lung cancer,

02:15:09.420 --> 02:15:12.660
the lung trucks remarkable
and the FIT tests

02:15:12.660 --> 02:15:15.090
and the cancer alliances.

02:15:15.090 --> 02:15:18.300
You know, there's huge
amounts of progress being made

02:15:18.300 --> 02:15:21.720
but we're continuously battling
this rising demand I think.

02:15:21.720 --> 02:15:23.247
But thank you very much

02:15:23.247 --> 02:15:25.530
for a very good presentation

02:15:25.530 --> 02:15:28.110
and conversation, Emily,
thank you very much.

02:15:28.110 --> 02:15:30.900
So moving to the last
item for which there is

02:15:30.900 --> 02:15:34.140
a sort of a discussion paper
being as always, Vin, you know

02:15:34.140 --> 02:15:35.940
that if you go last you get squeezed

02:15:37.213 --> 02:15:38.940
and anyways data digital and technology

02:15:38.940 --> 02:15:40.320
is a very good paper you've provided

02:15:40.320 --> 02:15:42.330
but you want to give us the highlights.

02:15:42.330 --> 02:15:44.180
- Yeah, thank you chair.

02:15:44.180 --> 02:15:46.710
So I'll just say one
word a bit, a sentence

02:15:46.710 --> 02:15:49.530
for introduction and then
just make three key points.

02:15:49.530 --> 02:15:52.620
So first can I just say it's
delight to be in Leeds today,

02:15:52.620 --> 02:15:54.540
not just 'cause Leeds is a great city

02:15:54.540 --> 02:15:58.500
but 2/3 of our digital staff
work in our Leeds office.

02:15:58.500 --> 02:16:00.950
And Richard, I was really
pleased to hear you say

02:16:00.950 --> 02:16:03.810
at the beginning, we manage the data

02:16:03.810 --> 02:16:05.010
with our national digital

02:16:05.010 --> 02:16:07.050
and data services

02:16:07.050 --> 02:16:10.527
for more customers than Amazon UK has

02:16:10.527 --> 02:16:12.660
and more subscribers than Netflix has.

02:16:12.660 --> 02:16:14.580
So we're a very big provider

02:16:14.580 --> 02:16:17.150
of digital services and
that's really important.

02:16:17.150 --> 02:16:20.010
So I think there's just three
key points I want to make.

02:16:20.010 --> 02:16:23.880
So number one is that we
all know digital maturity

02:16:23.880 --> 02:16:28.350
in the NHS lags behind most
other developed health systems

02:16:28.350 --> 02:16:30.990
by quite some considerable margin.

02:16:30.990 --> 02:16:34.170
11% of our staff say,
only 11% say they have

02:16:34.170 --> 02:16:36.150
the basic tools to do their job.

02:16:36.150 --> 02:16:38.850
And 70% of our staff say

02:16:38.850 --> 02:16:42.840
that our systems do not
interoperate or talk to each other.

02:16:42.840 --> 02:16:46.500
And that means is a
prerequisite for delivering

02:16:46.500 --> 02:16:49.950
the kind of neighbourhood
care that we've described.

02:16:49.950 --> 02:16:52.200
And digital maturity and
community services is worse

02:16:52.200 --> 02:16:54.000
than it is in the acute sector.

02:16:54.000 --> 02:16:56.691
And secondly,

02:16:56.691 --> 02:17:00.270
we've seen from recent reports
just your basic experience

02:17:00.270 --> 02:17:01.950
as a patient when you try

02:17:01.950 --> 02:17:05.100
and find out what's happened
when you ring up a service

02:17:05.100 --> 02:17:06.510
and you've been referred by somebody else

02:17:06.510 --> 02:17:08.610
and nope, somebody can't talk
to somebody else's system.

02:17:08.610 --> 02:17:10.740
So we've got to sort that out.

02:17:10.740 --> 02:17:12.240
So I think secondly the changes

02:17:12.240 --> 02:17:14.010
that we've described
in this paper, which is

02:17:14.010 --> 02:17:17.400
what we've been doing in
recent years are fantastic,

02:17:17.400 --> 02:17:20.310
but they're necessary but
they're by no means enough.

02:17:20.310 --> 02:17:22.350
And so looking forward,

02:17:22.350 --> 02:17:25.530
fixing the digital infrastructure
is really fantastic

02:17:25.530 --> 02:17:28.623
that by the end of next
year every trust in England

02:17:28.623 --> 02:17:30.810
will have an electronic patient record.

02:17:30.810 --> 02:17:31.770
But in the next phase,

02:17:31.770 --> 02:17:33.873
we've got to focus on interoperability.

02:17:34.980 --> 02:17:39.000
The second bit is
fantastic that we procured

02:17:39.000 --> 02:17:43.020
as NHS England in 2023, the
federated data platform.

02:17:43.020 --> 02:17:46.440
In the first wave of trusts
that we've implemented that in,

02:17:46.440 --> 02:17:50.010
we're seeing an extra just over
a hundred patients per month

02:17:50.010 --> 02:17:52.830
being treated where they've
implemented the surgical model.

02:17:52.830 --> 02:17:54.750
And of course for the next
year we're going to crack on

02:17:54.750 --> 02:17:57.990
and continue to roll out
the federated data platform

02:17:57.990 --> 02:18:00.960
and most importantly to
actually start testing

02:18:00.960 --> 02:18:02.550
new innovations that we can develop

02:18:02.550 --> 02:18:04.680
in the federated data platform

02:18:04.680 --> 02:18:07.200
with other suppliers
that we can then evaluate

02:18:07.200 --> 02:18:09.930
and then spread across
the rest of the network.

02:18:09.930 --> 02:18:12.150
For the NHS app,

02:18:12.150 --> 02:18:15.090
and you'll see in the paper
we've not just implemented

02:18:15.090 --> 02:18:18.150
digital telephony and digital
triage in primary care,

02:18:18.150 --> 02:18:21.060
but you can now request an
appointment through the NHS app.

02:18:21.060 --> 02:18:24.060
But in the next phase, everyone
complains about the fact

02:18:24.060 --> 02:18:27.600
they get a paper letter
as well as a text message

02:18:27.600 --> 02:18:29.640
from their supplier.

02:18:29.640 --> 02:18:31.800
The NHS app will send you a message free

02:18:31.800 --> 02:18:33.900
if you turn the notifications on.

02:18:33.900 --> 02:18:36.930
And that means we could send
36 million fewer letters

02:18:36.930 --> 02:18:39.600
in the NHS if everybody
turned the notification on.

02:18:39.600 --> 02:18:41.940
And that's why in our planning
guidance we've asked all

02:18:41.940 --> 02:18:44.580
organisations to start asking
patients if they'd like

02:18:44.580 --> 02:18:46.740
to receive app-based messaging alone.

02:18:46.740 --> 02:18:48.991
And we'll be pushing on that next year.

02:18:48.991 --> 02:18:51.390
- So I don't get multiple
letters as well as that.

02:18:51.390 --> 02:18:52.950
- You have to do both,
yeah, obviously there are

02:18:52.950 --> 02:18:55.770
some situations where members
of the public may prefer

02:18:55.770 --> 02:18:57.210
or for various reasons reserve paper,

02:18:57.210 --> 02:18:58.770
but that's why we're asking people first

02:18:58.770 --> 02:19:00.570
if that's what they'd like to do.

02:19:00.570 --> 02:19:04.320
And then finally we nationally
did a deal with Microsoft

02:19:04.320 --> 02:19:06.930
for the Microsoft 365 platform.

02:19:06.930 --> 02:19:08.517
It saved the NHS millions of pounds

02:19:08.517 --> 02:19:11.970
and the focus in the next year
is going to be on maximising

02:19:11.970 --> 02:19:13.620
the benefit of that national deal

02:19:13.620 --> 02:19:16.593
so that everyone adopts
all the features in that.

02:19:17.880 --> 02:19:21.330
So just a final point I think
I wanted to make is that,

02:19:21.330 --> 02:19:23.873
I mean you'll see from
announcements in recent days,

02:19:23.873 --> 02:19:26.400
I think the government shares our ambition

02:19:26.400 --> 02:19:27.930
for digital technology

02:19:27.930 --> 02:19:31.320
and for the digital
professions across government,

02:19:31.320 --> 02:19:33.060
particularly in the NHS,

02:19:33.060 --> 02:19:35.760
we're having very productive
conversations with government.

02:19:35.760 --> 02:19:37.020
I think it's not so much a question

02:19:37.020 --> 02:19:39.930
of whether government wishes
to invest in technology

02:19:39.930 --> 02:19:42.210
going forward into the spending
review, but just how much

02:19:42.210 --> 02:19:44.820
and how much can the NHS
actually go forward and do it.

02:19:44.820 --> 02:19:47.820
And I think that's great to
have that vote of confidence.

02:19:47.820 --> 02:19:49.186
Those are going to be key I think,

02:19:49.186 --> 02:19:50.202
to delivering productivity,

02:19:50.202 --> 02:19:52.830
to delivering performance and
getting a grip on the money.

02:19:52.830 --> 02:19:55.020
All the things that we spent
this Board talking about.

02:19:55.020 --> 02:19:57.690
And I feel certainly really
positive about the future,

02:19:57.690 --> 02:19:59.250
about what we can achieve with technology.

02:19:59.250 --> 02:20:01.740
- So thank you for the oral presentation.

02:20:01.740 --> 02:20:03.810
And actually also for the deck, thank you.

02:20:03.810 --> 02:20:05.070
I'm going to open up the comments.

02:20:05.070 --> 02:20:06.750
Mark, would you like to comment?

02:20:06.750 --> 02:20:10.020
'Cause you chair the digital technology.

02:20:10.020 --> 02:20:14.303
- I think I love the tone that Vin struck.

02:20:14.303 --> 02:20:19.303
I think that tone is
a direct reflection of

02:20:19.470 --> 02:20:21.594
the recent engagement with the government

02:20:21.594 --> 02:20:22.886
and the secretary of state.

02:20:22.886 --> 02:20:27.270
I think the digital
transformation of the NHS

02:20:27.270 --> 02:20:30.000
offers the root out of where we are today.

02:20:30.000 --> 02:20:34.080
And I am full of hope

02:20:34.080 --> 02:20:39.080
that if we commit, there
is a dramatically different

02:20:39.120 --> 02:20:43.020
and better place for patients
and our colleagues out there.

02:20:43.020 --> 02:20:44.280
But it's going to take time.

02:20:44.280 --> 02:20:46.950
And what this needs is consistent

02:20:46.950 --> 02:20:48.960
year-on-year discipline and funding.

02:20:48.960 --> 02:20:50.790
- Steady investment
- Steady.

02:20:50.790 --> 02:20:53.550
But there is a dramatically
different world there.

02:20:53.550 --> 02:20:55.290
- Yeah.
- For everybody.

02:20:55.290 --> 02:20:56.190
- Yeah.

02:20:56.190 --> 02:20:58.347
So comments or questions for Vin?

02:20:58.347 --> 02:21:00.300
And I have one which is just,

02:21:00.300 --> 02:21:02.378
which is I always ask you this actually,

02:21:02.378 --> 02:21:03.715
and so it's my very parochial question.

02:21:03.715 --> 02:21:05.640
It's just the power of the,

02:21:05.640 --> 02:21:08.310
how do you think about the
power of the NHS number

02:21:08.310 --> 02:21:10.270
as a single customer identifier

02:21:11.430 --> 02:21:13.440
and going beyond health

02:21:13.440 --> 02:21:16.560
and into other sources of data

02:21:16.560 --> 02:21:19.350
on population actually
can inform us on health.

02:21:19.350 --> 02:21:22.860
So is the NHS number really getting good,

02:21:22.860 --> 02:21:24.540
a good foothold in terms of being

02:21:24.540 --> 02:21:27.873
the aggregating identifier point?

02:21:28.723 --> 02:21:30.600
- Well, so it is the
aggregating data point

02:21:30.600 --> 02:21:32.663
that we all use.

02:21:32.663 --> 02:21:34.320
We are the only health system in the world

02:21:34.320 --> 02:21:36.150
that has a linked dataset

02:21:36.150 --> 02:21:38.520
linked via the single unique identifier.

02:21:38.520 --> 02:21:41.250
It's the only unique identifier
that you have from birth.

02:21:41.250 --> 02:21:43.670
So you get your National
Insurance number from birth.

02:21:44.987 --> 02:21:46.740
And so yeah, it's absolutely critical.

02:21:46.740 --> 02:21:50.400
- But is it capturing other
parts of the data we gather

02:21:50.400 --> 02:21:52.170
from other systems outside health?

02:21:52.170 --> 02:21:56.760
- Yes, so at the moment,
your NHS number is not used,

02:21:56.760 --> 02:21:58.980
you know, for example
for local authorities

02:21:58.980 --> 02:22:00.990
or you know, for ONS statistics

02:22:00.990 --> 02:22:03.210
or for education for that matter.

02:22:03.210 --> 02:22:05.250
But what we have been successful
in being able to do is

02:22:05.250 --> 02:22:07.680
to work with local
government at a local level,

02:22:07.680 --> 02:22:10.830
work with the ONS to do
the data linkages across

02:22:10.830 --> 02:22:14.130
and that's enabled us, for
example, to get far more data

02:22:14.130 --> 02:22:17.490
on how what we do in the
Health Service actually affects

02:22:17.490 --> 02:22:20.430
our lives more broadly on
things like employment,

02:22:20.430 --> 02:22:22.440
on education and health and so on.

02:22:22.440 --> 02:22:24.150
So the number's absolutely fantastic.

02:22:24.150 --> 02:22:25.320
We're working closely with government

02:22:25.320 --> 02:22:27.720
about how you can go
further on data linkages

02:22:27.720 --> 02:22:29.520
and that will be one of
the major pieces of work

02:22:29.520 --> 02:22:32.217
that we'll be doing over the next year.

02:22:32.217 --> 02:22:33.308
- Very good.

02:22:33.308 --> 02:22:34.560
Robert?

02:22:34.560 --> 02:22:36.330
- Multiple references, I
mean it's a great paper

02:22:36.330 --> 02:22:38.790
and terrific stuff going on.

02:22:38.790 --> 02:22:40.770
Multiple references to
the single patient record

02:22:40.770 --> 02:22:43.260
which we all crave for.

02:22:43.260 --> 02:22:45.060
What would you say is the timetable

02:22:45.060 --> 02:22:47.013
and is it investment dependent?

02:22:50.352 --> 02:22:53.127
- So yeah, so firstly I think

02:22:53.127 --> 02:22:55.700
the single patient record
when we describe that,

02:22:55.700 --> 02:22:59.100
it is basically a record
owned by the patient,

02:22:59.100 --> 02:23:01.560
which are currently,
we don't have a record

02:23:01.560 --> 02:23:03.900
that's owned by us as a clinician.

02:23:03.900 --> 02:23:05.880
The record sits with me.

02:23:05.880 --> 02:23:08.700
It contains all the
information about you no matter

02:23:08.700 --> 02:23:11.190
which part of the service
you're accessing, primary care,

02:23:11.190 --> 02:23:13.470
secondary care, Pharmacy First, whatever.

02:23:13.470 --> 02:23:16.650
And thirdly that then you
are able to make choices

02:23:16.650 --> 02:23:18.660
about who that information is shared with.

02:23:18.660 --> 02:23:21.330
It does mean all your
caregivers can see it.

02:23:21.330 --> 02:23:23.550
It's a commitment that the
secretary of state made

02:23:23.550 --> 02:23:26.190
in his speech to the Royal College of GPs

02:23:26.190 --> 02:23:28.650
in October last year,
we're currently working

02:23:28.650 --> 02:23:31.920
with government on how
that will be reflected

02:23:31.920 --> 02:23:33.420
in the spending review.

02:23:33.420 --> 02:23:35.447
And then actually because
we've not had anything like it

02:23:35.447 --> 02:23:37.440
in the NHS before,

02:23:37.440 --> 02:23:40.200
if we are successful in securing
the funding, we will work

02:23:40.200 --> 02:23:42.090
with the technology industry

02:23:42.090 --> 02:23:44.130
and with other countries that
we've already been meeting

02:23:44.130 --> 02:23:47.100
like India, Estonia, other parts

02:23:47.100 --> 02:23:49.140
of the world who've got these to work out

02:23:49.140 --> 02:23:51.510
how you might actually put
it together, architect it

02:23:51.510 --> 02:23:53.823
and make it work for
the rest of the system.

02:23:56.070 --> 02:23:59.193
- Any other comments or questions for Vin?

02:24:00.260 --> 02:24:02.250
I mean, I think Mark's captured,

02:24:02.250 --> 02:24:04.710
you captured it really
well actually the tone

02:24:04.710 --> 02:24:06.690
of this presentation, discussion from Vin

02:24:06.690 --> 02:24:10.380
and actually if we can
win through in partnership

02:24:10.380 --> 02:24:13.290
clearly so supported by
the secretary of state

02:24:13.290 --> 02:24:14.123
and in the 10-year health plan

02:24:14.123 --> 02:24:17.493
and we can actually
capture steady investment.

02:24:18.330 --> 02:24:22.830
I think significant steady
investment over several years,

02:24:22.830 --> 02:24:24.750
it will be utterly
transformational, I think.

02:24:24.750 --> 02:24:25.583
- It will.

02:24:25.583 --> 02:24:27.270
There's another point to Robert's question

02:24:27.270 --> 02:24:29.430
about the single patient record.

02:24:29.430 --> 02:24:32.290
The single patient record will unlock

02:24:33.150 --> 02:24:36.843
a much better treatment
pathway for all citizens.

02:24:38.399 --> 02:24:42.150
It will unlock amazing
efficiency for our colleagues

02:24:42.150 --> 02:24:45.480
and there are endless edge
cases where you could argue

02:24:45.480 --> 02:24:48.390
for years about why you
don't want to do it.

02:24:48.390 --> 02:24:52.850
And at some point as a
Board, as the government,

02:24:52.850 --> 02:24:55.110
as the custodians of the nation's health,

02:24:55.110 --> 02:24:57.870
we need to stop arguing about edge cases

02:24:57.870 --> 02:25:00.990
and start dealing with the
vast majority of society

02:25:00.990 --> 02:25:03.060
who desperately need this

02:25:03.060 --> 02:25:07.320
because we are going to get
dragged back into long grass

02:25:07.320 --> 02:25:10.050
with things that actually
in all probability,

02:25:10.050 --> 02:25:11.163
will never happen.

02:25:12.690 --> 02:25:13.523
- Very good.

02:25:13.523 --> 02:25:15.960
So Steve, I'm going to put Rob

02:25:15.960 --> 02:25:19.440
and Peter back on the sort of on the stage

02:25:19.440 --> 02:25:21.270
'cause you were going to
ask a question I think

02:25:21.270 --> 02:25:24.223
when we were doing the cancer discussion.

02:25:24.223 --> 02:25:26.910
Would you like to ask it
now because I've created,

02:25:26.910 --> 02:25:28.350
only if it's brief though, Steve.

02:25:28.350 --> 02:25:30.690
- Well Peter might have covered it off.

02:25:30.690 --> 02:25:32.520
I couldn't see it jumping out of the paper

02:25:32.520 --> 02:25:34.140
and apologies I couldn't hear everything.

02:25:34.140 --> 02:25:35.890
It was about inequalities in cancer

02:25:36.900 --> 02:25:41.900
and what progress is
being made in all cancers

02:25:41.940 --> 02:25:44.700
or certain cancers in
addressing health inequalities

02:25:44.700 --> 02:25:48.330
and what do you see as of the prospects

02:25:48.330 --> 02:25:50.670
in terms of diagnostic approaches,

02:25:50.670 --> 02:25:52.683
et cetera, of doing that in the future.

02:25:53.910 --> 02:25:54.743
- Thanks Steve.

02:25:54.743 --> 02:25:56.310
Sorry, you weren't
expecting me to come back.

02:25:56.310 --> 02:26:01.285
But what we've seen in the
lung health checks programme

02:26:01.285 --> 02:26:04.020
as others have alluded to and
I think maybe in the paper

02:26:04.020 --> 02:26:07.320
is that having started running
them in the communities

02:26:07.320 --> 02:26:08.277
with the highest rates of lung cancer

02:26:08.277 --> 02:26:10.770
and the highest rates of late diagnosis,

02:26:10.770 --> 02:26:13.180
which of course is the
most deprived communities

02:26:14.220 --> 02:26:16.300
because it's an effective
way of picking up lung cancer

02:26:16.300 --> 02:26:18.630
at an early stage, we've
inverted the relationship

02:26:18.630 --> 02:26:21.720
between deprivation and stage
of diagnosis of lung cancer.

02:26:21.720 --> 02:26:23.040
So you're actually more likely

02:26:23.040 --> 02:26:25.380
to have your lung cancer
diagnosed at an early stage now

02:26:25.380 --> 02:26:27.480
if you're in the most deprived quintile.

02:26:27.480 --> 02:26:30.180
And and that is, as Julian
alluded to, is a reflection

02:26:30.180 --> 02:26:31.860
of the fact that if you put the investment

02:26:31.860 --> 02:26:34.860
in a very specific and
targeted way, we are not

02:26:34.860 --> 02:26:36.750
the sort of helpless recipients

02:26:36.750 --> 02:26:38.220
of the social inequalities of health.

02:26:38.220 --> 02:26:40.830
There are actually things
that we can do to influence it

02:26:40.830 --> 02:26:43.200
if you get it right, now
there's not an infinite number

02:26:43.200 --> 02:26:45.060
of interventions like that.

02:26:45.060 --> 02:26:48.780
But what I think it speaks
to is a more specific

02:26:48.780 --> 02:26:51.300
and targeted approach to finding people

02:26:51.300 --> 02:26:53.820
or giving people with the high potential

02:26:53.820 --> 02:26:55.440
of having those illnesses

02:26:55.440 --> 02:26:57.630
ways into the system that we've not done.

02:26:57.630 --> 02:26:59.640
So simply waiting for people to turn up

02:26:59.640 --> 02:27:03.540
with symptoms has not historically
done what we need to do.

02:27:03.540 --> 02:27:05.610
Working out who's at
highest risk, working out

02:27:05.610 --> 02:27:08.010
how you target them
using the local knowledge

02:27:08.010 --> 02:27:09.030
and the cancer alliances.

02:27:09.030 --> 02:27:11.490
So the West Midlands
Cancer Alliance working

02:27:11.490 --> 02:27:13.320
with their Africa Caribbean
community have done

02:27:13.320 --> 02:27:16.140
a massive amount to raise
awareness of prostate cancer

02:27:16.140 --> 02:27:18.510
among that group, as
a consequence of which

02:27:18.510 --> 02:27:19.620
we actually see people

02:27:19.620 --> 02:27:23.010
with Black heritage
presenting at an earlier stage

02:27:23.010 --> 02:27:24.090
than people with white heritage

02:27:24.090 --> 02:27:25.440
because the community groups have

02:27:25.440 --> 02:27:26.820
been engaged to raise awareness

02:27:26.820 --> 02:27:29.430
and get people coming in to get checked.

02:27:29.430 --> 02:27:31.260
So I think there are
things that can be done.

02:27:31.260 --> 02:27:33.000
A lot of it is quite local

02:27:33.000 --> 02:27:35.160
because you have to
understand your community.

02:27:35.160 --> 02:27:36.300
But there are also some things

02:27:36.300 --> 02:27:39.930
that we can do at a national
level to move the dial on that

02:27:39.930 --> 02:27:42.450
and work out how you
really get the healthcare

02:27:42.450 --> 02:27:45.000
to people rather than waiting
for the people to come to you.

02:27:45.000 --> 02:27:47.160
And I think that's going to be
a really important component

02:27:47.160 --> 02:27:49.060
of how we think about the cancer plan.

02:27:49.950 --> 02:27:51.559
- So thank you Steve.

02:27:51.559 --> 02:27:52.770
Thank you very much Peter.

02:27:52.770 --> 02:27:54.603
So there are three more items.

02:27:55.680 --> 02:27:58.350
There's the Annual Emergency
Preparedness Resilience

02:27:58.350 --> 02:28:00.780
and Response Assurance Report.

02:28:00.780 --> 02:28:02.430
I know Emily, you've kindly provided it.

02:28:02.430 --> 02:28:03.420
It's a starred item.

02:28:03.420 --> 02:28:06.600
I'd just like to say we had
a good discussion about this

02:28:06.600 --> 02:28:09.060
in this morning's Private Board

02:28:09.060 --> 02:28:11.370
and just to reflect, you
know, we should note it

02:28:11.370 --> 02:28:15.120
and also recognise this
continuously evolving picture.

02:28:15.120 --> 02:28:17.550
And we had as I say,
a very good discussion

02:28:17.550 --> 02:28:20.640
and we're looking as we
go forward both to improve

02:28:20.640 --> 02:28:23.020
and amend actually some of our practises

02:28:24.287 --> 02:28:25.980
and some of our tests of resilience.

02:28:25.980 --> 02:28:28.080
But the Board should just note this report

02:28:29.672 --> 02:28:30.780
in its current state.

02:28:30.780 --> 02:28:33.210
And the other two reports,
again just for noting,

02:28:33.210 --> 02:28:36.390
there's the directions
and mandatory requests

02:28:36.390 --> 02:28:37.707
that we receive essentially
from the secretary of state

02:28:37.707 --> 02:28:41.060
and one is under the
notify service directions

02:28:41.060 --> 02:28:44.190
and the second is national
proxy service directions.

02:28:44.190 --> 02:28:45.960
So we've had those from
the secretary of state

02:28:45.960 --> 02:28:48.270
and the Board's asked to
note those new directions

02:28:48.270 --> 02:28:50.420
and the information
provided in the papers.

02:28:51.870 --> 02:28:55.050
- Those are absolutely
critical to moving to paperless

02:28:55.050 --> 02:28:58.350
and also to proxy access
for carers, for parents

02:28:58.350 --> 02:28:59.940
to their child's NHS app.

02:28:59.940 --> 02:29:01.590
So they are important.
- I saw that, yeah.

02:29:01.590 --> 02:29:05.220
The proxy and yep, I saw
that, even more of a reason

02:29:05.220 --> 02:29:08.547
for no Board member to vote
against those, I think.

02:29:08.547 --> 02:29:11.820
And then lastly, we've
got the report on the use

02:29:11.820 --> 02:29:14.250
of the NHS England seal, where Julian,

02:29:14.250 --> 02:29:16.770
you have been extremely busy again

02:29:16.770 --> 02:29:18.630
and there's a long list
of documents signed

02:29:18.630 --> 02:29:20.430
and sealed with the NHS England seals.

02:29:20.430 --> 02:29:24.480
So we can just for
information and note those.

02:29:24.480 --> 02:29:26.760
Is there any other
business before we close?

02:29:26.760 --> 02:29:27.660
Just going around?

02:29:28.980 --> 02:29:32.610
Well, I'm going to finish
then just with my thanks

02:29:32.610 --> 02:29:34.980
obviously to Board colleagues

02:29:34.980 --> 02:29:36.900
and executive colleagues.

02:29:36.900 --> 02:29:39.390
And also as I said at the
start, actually frankly

02:29:39.390 --> 02:29:43.080
to all frontline staff and all chairs

02:29:43.080 --> 02:29:44.610
and leaders across our system.

02:29:44.610 --> 02:29:48.070
So it's an amazing workforce

02:29:49.050 --> 02:29:51.030
and we should note our
thanks to all of them.

02:29:51.030 --> 02:29:54.000
But just for the purpose of
this meeting specifically,

02:29:54.000 --> 02:29:57.090
my thanks to the Board and
to our executive colleagues,

02:29:57.090 --> 02:30:01.740
a number of us are going
as of Monday actually.

02:30:01.740 --> 02:30:06.740
So Amanda and Julian and Emily and Steve,

02:30:06.960 --> 02:30:11.867
particular thanks for
your actually friendship

02:30:11.867 --> 02:30:14.670
as well as your huge executive commitment

02:30:14.670 --> 02:30:19.670
and the performance as execs
on the NHS England Board.

02:30:23.070 --> 02:30:27.033
to Penny and to Jim,
I know has had to run.

02:30:28.380 --> 02:30:32.433
To Penny and to Jim, we wish
you all the best success.

02:30:33.270 --> 02:30:35.700
It's a sort of remarkable, almost chalice

02:30:35.700 --> 02:30:38.073
to be handed actually.

02:30:39.270 --> 02:30:43.080
And so we wish you all
the best success with it.

02:30:43.080 --> 02:30:44.640
It truly, truly matters.

02:30:44.640 --> 02:30:49.053
It is such an important
